[
  {
    "sourceRow": 2,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__counting-objects-to-20",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Counting objects to 20",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Counting objects to 20</h2><p>Counting objects to 20 means matching each object with one number from 1 to 20.</p>\n<p>It helps children know <strong>how many</strong> things are in a small group.</p>\n<p>This is important in daily life when counting pencils, toys, chocolates, steps, or books in a school bag.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Counting objects to 20</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Counting tells the total number of objects.</p>\n<p>We say one number for each object.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>First, touch or point to one object.</p>\n<p>Next, say the next number.</p>\n<p>Continue until every object is counted.</p>\n<p>The last number tells the total.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Numbers have a fixed order.</p>\n<p>Each object gets only one number, so nothing is missed.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>Children count crayons, water bottles, biscuits, balls, and fingers.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Some students count one object twice or skip an object.</p>\n<p>Move objects aside after counting.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Touch, say, move.</p>\n<p>Key Point: The last number you say tells how many objects there are.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Counting objects to 20</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Count <strong>10 fingers</strong> on both hands.</li><li>Count <strong>12 crayons</strong> in a box.</li><li>Count <strong>15 balls</strong> in a basket.</li><li>Count <strong>20 pages</strong> turned in a notebook.</li><li>Counting is like giving each object its own name tag. The number tag shows its place in the count.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Counting objects to 20 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Put the objects in one line.</li><li>Touch the first object and say <strong>one</strong>.</li><li>Touch the next object and say <strong>two</strong>.</li><li>Keep going until all objects are counted.</li><li>The final number is the answer.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>For this level, the advanced idea is <strong>one-to-one counting</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each object must match exactly one counting number.</p>\n<p>This builds the base for addition, subtraction, grouping, and place value.</p>\n<p>A child should also understand that changing the order does not change the total.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Place 16 small objects on a table.</li><li>Count them slowly by touching each one.</li><li>Then mix them and count again.</li><li>Write the number you get both times.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555352
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 3,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__counting-objects-to-50",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Counting objects to 50",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Counting objects to 50</h2><p>Counting objects to 50 means finding totals in a bigger group.</p>\n<p>Children use numbers from 1 to 50 in the correct order.</p>\n<p>This helps in daily life when counting coins, pages, stairs, students, seeds, or small items in a box.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Counting objects to 50</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Counting to 50 extends counting beyond small groups.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Arrange objects in rows or small groups.</p>\n<p>Point to each object once.</p>\n<p>Say the numbers in order from 1 onward.</p>\n<p>Stop when no objects are left.</p>\n<p>The last number is the total.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Counting works because every number follows a fixed sequence.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We count classroom chairs, beads, buttons, mangoes, and rupee coins.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may lose track after 29 or 39.</p>\n<p>Count in small groups of 10 to stay organized.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Make groups of ten, then count the extra ones.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Groups of ten make counting to 50 easier.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Counting objects to 50</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Count <strong>50 paise or rupee coins</strong> in groups of 10.</li><li>Count <strong>students</strong> standing in assembly rows.</li><li>Count <strong>pages</strong> read in a book.</li><li>Count <strong>beads</strong> while making a bracelet.</li><li>Counting to 50 is like climbing stairs one step at a time.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Counting objects to 50 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>First make small groups of <strong>10</strong> objects.</li><li>Count 10, 20, 30, 40, 50.</li><li>If some objects are left, count them one by one.</li><li>This is easier than counting a large messy pile.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Counting to 50 prepares children for <strong>place value</strong>.</p>\n<p>They begin seeing 50 as five groups of ten.</p>\n<p>This supports later ideas like tens and ones, skip counting, addition with carry, and mental math.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Take 43 buttons or seeds.</li><li>Make groups of 10.</li><li>Count the groups first, then count the remaining objects.</li><li>Write the total as <strong>4 tens and 3 ones</strong>.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555353
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 4,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__counting-objects-to-100",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Counting objects to 100",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Counting objects to 100</h2><p>Counting objects to 100 means counting a large group using numbers from 1 to 100.</p>\n<p>It helps children understand <strong>hundreds</strong>, <strong>tens</strong>, and <strong>ones</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is useful for counting money, marks, pages, packets, or people in a hall.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Counting objects to 100</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Counting to 100 finds the total in a large collection.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Put objects into groups of 10.</p>\n<p>Count each group as ten.</p>\n<p>Then count leftover objects one by one.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Our number system uses groups of ten.</p>\n<p>Ten ones make one ten, and ten tens make one hundred.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We count chart stickers, matchsticks, coins, classroom points, and steps.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students often forget what comes after 69, 79, or 89.</p>\n<p>Practice the tens names clearly.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Ten groups of ten make 100.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Counting to 100 is easier when objects are grouped in tens.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Counting objects to 100</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Count <strong>100 chart stickers</strong> for a school project.</li><li>Count <strong>100 runs</strong> in cricket.</li><li>Count <strong>100 rupees</strong> using ten 10-rupee coins.</li><li>Count <strong>100 steps</strong> while walking.</li><li>It is like filling ten small boxes, each holding ten objects.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Counting objects to 100 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Do not count a big pile randomly.</li><li>Make <strong>10 groups of 10</strong>.</li><li>Say 10, 20, 30, and so on.</li><li>If there are extra objects, count them after the tens groups.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>This concept introduces the base-ten system.</p>\n<p>A child learns that 100 is not just a long count.</p>\n<p>It is <strong>10 tens</strong>.</p>\n<p>This helps later with place value, comparing numbers, addition, subtraction, and multiplication tables.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use 100 matchsticks or paper bits.</li><li>Make ten groups with ten items in each group.</li><li>Count by tens to 100.</li><li>Then remove one group and tell how many are left.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555353
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 5,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__one-to-one-correspondence",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "One-to-one correspondence",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>One-to-one correspondence</h2><p>One-to-one correspondence means matching <strong>one object</strong> with <strong>one number</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each object gets counted once only.</p>\n<p>This idea is important because correct counting, addition, sharing, and comparing groups all depend on it.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>One-to-one correspondence</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Every object must match exactly one counting word.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Point to an object, say one number, and then move to the next object.</p>\n<p>Do not say two numbers for one object.</p>\n<p>Do not skip any object.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Counting is fair only when each object gets one number.</p>\n<p>This makes the final total correct.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We give one pencil to each student, one plate to each person, or one ticket to each passenger.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>A child may point faster than speaking, or speak faster than pointing.</p>\n<p>Slow counting fixes this.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>One touch, one number.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Correct counting needs one number for each object.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>One-to-one correspondence</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Give <strong>one notebook</strong> to each child.</li><li>Put <strong>one spoon</strong> near each plate.</li><li>Count <strong>one bead</strong> for each number spoken.</li><li>Give <strong>one ticket</strong> to each train passenger.</li><li>It is like pairing socks. Each sock needs exactly one matching sock.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>One-to-one correspondence becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Touch one object and say one number.</li><li>Then touch the next object and say the next number.</li><li>Keep the touching and speaking together.</li><li>If your finger and voice do not match, count again.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>One-to-one correspondence is a foundation for <strong>cardinality</strong>, which means knowing the total after counting.</p>\n<p>It also supports comparing sets, equal sharing, simple multiplication, and understanding that numbers represent quantities, not just words.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Place 12 pencils and 12 erasers on a table.</li><li>Match one pencil with one eraser.</li><li>Are any left over?</li><li>Explain how matching helps you know whether the two groups are equal.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555354
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 6,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__forward-number-sequence",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Forward number sequence",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Forward number sequence</h2><p>A forward number sequence means saying numbers in increasing order.</p>\n<p>Example: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.</p>\n<p>It is important because children use it to count objects, move on a number line, add numbers, and understand time or steps.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Forward number sequence</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Forward counting moves from a smaller number to a bigger number.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start with a number.</p>\n<p>Say the next number that comes after it.</p>\n<p>Keep moving one step ahead each time.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Numbers are arranged in a fixed order.</p>\n<p>Each next number is one more than the number before it.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We count lift floors going up, cricket runs increasing, pages turning, and steps while walking.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may jump over a number, like saying 7, 8, 10.</p>\n<p>Practice slowly with a number line.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Forward means plus one each step.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Forward sequence shows numbers growing by one.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Forward number sequence</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>A <strong>lift</strong> goes from floor 1 to 2 to 3.</li><li>A cricket score goes from <strong>8 to 9 to 10</strong>.</li><li>A child counts <strong>steps</strong> while walking.</li><li>Pages in a book move forward.</li><li>Forward counting is like walking ahead on a straight path.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Forward number sequence becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Start at any number.</li><li>Say the number that comes just after it.</li><li>Then continue.</li><li>If you start at 6, the forward sequence is 6, 7, 8, 9, 10.</li><li>Each step adds <strong>one</strong>.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Forward sequence builds the idea of the <strong>successor</strong>, which means the next number.</p>\n<p>It supports addition because adding 1 gives the next number.</p>\n<p>It also prepares children for skip counting, number lines, and patterns.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Start from 13 and write the next 10 numbers.</li><li>Then circle the number that is 5 steps after 13.</li><li>Explain how you counted forward.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555354
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 7,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__backward-number-sequence",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Backward number sequence",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Backward number sequence</h2><p>A backward number sequence means saying numbers in decreasing order.</p>\n<p>Example: 10, 9, 8, 7, 6.</p>\n<p>It is useful for countdowns, subtraction, checking remaining items, and understanding numbers on a number line.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Backward number sequence</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Backward counting moves from a bigger number to a smaller number.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Start with a number.</p>\n<p>Say the number just before it.</p>\n<p>Keep moving one step back each time.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Every counting number has a number before it, except the starting point 1 in early counting.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We hear countdowns before a rocket launch, count remaining balls in cricket, and count floors while a lift comes down.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may confuse the order near 20, like 20, 18, 19.</p>\n<p>Use a number line.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Backward means minus one each step.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Backward sequence shows numbers becoming smaller by one.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Backward number sequence</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>A <strong>rocket</strong> countdown says 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.</li><li>A <strong>lift</strong> comes down from floor 6 to 5 to 4.</li><li>A cricket team has fewer balls left after each ball.</li><li>A timer counts seconds down.</li><li>Backward counting is like stepping down stairs.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Backward number sequence becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Choose a starting number.</li><li>Say the number just before it.</li><li>Keep going down by <strong>one</strong>.</li><li>If you start at 15, say 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Backward sequence introduces the <strong>predecessor</strong>, which means the number just before.</p>\n<p>It prepares children for subtraction because taking away 1 gives the previous number.</p>\n<p>It also helps with countdowns and comparing smaller numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Start from 30 and count backward to 20.</li><li>Write all numbers.</li><li>Then answer: what number comes just before 25?</li><li>Explain how you know.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555355
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 8,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__missing-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Missing numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Missing numbers</h2><p>Missing numbers are blanks in a number sequence.</p>\n<p>Children find the number that should come in the empty place.</p>\n<p>This is important because it builds number order, pattern thinking, addition, subtraction, and careful checking.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Missing numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A missing number is a number left out from a correct sequence.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Read the numbers before and after the blank.</p>\n<p>Check whether the sequence is going forward or backward.</p>\n<p>Then fill the number that fits.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Number sequences follow a rule, usually plus one or minus one in Class 1.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We notice missing page numbers, skipped roll numbers, or missing floor labels.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may look at only one side of the blank.</p>\n<p>Always check both sides.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Look left, look right, then write.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A missing number must fit the order of the sequence.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Missing numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Page numbers: 21, 22, __, 24.</li><li>Lift floors: 3, 4, __, 6.</li><li>Roll numbers: 8, __, 10.</li><li>Counting steps: 15, 16, __, 18.</li><li>Finding a missing number is like finding a lost bead in a chain.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Missing numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at the numbers around the blank.</li><li>If numbers are going up, count forward.</li><li>If they are going down, count backward.</li><li>Write the number that makes the sequence correct.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Missing-number tasks train children to identify a <strong>pattern rule</strong>.</p>\n<p>At this level, the rule is usually adding or subtracting one.</p>\n<p>Later, the same idea helps with skip counting, tables, algebra, and equations.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Fill the blanks: 18, 19, __, 21, __.</li><li>Then create your own sequence from 30 to 35 with two missing numbers for a friend to solve.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555355
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 9,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__before-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Before numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Before numbers</h2><p>Before numbers are numbers that come just earlier in the counting order.</p>\n<p>The number before 8 is 7.</p>\n<p>This idea helps children count backward, subtract one, arrange numbers, and understand positions in daily life.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Before numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>The before number is one less than the given number.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Think of the counting order.</p>\n<p>Find the number just to the left or just earlier.</p>\n<p>For 12, count 10, 11, 12.</p>\n<p>So 11 is before 12.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Numbers have a fixed order, and each step backward reduces the number by one.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We use it for roll numbers, page numbers, house numbers, and lift floors.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students sometimes write the after number instead.</p>\n<p>Ask, “Which number came first?”</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Before means back one step.</p>\n<p>Key Point: The before number is one less.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Before numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>The page before <strong>page 9</strong> is page 8.</li><li>The floor before <strong>floor 5</strong> is floor 4.</li><li>The roll number before <strong>14</strong> is 13.</li><li>In a queue, the person before you stands ahead of you.</li><li>It is like taking one step back.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Before numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>To find the before number, count one step backward.</li><li>Before 6 is 5.</li><li>Before 20 is 19.</li><li>You can also use your fingers or a number line to check.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The before number is called the <strong>predecessor</strong> in mathematics.</p>\n<p>For any counting number n, its predecessor is one less.</p>\n<p>This idea becomes important in subtraction, ordering numbers, and understanding number-line movement.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write the number before each number: 7, 15, 20, 31, 50.</li><li>Then choose one answer and show it on a number line.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555355
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 10,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__after-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "After numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>After numbers</h2><p>After numbers are numbers that come just later in the counting order.</p>\n<p>The number after 8 is 9.</p>\n<p>This idea helps children count forward, add one, arrange numbers, and follow number labels in real life.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>After numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>The after number is one more than the given number.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Think of the counting order.</p>\n<p>Find the next number.</p>\n<p>For 16, say 16, then 17.</p>\n<p>So 17 comes after 16.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Each forward step increases a counting number by one.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We use after numbers in page numbers, lift floors, scores, dates, and classroom roll numbers.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may write the before number by mistake.</p>\n<p>Ask, “Which number comes next?”</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>After means forward one step.</p>\n<p>Key Point: The after number is one more.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>After numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>The page after <strong>page 12</strong> is page 13.</li><li>The floor after <strong>floor 4</strong> is floor 5.</li><li>A cricket score after <strong>29 runs</strong> may become 30.</li><li>The house after number 18 may be 19.</li><li>It is like taking one step forward.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>After numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>To find the after number, count one step forward.</li><li>After 9 is 10.</li><li>After 24 is 25.</li><li>A number line can help you see the next number clearly.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The after number is called the <strong>successor</strong> in mathematics.</p>\n<p>For any counting number n, its successor is one more.</p>\n<p>This supports addition, increasing patterns, number comparison, and place-value learning.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write the number after each number: 6, 13, 19, 29, 49.</li><li>Then explain why the number after 29 is 30.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 11,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__between-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Between numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Between numbers</h2><p>Between numbers are numbers that come in the middle of two given numbers.</p>\n<p>For example, 6 is between 5 and 7.</p>\n<p>This idea helps children understand order, compare numbers, and place numbers correctly on a number line.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Between numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A between number comes after the first number and before the second number.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Read the two numbers.</p>\n<p>Count forward from the smaller number.</p>\n<p>The number you say before reaching the larger number is between them.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Numbers are arranged in order, like points on a line.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we see it</h3></div><p>We use between numbers for pages, house numbers, seats, floors, and scores.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may include the two end numbers.</p>\n<p>The between number is in the middle, not at the ends.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Between means in the middle.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A between number sits after one number and before another.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Between numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Page <strong>8</strong> is between pages 7 and 9.</li><li>Seat <strong>15</strong> is between seats 14 and 16.</li><li>Floor <strong>3</strong> is between floors 2 and 4.</li><li>House number <strong>22</strong> is between 21 and 23.</li><li>It is like standing between two friends in a line.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Between numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at the two numbers.</li><li>Count from the first number to the second number.</li><li>The number in the middle is the between number.</li><li>For 10 and 12, the between number is 11.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Between numbers help children understand <strong>ordering</strong> and number-line position.</p>\n<p>This later supports inequalities, intervals, measurement scales, and estimation.</p>\n<p>At Class 1 level, most between-number questions use consecutive numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find the number between each pair: 4 and 6, 11 and 13, 20 and 22, 35 and 37.</li><li>Then make two of your own examples.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 12,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__number-names-1-20",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Number names 1-20",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Number names 1-20</h2><p>Number names from 1 to 20 are the word forms of numbers, like one, two, and three.</p>\n<p>They help children read and write numbers in books, marks, money, and classroom work.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Number names 1-20</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A number can be shown by a numeral and by a name.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>See the numeral, say its name, then write the word.</p>\n<p>Example: 14 is <strong>fourteen</strong>.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Number names are fixed language labels for quantities.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use them in school marks, page numbers, birthday cards, and counting objects.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Children may mix thirteen and thirty.</p>\n<p>Say the word slowly and match it with the numeral.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Numbers 13 to 19 mostly end with <strong>teen</strong>.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Number names help us read and write numbers in words.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Number names 1-20</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Write <strong>7</strong> as seven in a notebook.</li><li>Read <strong>12</strong> marks as twelve marks.</li><li>Count <strong>20</strong> chocolates and write twenty.</li><li>Number names are like names of friends. Each numeral has its own word name.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Number names 1-20 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at the number.</li><li>Say it aloud.</li><li>Then write its name.</li><li>For 1 to 20, practice daily with objects.</li><li>Read 15 as <strong>fifteen</strong>, not five-ten.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Number names connect symbols with language.</p>\n<p>This supports reading word problems later.</p>\n<p>Children should link three forms: object count, numeral, and word name.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write number names for 3, 8, 11, 14, and 20.</li><li>Then count 16 pencils and write the numeral and word name.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 13,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__number-names-21-100",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Number names 21-100",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Number names 21-100</h2><p>Number names from 21 to 100 show how tens and ones join in words.</p>\n<p>They help children read prices, page numbers, house numbers, and scores correctly.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Number names 21-100</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Number names 21-100 needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Number names 21-100</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Number names 21-100, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Number names 21-100 with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Number names 21-100 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Break the number into tens and ones.</li><li>For 58, say fifty and eight.</li><li>Together it becomes <strong>fifty-eight</strong>.</li><li>Practice 21, 32, 43, and 54.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>These names prepare children for place value.</p>\n<p>They show that 76 means seven tens and six ones.</p>\n<p>This helps with addition, subtraction, and comparing numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write the number names for 24, 39, 50, 68, and 100.</li><li>Underline the tens word in each answer.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555357
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 14,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__counting-and-number-sense__matching-numerals-and-quantities",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Counting and Number Sense",
    "subtopic": "Matching numerals and quantities",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Counting and Number Sense</p><h2>Matching numerals and quantities</h2><p>Matching numerals and quantities explains a specific idea inside Counting and Number Sense.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Matching numerals and quantities</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Matching numerals and quantities needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Matching numerals and quantities</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Matching numerals and quantities, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Matching numerals and quantities with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Matching numerals and quantities becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Count the objects one by one.</li><li>Say the last number.</li><li>Find the numeral with the same value.</li><li>If you count 9 balls, match them with <strong>9</strong>.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Matching numerals and quantities connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw 14 circles.</li><li>Write the numeral that matches them.</li><li>Then draw objects for the numerals 6, 10, and 18.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555357
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 15,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__ones",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Ones",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Ones</h2><p>Ones are single units.</p>\n<p>One pencil, one coin, or one block is one <strong>one</strong>.</p>\n<p>Learning ones helps children count small objects and understand place value.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Ones</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>one</strong> means one single item.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Count each separate object as one.</p>\n<p>Ten separate ones can later make one ten.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Numbers are built from single units.</p>\n<p>Every bigger number starts with ones.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We count coins, pencils, fingers, chocolates, and bottle caps.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Children may call a group of ten a one.</p>\n<p>A one is only a single item.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>One object is one one.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Ones are single units used to build numbers.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Ones, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Ones but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Ones becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Ones</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Ones, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Ones with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Ones becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at each separate object.</li><li>Count it as one.</li><li>If there are 7 separate buttons, there are <strong>7 ones</strong>.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Ones are the first place in place value.</p>\n<p>In 36, the digit 6 means six ones.</p>\n<p>This helps children understand two-digit numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Show 8 ones using small sticks.</li><li>Then write the digit that tells how many ones you have.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555357
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 16,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__tens",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Tens",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Tens</h2><p>Tens are groups of ten ones.</p>\n<p>One ten means 10 single objects bundled together.</p>\n<p>Learning tens makes counting faster and helps children understand two-digit numbers.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Tens</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>ten</strong> is one group of 10 ones.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Count 10 objects and tie or circle them as one group.</p>\n<p>Each group is counted as ten.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Our number system uses groups of ten.</p>\n<p>This is called base ten.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We count sticks, coins, pages, marks, and classroom objects quickly.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may count a ten-bundle as 1 object instead of 10 objects.</p>\n<p>Remember it is one group worth ten.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Ten ones make one ten.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Tens are groups of 10 ones.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Tens</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Ten pencils tied together make <strong>1 ten</strong>.</li><li>Two 10-rupee notes make <strong>2 tens of rupees</strong>.</li><li>30 students can be seen as <strong>3 tens</strong>.</li><li>A ten is like a packet holding 10 items.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Tens becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Make a group of 10 objects.</li><li>Call it one ten.</li><li>Two such groups are two tens, or 20.</li><li>Count tens as 10, 20, 30.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Tens make place value easier.</p>\n<p>In 42, the digit 4 means four tens, or 40.</p>\n<p>This helps in addition and subtraction.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Make 3 bundles of 10 sticks.</li><li>How many sticks are there?</li><li>Write the number in tens and ones.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555358
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 17,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__bundles-of-ten",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Bundles of ten",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Bundles of ten</h2><p>Bundles of ten explains a specific idea inside Place Value.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Bundles of ten</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A bundle of ten has exactly 10 objects.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Count 10 objects, group them, then start a new bundle.</p>\n<p>Count bundles by tens.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Grouping reduces repeated counting.</p>\n<p>It follows the base-ten system.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>Shops bundle pencils, banks bundle notes, and teachers group sticks or beads.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may make bundles with 9 or 11 objects.</p>\n<p>Count each bundle carefully.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Full bundle means exactly ten.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Bundles of ten make counting quicker and clearer.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Bundles of ten, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Bundles of ten but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Bundles of ten becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Bundles of ten</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Bundles of ten, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Bundles of ten with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Bundles of ten becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Count 10 objects.</li><li>Put them together.</li><li>That is one bundle.</li><li>If you make 5 bundles, count 10, 20, 30, 40, 50.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Bundles of ten prepare children for carrying in addition and borrowing in subtraction.</p>\n<p>They also show why digits have different values in different places.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Use 37 sticks.</li><li>Make bundles of ten.</li><li>Write how many bundles and loose sticks you have.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555358
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 18,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__two-digit-place-value",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Two-digit place value",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Two-digit place value</h2><p>Two-digit place value tells the value of each digit in numbers from 10 to 99.</p>\n<p>It helps children read, compare, and calculate with numbers correctly.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Two-digit place value</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A two-digit number has a <strong>tens</strong> place and a <strong>ones</strong> place.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>In 47, 4 is in the tens place, so it means 40.</p>\n<p>The 7 is in the ones place, so it means 7.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Place changes value.</p>\n<p>The same digit can mean different amounts in different places.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use it in money, marks, page numbers, and ages.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may think 47 means 4 plus 7 only.</p>\n<p>It means 40 plus</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Left digit is tens, right digit is ones.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Place decides the value of a digit.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Two-digit place value</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>58 means <strong>5 tens and 8 ones</strong>.</li><li>₹72 means 7 tens of rupees and 2 ones.</li><li>Page 34 means 3 tens and 4 ones.</li><li>Place value is like seats in a bus. Position matters.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Two-digit place value becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at the left digit for tens.</li><li>Look at the right digit for ones.</li><li>In 26, there are 2 tens and 6 ones.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Two-digit place value is the base for expanded form, comparing numbers, regrouping, and column addition.</p>\n<p>It teaches that digits have positional value.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write the tens and ones in 19, 43, 60, and 98.</li><li>Show one number using sticks or blocks.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555359
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 19,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__expanded-form-to-99",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Expanded form to 99",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Expanded form to 99</h2><p>Expanded form to 99 explains a specific idea inside Place Value.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Expanded form to 99</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Expanded form to 99 needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Expanded form to 99</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Expanded form to 99, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Expanded form to 99 with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Expanded form to 99 becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Take 47.</li><li>The 4 means 40.</li><li>The 7 means 7.</li><li>So 47 = 40 + 7.</li><li>Do the same for any number up to 99.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Expanded form to 99 connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Write expanded form for 25, 61, 70, 89, and 99.</li><li>Then draw tens and ones for 42.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555359
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 20,
    "id": "class-1__numbers-and-counting__place-value__comparing-two-digit-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 1",
    "chapter": "Numbers and Counting",
    "topic": "Place Value",
    "subtopic": "Comparing two-digit numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 1 / Numbers and Counting / Place Value</p><h2>Comparing two-digit numbers</h2><p>Comparing two-digit numbers explains a specific idea inside Place Value.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Numbers and Counting accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Comparing two-digit numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Comparing two-digit numbers needs the correct rule, condition, and check.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Comparing two-digit numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Comparing two-digit numbers, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Comparing two-digit numbers with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Comparing two-digit numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Look at the tens digits first.</li><li>If they are different, decide.</li><li>If they are same, compare the ones digits.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Comparing two-digit numbers connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Compare using greater or smaller: 34 and 43, 67 and 62, 90 and 89, 55 and 55.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786271555359
