    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 3 / Arithmetic / Multiplication Tables</p><h2>Patterns in multiplication tables</h2><p>Patterns in multiplication tables explains a specific idea inside Multiplication Tables.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Arithmetic accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Patterns in multiplication tables</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>Multiplication tables follow repeated steps.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Look at the answers in a table.</p>\n<p>Find what changes each time.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Each next answer adds the same table number.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>We use patterns to check table facts, skip count, and find mistakes.</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may trust memory even when a pattern shows an error.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Same table, same jump.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Table patterns come from equal jumps.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Patterns in multiplication tables, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Patterns in multiplication tables but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Patterns in multiplication tables becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Patterns in multiplication tables</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Patterns in multiplication tables, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Patterns in multiplication tables with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Patterns in multiplication tables becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Patterns in multiplication tables is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Patterns in multiplication tables connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Patterns in multiplication tables.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
