  {
    "sourceRow": 313,
    "id": "class-5__arithmetic__factors-and-operations__factors",
    "classLevel": "Class 5",
    "chapter": "Arithmetic",
    "topic": "Factors and Operations",
    "subtopic": "Factors",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 5 / Arithmetic / Factors and Operations</p><h2>Factors</h2><p>Factors explains a specific idea inside Factors and Operations.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Arithmetic accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Factors</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>factor</strong> fits into a number with no remainder.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Test multiplication pairs or divide exactly.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Factors make equal groups that use the whole number.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>arrays, sharing, simplifying fractions, and divisibility</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may include numbers that leave remainders.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Factor fits exactly.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Factors divide exactly.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Factors, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Factors but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Factors becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Factors</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Factors, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Factors with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Factors becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Factors is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Factors connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Factors.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489215
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 314,
    "id": "class-5__arithmetic__factors-and-operations__multiples",
    "classLevel": "Class 5",
    "chapter": "Arithmetic",
    "topic": "Factors and Operations",
    "subtopic": "Multiples",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 5 / Arithmetic / Factors and Operations</p><h2>Multiples</h2><p>Multiples explains a specific idea inside Factors and Operations.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Arithmetic accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Multiples</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>multiple</strong> is a table answer.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Multiply the number by 1, 2, 3, and so on.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Each multiple contains an exact number of equal groups.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>time, money, packaging, calendars, and patterns</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may confuse multiples with factors.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Multiples march forward.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Multiples are products of a number.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Multiples, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Multiples but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Multiples becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Multiples</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Multiples, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Multiples with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Multiples becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Multiples is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Multiples connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Multiples.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489215
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 315,
    "id": "class-5__arithmetic__factors-and-operations__prime-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 5",
    "chapter": "Arithmetic",
    "topic": "Factors and Operations",
    "subtopic": "Prime numbers",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 5 / Arithmetic / Factors and Operations</p><h2>Prime numbers</h2><p>Prime numbers explains a specific idea inside Factors and Operations.</p>\n<p>It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving.</p>\n<p>This helps students use Arithmetic accurately in examples and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Prime numbers</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>Basic idea</h3></div><p>A <strong>prime number</strong> has only factors 1 and itself.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon info\"></span><h3>How it works</h3></div><p>Check whether any number other than 1 and itself divides it exactly.</p></section><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon lightbulb\"></span><h3>Why this is true</h3></div><p>Prime numbers cannot be split into smaller whole-number factor pairs.</p></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Where we use it</h3></div><p>factors, divisibility, fractions, coding, and number theory</p></section><section class=\"common-mistakes\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon alert-circle\"></span><h3>Common mistake</h3></div><p>Students may call 1 prime.</p>\n<p>1 is not prime.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Memory trick</h3></div><p>Prime has two factors.</p>\n<p>Key Point: A prime number has exactly two factors.</p>\n<p>Worked Example: For Prime numbers, use a small example with money or blocks.</p>\n<p>Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</p>\n<p>Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example.</p>\n<p>Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Prime numbers but skip the condition or unit.</p>\n<p>Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem.</p>\n<p>Visual Understanding: Use place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces so the symbols connect to something visible.</p>\n<p>Key Point: Prime numbers becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Prime numbers</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: For Prime numbers, use a small example with money or blocks. Show the value using digits, words, and a number line or place-value model, then compare the results.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Use Prime numbers with money, shopping, sharing food, counting marks, arranging books, or reading time.</li><li>Visual Model: Show the idea using place-value blocks, a number line, equal groups, or fraction pieces and explain what each part represents.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Prime numbers becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Prime numbers is like organizing values so each digit or part has a clear job.</li><li>Use a number line, blocks, or money to see the idea before calculating.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Prime numbers connects to place value, divisibility, rational-number structure, or arithmetic algorithms.</p>\n<p>Later mathematics keeps the same idea but states it using precise operations and proof conditions.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Prime numbers.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489216
  },
