  {
    "sourceRow": 565,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__quadrilaterals__square",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Square",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Quadrilaterals</p><h2>Square</h2><p>A square has four equal sides and four right angles.</p>\n<p>Its properties help us identify the shape and solve angle, side, and diagonal problems.</p>\n<p>These ideas are useful in tiles, windows, frames, patterns, and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Square</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Square must be identified by its defining side and angle properties.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Square</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A square has four equal sides and four right angles.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Every square is a rectangle, but not every rectangle is a square.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Floor tiles and window frames often use quadrilateral properties.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Mark equal sides with ticks and parallel sides with arrows.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Square becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Square is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Square connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Square.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489354
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 566,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__quadrilaterals__kite",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Kite",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Quadrilaterals</p><h2>Kite</h2><p>A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides.</p>\n<p>Its properties help us identify the shape and solve angle, side, and diagonal problems.</p>\n<p>These ideas are useful in tiles, windows, frames, patterns, and proofs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Kite</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Kite must be identified by its defining side and angle properties.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Kite</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A kite is not usually a parallelogram.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Floor tiles and window frames often use quadrilateral properties.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Mark equal sides with ticks and parallel sides with arrows.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Kite becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Kite is easiest when you draw it.</li><li>Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Kite connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations.</p>\n<p>In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Create one example for Kite.</li><li>Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489355
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 567,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__visualising-solids__2d-representations-of-3d-objects",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Visualising Solids",
    "subtopic": "2D representations of 3D objects",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Visualising Solids</p><h2>2D representations of 3D objects</h2><p>A 3D object can be represented on flat paper using 2D drawings.</p>\n<p>These drawings show shape, faces, and hidden structure.</p>\n<p>They help understand boxes, buildings, packages, and models.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>2D representations of 3D objects</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>2D drawings represent 3D solids but do not contain full depth.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>2D representations of 3D objects</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A box drawn on paper represents a cuboid.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A square drawing may represent one face, not the whole cube.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Architects use 2D drawings to plan 3D buildings.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Compare the front view, side view, and top view of a box.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>2D representations of 3D objects becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A 2D drawing is a shadow or plan of a 3D object.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Projection methods include orthographic and perspective projection.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw a cuboid and label one face, one edge, and one vertex.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 568,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__visualising-solids__nets",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Visualising Solids",
    "subtopic": "Nets",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Visualising Solids</p><h2>Nets</h2><p>A net is a flat pattern that folds into a 3D solid.</p>\n<p>It shows all faces laid out in 2D.</p>\n<p>It helps design boxes, packaging, and paper models.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Nets</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A valid net must fold into the solid without overlap or gaps.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Nets</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Six connected squares can form a cube net if arranged correctly.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Six squares in one straight line cannot close into a cube.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Cardboard box templates are nets.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine lifting faces up around a base face.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Nets becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A net is a flat dress pattern for a solid.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>A polyhedron can have many non-congruent nets; cube has 11 distinct nets.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Sketch one valid cube net and explain how it folds.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 569,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__visualising-solids__euler-relation-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Visualising Solids",
    "subtopic": "Euler relation introduction",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Visualising Solids</p><h2>Euler relation introduction</h2><p>Euler relation connects faces, vertices, and edges of many solids.</p>\n<p>It reveals a deep pattern in polyhedra.</p>\n<p>It helps check whether a solid’s counted parts are consistent.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Euler relation introduction</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Euler relation checks the structure of convex polyhedra.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Euler relation introduction</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A cube satisfies 6 + 8 - 12 = 2.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A sphere has no polygonal faces or edges for this formula.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Model builders can check solid meshes using Euler relation.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Label faces, corners, and edges in different colours.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Euler relation introduction becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Euler relation is a counting test for many flat-faced solids.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>More generally, F - E + V is the Euler characteristic, a topological invariant.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Check Euler relation for a triangular prism: F = 5, V = 6, E = 9.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489356
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 570,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__visualising-solids__views",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Visualising Solids",
    "subtopic": "Views",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Visualising Solids</p><h2>Views</h2><p>Views show a 3D object from different directions.</p>\n<p>Common views are front, side, and top.</p>\n<p>They help understand shapes that are hard to see from one drawing.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Views</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Different views reveal different information about a 3D object.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Views</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A cylinder top view may look like a circle.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A front view is not the same as a top view.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Builders read front and top views in plans.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Place arrows labelled front, side, and top around the object.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Views becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A view is what your eye sees from one side.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Orthographic projection preserves measurements along axes parallel to the viewing plane.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Draw front, side, and top views of a cuboid.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489357
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 571,
    "id": "class-8__geometry__visualising-solids__maps",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Visualising Solids",
    "subtopic": "Maps",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Geometry / Visualising Solids</p><h2>Maps</h2><p>Maps represent real places using scale and symbols.</p>\n<p>They turn large spaces into readable drawings.</p>\n<p>They help understand position, distance, direction, and routes.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Maps</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>A map uses scale to represent real distances proportionally.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Maps</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: 2 cm on a map may represent 10 km.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A drawing without scale cannot give exact real distance.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Train route maps and road maps use scale ideas.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Draw a line on a map and compare it with the scale bar.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Maps becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>A map is a smaller, coded version of a real place.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Map projections can distort distance, area, shape, or direction depending on design.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>On a map, 3 cm represents 15 km.</li><li>What does 7 cm represent?</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489357
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 572,
    "id": "class-8__mensuration__area-surface-area-volume__area-trapezium",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Area Surface Area Volume",
    "subtopic": "Area trapezium",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Mensuration / Area Surface Area Volume</p><h2>Area trapezium</h2><p>A trapezium has one pair of parallel sides.</p>\n<p>Its area uses the average of the parallel sides and the height.</p>\n<p>It helps measure fields, roofs, and shapes with one pair of parallel sides.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Area trapezium</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Trapezium area uses the two parallel sides and perpendicular height.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Area trapezium</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Parallel sides 6 and 10, height 4 gives area 32.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Non-parallel sides are not used as bases in this formula.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: A trapezium-shaped plot can be measured this way.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Join two copies to form a parallelogram.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Area trapezium becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Use the middle width between the two parallel sides.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>The formula matches integration of a linear width function across height.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find area when parallel sides are 9 m and 15 m, height 6 m.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489358
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 573,
    "id": "class-8__mensuration__area-surface-area-volume__area-quadrilateral",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Area Surface Area Volume",
    "subtopic": "Area quadrilateral",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Mensuration / Area Surface Area Volume</p><h2>Area quadrilateral</h2><p>A quadrilateral’s area can often be found by splitting it into triangles.</p>\n<p>This works even when no simple formula fits the whole shape.</p>\n<p>It helps measure irregular four-sided plots and designs.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Area quadrilateral</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Quadrilateral area can be found by decomposing it into triangles.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Area quadrilateral</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A kite can be split into two triangles.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: A random quadrilateral cannot always use length x breadth.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: Surveyors split land plots into triangles.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Draw one diagonal and shade the two triangles.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Area quadrilateral becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Turn the four-sided shape into two triangle problems.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Coordinate geometry can compute polygon area using the shoelace formula.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A diagonal splits a quadrilateral into areas 13 m2 and 17 m2.</li><li>Find total area.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489359
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 574,
    "id": "class-8__mensuration__area-surface-area-volume__surface-area-cube",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Area Surface Area Volume",
    "subtopic": "Surface area cube",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Mensuration / Area Surface Area Volume</p><h2>Surface area cube</h2><p>Cube volume measures the space inside a cube.</p>\n<p>A cube has all edges equal.</p>\n<p>This helps measure boxes, dice, tanks, and packing space.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Surface area cube</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Cube volume is side cubed.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Surface area cube</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: Side 3 cm gives volume 27 cm3.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: cm2 measures covering; cm3 measures space inside.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: A dice or cube box uses cube measurement.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine a cube made from unit cubes or covered by six square stickers.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Surface area cube becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fill the cube with tiny cubes.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Scaling side by k multiplies surface area by k^2 and volume by k^3.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A cube has side 5 cm.</li><li>Find its volume.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489359
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 575,
    "id": "class-8__mensuration__area-surface-area-volume__cuboid",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Area Surface Area Volume",
    "subtopic": "Cuboid",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Mensuration / Area Surface Area Volume</p><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>Cuboid volume measures the space inside a box-shaped solid.</p>\n<p>It has length, breadth, and height.</p>\n<p>This helps with cartons, rooms, tanks, and packages.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cuboid</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Cuboid volume equals l x b x h.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cuboid</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A 2 by 3 by 4 box has volume 24 cubic units.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Perimeter is not surface area or volume.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: A shipping box capacity uses volume.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Imagine stacking rectangular layers upward.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cuboid becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Fill the box with unit cubes.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cuboid formulas are special cases of prism formulas: volume = base area x height.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>Find the volume of a cuboid 8 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489360
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 576,
    "id": "class-8__mensuration__area-surface-area-volume__cylinder",
    "classLevel": "Class 8",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Area Surface Area Volume",
    "subtopic": "Cylinder",
    "introduction": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"lesson-introduction\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon book-open\"></span><h3>Lesson focus</h3></div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Class 8 / Mensuration / Area Surface Area Volume</p><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>Cylinder volume measures the space inside a circular tube-like solid.</p>\n<p>This helps measure cans, pipes, tanks, and rolls.</p></section></div>",
    "detailedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card hero-explanation\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Learn clearly</h3></div><h2>Cylinder</h2><p>Start with the main idea, then use the examples to check that the idea is clear in your mind.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key Point</h3></div><p>Cylinder volume is circular base area times height.</p></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Key takeaway</h3></div><p>The central idea is <strong>Cylinder</strong>. Say it in your own words before moving to practice.</p></section></div>",
    "realtimeExamples": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"example-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon pencil\"></span><h3>Worked classroom examples</h3></div><ul><li>Example 1 - Easy: A can is shaped like a cylinder.</li><li>Example 2 - Contrast: Diameter cannot be used as radius.</li><li>Example 3 - Application: A water tank’s capacity uses cylinder volume.</li><li>Visual Understanding: Unroll the curved surface into a rectangle.</li></ul></section><section class=\"real-life-connection\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon globe\"></span><h3>Real-life connection</h3></div><p>Cylinder becomes easier when you connect it with objects, quantities, shapes, patterns, or measurements you can actually see.</p></section></div>",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"step-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Step-by-step method</h3></div><ul><li>Stack equal circular sheets.</li></ul></section></div>",
    "advancedExplanation": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"concept-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon sparkles\"></span><h3>Next mathematical idea</h3></div><p>Cylinder volume follows from Cavalieri’s principle for equal cross-sectional areas.</p></section></div>",
    "practicePrompt": "<div class=\"lesson-content\"><section class=\"practice-card\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon target\"></span><h3>Quick check</h3></div><ul><li>A cylinder has radius 7 cm and height 5 cm.</li><li>Find its volume using pi = 22/7.</li></ul></section><section class=\"key-takeaways\"><div class=\"section-heading\"><span class=\"lesson-icon check-circle\"></span><h3>Before you go next</h3></div><ul><li>Can you state the main idea?</li><li>Can you give one example?</li><li>Can you avoid the common mistake?</li></ul></section></div>",
    "updatedAt": 1786272489360
  },
