  {
    "sourceRow": 634,
    "id": "class-9__geometry__quadrilaterals__parallelogram-theorems",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Parallelogram theorems",
    "introduction": "A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel. Its theorems describe equal sides, equal angles, and diagonals. They help prove a shape is a parallelogram and find missing lengths.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Opposite sides are equal, opposite angles are equal, and diagonals bisect each other.\n\nHow It Works: First prove or identify parallel opposite sides. Then apply the matching parallelogram property.\n\nWhy It Works: A diagonal divides a parallelogram into two congruent triangles, giving equal corresponding parts.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in geometry proofs, constructions, graphs, measurement, and problem solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the result when its conditions are not fully satisfied.\n\nCorrect idea: Check every condition before applying the result. A similar-looking diagram is not proof.\n\nTheorem: In a parallelogram, opposite sides and opposite angles are equal, and diagonals bisect each other.\n\nWorked Example: If ABCD is a parallelogram and AB = 9 cm, then CD = 9 cm.\n\nVerification: Draw diagonal AC and use alternate interior angles to prove triangle congruence.\n\n**Key Point:** Parallelograms have equal opposite parts and bisecting diagonals.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A slanting tile pattern often forms parallelograms.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A kite has two pairs of adjacent equal sides but is not usually a parallelogram.\nExample 3 - Application: Parallelogram frames appear in scissor lifts and drafting tools.\nVisual Understanding: Draw both diagonals. Notice each diagonal cuts the other into two equal parts.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A parallelogram is a pushed rectangle; opposite parts still match.",
    "advancedExplanation": "In vector geometry, ABCD is a parallelogram exactly when A + C = B + D for position vectors.",
    "practicePrompt": "In parallelogram ABCD, angle A = 70 degrees. Find angle C and explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 635,
    "id": "class-9__geometry__quadrilaterals__midpoint-theorem-applications",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Geometry",
    "topic": "Quadrilaterals",
    "subtopic": "Midpoint theorem applications",
    "introduction": "Midpoint theorem applications use triangle midpoint facts inside larger figures. They often prove parallel sides or find half-lengths. They simplify quadrilateral proofs by creating useful triangles.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Join midpoints, then use the midpoint theorem in a triangle.\n\nHow It Works: Choose the triangle containing the midpoint segment. Check both endpoints are midpoints. Apply parallel and half-length conclusions.\n\nWhy It Works: The midpoint segment forms a triangle similar to the original with scale factor 1/2.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in geometry proofs, constructions, graphs, measurement, and problem solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the result when its conditions are not fully satisfied.\n\nCorrect idea: Check every condition before applying the result. A similar-looking diagram is not proof.\n\nApplication: The midpoint theorem can prove properties of quadrilaterals by working inside their triangles.\n\nWorked Example: In quadrilateral ABCD, if E and F are midpoints of AB and AC, then EF is parallel to BC and EF = BC/2.\n\nVerification: Check the answer against the original conditions.\n\n**Key Point:** Midpoint theorem applications work by finding the right triangle inside the figure.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Joining midpoints in a paper triangle creates a smaller matching triangle.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Joining random points on sides does not guarantee a parallel half-length segment.\nExample 3 - Application: Map scaling and frame design use midpoint segments.\nVisual Understanding: Draw a diagonal to split the quadrilateral into triangles, then mark midpoints.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Break the bigger shape into triangles. Midpoints then reveal parallel mini-sides.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The theorem is an affine result, so it remains true under shearing transformations that preserve midpoints and parallelism.",
    "practicePrompt": "Draw quadrilateral ABCD with diagonal AC. Mark midpoints on AB and BC. What theorem can you use?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
