  {
    "sourceRow": 644,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__heron-formula",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Heron formula",
    "introduction": "Heron's formula finds a triangle's area when all three sides are known. It avoids needing a height.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use side lengths a, b, c and semiperimeter s.\n\nHow It Works: Find s=(a+b+c)/2, then substitute in the formula.\n\nWhy It Works: The formula comes from expressing height using side lengths and simplifying.\n\nWhere We Use It: Surveying, land plots, and triangle measurements use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the formula when the side lengths cannot form a triangle.\n\nCorrect idea: Check triangle inequality before calculating area.\n\nFormula: Area = sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)).\n\nWorked Example: For sides 3,4,5, s=6, area=sqrt(6x3x2x1)=6 square units.\n\nVerification: A 3-4-5 right triangle also has area 1/2 x 3 x 4 = 6.\n\n**Key Point:** Area = sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For sides 3,4,5, s=6, area=sqrt(6x3x2x1)=6 square units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check triangle inequality before calculating area.\nExample 3 - Application: Surveying, land plots, and triangle measurements use it.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine the height hidden inside the three side lengths.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Three sides can give area without height.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Heron's formula is equivalent to the determinant-based area formula after algebraic simplification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the area of a triangle with sides 5, 5, and 6.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 645,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__area-triangle",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Area triangle",
    "introduction": "Triangle area measures the surface inside a triangle. It uses a base and the perpendicular height.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose any side as base and measure height perpendicular to it.\n\nHow It Works: Multiply base by height and divide by 2.\n\nWhy It Works: Two equal triangles make a parallelogram of area base times height.\n\nWhere We Use It: Fields, roofs, and design patterns use triangle area.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a slant side as height.\n\nCorrect idea: Height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.\n\nFormula: Area = 1/2 x base x height.\n\nWorked Example: If base=10 cm and height=6 cm, area=30 square cm.\n\nVerification: Double the triangle to get a 10 by 6 parallelogram.\n\n**Key Point:** Area = 1/2 x base x height.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If base=10 cm and height=6 cm, area=30 square cm.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.\nExample 3 - Application: Fields, roofs, and design patterns use triangle area.\nVisual Understanding: See two copies forming one parallelogram.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Triangle area is half a matching parallelogram.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The formula works for any chosen base with its corresponding altitude.",
    "practicePrompt": "A triangle has base 12 m and height 7 m. Find its area.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 646,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__area-quadrilateral",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Area quadrilateral",
    "introduction": "A quadrilateral's area can often be found by splitting it into triangles. This makes an irregular four-sided shape manageable.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Draw a diagonal to divide the quadrilateral.\n\nHow It Works: Find the area of each triangle, then add them.\n\nWhy It Works: Area is additive when non-overlapping parts exactly fill the shape.\n\nWhere We Use It: Land areas, floor plans, and maps use this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding side lengths and calling it area.\n\nCorrect idea: Side lengths around the edge give perimeter, not area.\n\nMethod: Area of quadrilateral = sum of areas of non-overlapping parts.\n\nWorked Example: A quadrilateral split into triangles of areas 12 and 15 has total area 27 square units.\n\nVerification: The two triangles fill the whole shape without overlap.\n\n**Key Point:** Area of quadrilateral = sum of areas of non-overlapping parts.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A quadrilateral split into triangles of areas 12 and 15 has total area 27 square units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Side lengths around the edge give perimeter, not area.\nExample 3 - Application: Land areas, floor plans, and maps use this method.\nVisual Understanding: A diagonal turns four sides into two triangles.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cut the shape into triangles.",
    "advancedExplanation": "General quadrilateral area can also be expressed using diagonals and included angle.",
    "practicePrompt": "Split a quadrilateral into two triangles with areas 18 and 22. Find total area.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 647,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cube",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cube",
    "introduction": "A cube is a solid with six equal square faces. All edges have the same length.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use side length a for every edge.\n\nHow It Works: Surface area counts six square faces; volume counts unit cubes inside.\n\nWhy It Works: Equal edges make every face congruent and square.\n\nWhere We Use It: Dice, boxes, and blocks model cubes.\n\nCommon mistake: Using rectangle formulas with different length, breadth, and height.\n\nCorrect idea: For a cube, l=b=h=a.\n\nFormulas: Surface area = 6a^2; Volume = a^3.\n\nWorked Example: If a=4 cm, surface area=96 square cm and volume=64 cubic cm.\n\nVerification: Count 4x4 squares on each of 6 faces; volume has 4x4x4 unit cubes.\n\n**Key Point:** Surface area = 6a^2; Volume = a^3.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a=4 cm, surface area=96 square cm and volume=64 cubic cm.\nExample 2 - Contrast: For a cube, l=b=h=a.\nExample 3 - Application: Dice, boxes, and blocks model cubes.\nVisual Understanding: Picture six identical square faces.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A cube is a perfectly equal box.",
    "advancedExplanation": "A cube is a regular hexahedron and one of the Platonic solids.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find surface area and volume of a cube of side 7 cm.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 648,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cuboid",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cuboid",
    "introduction": "A cuboid is a box-shaped solid with rectangular faces. Length, breadth, and height may differ.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use l, b, and h as three perpendicular dimensions.\n\nHow It Works: Compute face areas for surface area and multiply lbh for volume.\n\nWhy It Works: Volume counts layers of rectangular grids stacked by height.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rooms, books, tanks, and packages are cuboids.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing surface area with volume.\n\nCorrect idea: Surface area uses square units; volume uses cubic units.\n\nFormulas: TSA = 2(lb+bh+hl); Volume = lbh.\n\nWorked Example: For l=5,b=3,h=2, volume=30 cubic units and TSA=62 square units.\n\nVerification: Add areas of three face pairs: 15,6,10 doubled.\n\n**Key Point:** TSA = 2(lb+bh+hl); Volume = lbh.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For l=5,b=3,h=2, volume=30 cubic units and TSA=62 square units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Surface area uses square units; volume uses cubic units.\nExample 3 - Application: Rooms, books, tanks, and packages are cuboids.\nVisual Understanding: See layers of rectangles stacked upward.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cuboid is a rectangular box.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cuboids are rectangular prisms in three-dimensional Euclidean geometry.",
    "practicePrompt": "A box is 8 cm by 4 cm by 3 cm. Find volume.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 649,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cylinder",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cylinder",
    "introduction": "A cylinder has two circular bases and one curved surface. Its size depends on radius and height.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use the base circle area and height.\n\nHow It Works: Volume is base area times height; curved surface unwraps into a rectangle.\n\nWhy It Works: Stacking equal circles creates the volume; circumference times height gives curved area.\n\nWhere We Use It: Cans, pipes, and water tanks use cylinder formulas.\n\nCommon mistake: Using diameter as radius.\n\nCorrect idea: Radius is half the diameter.\n\nFormulas: Volume = pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = 2 pi r h.\n\nWorked Example: If r=3 and h=10, volume=90pi cubic units.\n\nVerification: Base area is 9pi; ten layers give 90pi.\n\n**Key Point:** Volume = pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = 2 pi r h.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If r=3 and h=10, volume=90pi cubic units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Radius is half the diameter.\nExample 3 - Application: Cans, pipes, and water tanks use cylinder formulas.\nVisual Understanding: Unroll the side into a rectangle.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cylinder is a stack of equal circles.",
    "advancedExplanation": "A right circular cylinder is a prism-like solid with circular cross-sections.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find volume of a cylinder with radius 4 cm and height 6 cm.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 650,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__cone",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Cone",
    "introduction": "A cone has one circular base and one pointed vertex. Its volume is one-third of a matching cylinder.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use radius, height, and slant height when needed.\n\nHow It Works: Volume uses perpendicular height; curved surface area uses slant height.\n\nWhy It Works: Three equal cones fill a cylinder with the same base and height.\n\nWhere We Use It: Ice-cream cones, funnels, and traffic cones use cone formulas.\n\nCommon mistake: Using slant height in the volume formula.\n\nCorrect idea: Volume uses vertical height, not slant height.\n\nFormulas: Volume = 1/3 pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = pi r l.\n\nWorked Example: If r=3,h=4, volume=12pi cubic units.\n\nVerification: A cylinder with same base and height has volume 36pi.\n\n**Key Point:** Volume = 1/3 pi r^2 h; Curved surface area = pi r l.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If r=3,h=4, volume=12pi cubic units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Volume uses vertical height, not slant height.\nExample 3 - Application: Ice-cream cones, funnels, and traffic cones use cone formulas.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine shrinking circular slices up to a point.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cone volume is one-third cylinder volume.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cone formulas follow from similarity or integration of circular cross-sections.",
    "practicePrompt": "A cone has r=5 cm and h=12 cm. Find volume.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 651,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__sphere",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Sphere",
    "introduction": "A sphere is a perfectly round solid where every surface point is the same distance from the centre. That distance is the radius.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use radius r for surface area and volume.\n\nHow It Works: Substitute r carefully and use cubic units for volume.\n\nWhy It Works: The formulas come from adding circular cross-sections or advanced geometric reasoning.\n\nWhere We Use It: Balls, globes, and bubbles are sphere models.\n\nCommon mistake: Using diameter as radius.\n\nCorrect idea: If diameter is given, divide by 2 first.\n\nFormulas: Surface area = 4 pi r^2; Volume = 4/3 pi r^3.\n\nWorked Example: If r=3, surface area=36pi and volume=36pi cubic units.\n\nVerification: Check units: area is square, volume is cubic.\n\n**Key Point:** Surface area = 4 pi r^2; Volume = 4/3 pi r^3.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If r=3, surface area=36pi and volume=36pi cubic units.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If diameter is given, divide by 2 first.\nExample 3 - Application: Balls, globes, and bubbles are sphere models.\nVisual Understanding: Every radius reaches the surface equally.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Sphere is a 3D circle-like solid.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Sphere volume can be derived using Cavalieri's principle or integration.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find surface area of a sphere with diameter 10 cm.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 652,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__hemisphere",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Hemisphere",
    "introduction": "A hemisphere is half of a sphere. It has one curved surface and one circular flat face.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use sphere formulas and include the base circle when total surface area is needed.\n\nHow It Works: Curved area is half sphere surface area; total area adds the circular base.\n\nWhy It Works: Cutting a sphere in half creates a flat circular boundary.\n\nWhere We Use It: Bowls, domes, and half-globes use hemisphere formulas.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting the flat circular base in total surface area.\n\nCorrect idea: CSA excludes the base; TSA includes it.\n\nFormulas: CSA = 2 pi r^2; TSA = 3 pi r^2; Volume = 2/3 pi r^3.\n\nWorked Example: If r=3, curved area=18pi, total area=27pi, volume=18pi.\n\nVerification: Double the volume to get the sphere volume 36pi.\n\n**Key Point:** CSA = 2 pi r^2; TSA = 3 pi r^2; Volume = 2/3 pi r^3.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If r=3, curved area=18pi, total area=27pi, volume=18pi.\nExample 2 - Contrast: CSA excludes the base; TSA includes it.\nExample 3 - Application: Bowls, domes, and half-globes use hemisphere formulas.\nVisual Understanding: Visualize a sphere sliced through its centre.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Hemisphere is half a ball plus a circular base.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Hemisphere measures follow directly from sphere formulas and disk area.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find CSA and TSA for a hemisphere of radius 7 cm.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 653,
    "id": "class-9__mensuration__heron-and-solids__composite-solids-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Mensuration",
    "topic": "Heron and Solids",
    "subtopic": "Composite solids introduction",
    "introduction": "Composite solids are made by joining or cutting simpler solids. Their measurements come from adding or subtracting known parts.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify the basic solids inside the shape.\n\nHow It Works: Add volumes for joined parts and subtract removed parts.\n\nWhy It Works: Volume and surface area are additive when parts do not overlap, but hidden faces are not exposed surface.\n\nWhere We Use It: Tanks, toys, buildings, and containers use composite solids.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding surface areas of all parts including faces glued together.\n\nCorrect idea: Internal joined faces are not part of outside surface area.\n\nMethod: Composite volume = sum of included part volumes - removed part volumes.\n\nWorked Example: A cylinder of volume 100 with a cone of volume 30 on top has total volume 130.\n\nVerification: Check that the parts do not overlap.\n\n**Key Point:** Composite volume = sum of included part volumes - removed part volumes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A cylinder of volume 100 with a cone of volume 30 on top has total volume 130.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Internal joined faces are not part of outside surface area.\nExample 3 - Application: Tanks, toys, buildings, and containers use composite solids.\nVisual Understanding: Color only the outside surface to avoid hidden faces.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Break the solid into known pieces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Composite-solid work uses decomposition, often extended by integration for curved or irregular solids.",
    "practicePrompt": "A cuboid has a cylindrical hole removed. Should you add or subtract the cylinder volume?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
