  {
    "sourceRow": 661,
    "id": "class-9__statistics-and-probability__probability__empirical-probability",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Empirical probability",
    "introduction": "Empirical probability is based on results from experiments. It estimates chance using observed data. It helps understand games, surveys, weather estimates, and random experiments.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Probability is estimated as successful trials divided by total trials.\n\nHow It Works: Define the outcome of interest. Count how many times it occurs. Divide by the total number of trials.\n\nWhy It Works: Observed relative frequency tends to settle near the true probability when trials are repeated many times under similar conditions.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coin tossing, dice rolling, quality checks, and sports statistics use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Expecting experimental probability to match theoretical probability exactly in a small number of trials.\n\nCorrect idea: Small samples can vary. Larger repeated trials usually give a more stable estimate.\n\nDefinition: Empirical probability = number of times the event occurs / total number of trials.\n\nWorked Example: A coin is tossed 50 times and heads appears 28 times. Empirical probability of heads = 28/50 = 0.56.\n\nVerification: The probability must lie between 0 and 1.\n\nMemory Trick: Observed wins over total tries.\n\n**Key Point:** Empirical probability uses actual experiment results, not only expected outcomes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Tossing a coin ten times gives observed heads and tails.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Getting 7 heads in 10 tosses does not prove the coin always gives heads.\nExample 3 - Application: A shop may estimate demand by observing previous customer choices.\nVisual Understanding: Make a tally table. The fraction of tallies in one row gives the estimate.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Experimental chance means 'what happened in our trials.'",
    "advancedExplanation": "Empirical probability is relative frequency. The law of large numbers explains why it often approaches theoretical probability as trials increase.",
    "practicePrompt": "A die is rolled 40 times and 6 appears 5 times. Find the empirical probability of getting 6.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 662,
    "id": "class-9__statistics-and-probability__probability__repeated-experiments",
    "classLevel": "Class 9",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Repeated experiments",
    "introduction": "Repeated experiments show how chance behaves over many trials. More trials often give a steadier estimate. It helps understand games, surveys, weather estimates, and random experiments.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Repeat the same random experiment many times and record outcomes.\n\nHow It Works: Define the outcome of interest. Count how many times it occurs. Divide by the total number of trials.\n\nWhy It Works: Observed relative frequency tends to settle near the true probability when trials are repeated many times under similar conditions.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coin tossing, dice rolling, quality checks, and sports statistics use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Expecting experimental probability to match theoretical probability exactly in a small number of trials.\n\nCorrect idea: Small samples can vary. Larger repeated trials usually give a more stable estimate.\n\nDefinition: Empirical probability = number of times the event occurs / total number of trials.\n\nWorked Example: A coin is tossed 50 times and heads appears 28 times. Empirical probability of heads = 28/50 = 0.56.\n\nVerification: The probability must lie between 0 and 1.\n\nMemory Trick: Observed wins over total tries.\n\n**Key Point:** Empirical probability uses actual experiment results, not only expected outcomes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Tossing a coin ten times gives observed heads and tails.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Getting 7 heads in 10 tosses does not prove the coin always gives heads.\nExample 3 - Application: A shop may estimate demand by observing previous customer choices.\nVisual Understanding: Make a tally table. The fraction of tallies in one row gives the estimate.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Experimental chance means 'what happened in our trials.'",
    "advancedExplanation": "Empirical probability is relative frequency. The law of large numbers explains why it often approaches theoretical probability as trials increase.",
    "practicePrompt": "A die is rolled 40 times and 6 appears 5 times. Find the empirical probability of getting 6.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
