  {
    "sourceRow": 664,
    "id": "class-10__number-systems__real-numbers__fundamental-theorem-of-arithmetic",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Number Systems",
    "topic": "Real Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic",
    "introduction": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic is a specific idea in Real Numbers. The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of number theory, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Number Systems when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of ignoring conditions such as prime modulus, coprimality, or membership in the correct subgroup.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: 60=2^2 x 3 x 5. Any correct prime factorization of 60 uses the same primes with the same powers.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 60=2^2 x 3 x 5. Any correct prime factorization of 60 uses the same primes with the same powers.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Number Systems, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic is about one main check: The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic says every integer greater than 1 has a unique prime factorization, apart from order. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The theorem depends on Euclid's lemma. It is the reason gcd, lcm, and divisibility tests work prime by prime. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Real Numbers.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
