    "subtopic": "Applications",
    "introduction": "Applications is a specific idea in Arithmetic Progressions. Applications of variation use direct or inverse variation to model real relationships before calculating. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Applications of variation use direct or inverse variation to model real relationships before calculating.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Applications. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of algebra, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Algebra when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using a theorem for the wrong algebraic object or a map that does not preserve structure.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: More identical pens cost more money, so cost is direct variation. More workers can reduce time, so time may be inverse variation.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Applications of variation use direct or inverse variation to model real relationships before calculating.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: More identical pens cost more money, so cost is direct variation. More workers can reduce time, so time may be inverse variation.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Applications may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Algebra, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Applications.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Applications is about one main check: Applications of variation use direct or inverse variation to model real relationships before calculating. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The modelling step is deciding whether a ratio stays constant or a product stays constant. Wrong model choice gives wrong answers. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Arithmetic Progressions.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Applications, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
