  {
    "sourceRow": 714,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__standard-angle-values",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Standard-angle values",
    "introduction": "Standard-angle values are exact trigonometric ratios for common angles. They help solve problems quickly without a calculator. They are used in equations, heights, distances, and identities.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Important angles include 0, 30, 45, 60, and 90 degrees.\n\nHow It Works: Use standard triangles: 45-45-90 and 30-60-90. Read the side ratios and simplify.\n\nWhy It Works: These special triangles have exact side ratios, so their trigonometric values are exact.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in geometry proofs, constructions, graphs, measurement, and problem solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the result when its conditions are not fully satisfied.\n\nCorrect idea: Check every condition before applying the result. A similar-looking diagram is not proof.\n\nTable: Standard values come from special right triangles and the unit circle.\n\nWorked Example: In a 30-60-90 triangle, sides are 1, sqrt3, 2. Therefore sin30 degrees = 1/2 and cos30 degrees = sqrt3/2.\n\nVerification: Check the answer against the original conditions.\n\nMemory Trick: For sin 0,30,45,60,90: sqrt(0,1,2,3,4)/2.\n\n**Key Point:** Standard-angle values are exact ratios from special triangles.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A square cut diagonally gives a 45-45-90 triangle.\nExample 2 - Contrast: sin30 degrees = 1/2, but sin60 degrees = sqrt3/2.\nExample 3 - Application: Standard values simplify many Class 10 trigonometry problems.\nVisual Understanding: Draw the two special triangles and write their side ratios.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Memorise the values by understanding the two special triangles.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Standard values also appear as coordinates on the unit circle at special angles.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find sin60 degrees, cos45 degrees, and tan30 degrees using special triangles.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 715,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__relationships",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Relationships",
    "introduction": "Trigonometric relationships connect the six ratios. They help convert one ratio into another. They reduce work in identities and equation solving.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: cosec theta = 1/sin theta, sec theta = 1/cos theta, cot theta = 1/tan theta, and tan theta = sin theta/cos theta.\n\nHow It Works: Start from sine, cosine, and tangent. Use reciprocal or quotient relationships carefully.\n\nWhy It Works: The relationships come directly from side ratios in the same right triangle.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in geometry proofs, constructions, graphs, measurement, and problem solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the result when its conditions are not fully satisfied.\n\nCorrect idea: Check every condition before applying the result. A similar-looking diagram is not proof.\n\nIdentity: tan theta = sin theta/cos theta and reciprocal identities connect the six ratios.\n\nWorked Example: If sin theta = 3/5 and cos theta = 4/5, then tan theta = (3/5)/(4/5) = 3/4.\n\nVerification: Check the answer against the original conditions.\n\nMemory Trick: Reciprocal means flip the fraction.\n\n**Key Point:** Trigonometric relationships come from comparing the same triangle sides.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a fraction is 3/5, its reciprocal is 5/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: sin theta + cos theta is not generally equal to 1.\nExample 3 - Application: Relationships help simplify trigonometric expressions.\nVisual Understanding: Write all ratios from one labelled right triangle and compare them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Most trig ratios are built from sine and cosine.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Quotient and reciprocal identities remain valid wherever denominators are non-zero in the unit-circle definition.",
    "practicePrompt": "If tan theta = 5/12, find cot theta and explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 716,
    "id": "class-10__trigonometry__trigonometric-ratios__identities",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Trigonometry",
    "topic": "Trigonometric Ratios",
    "subtopic": "Identities",
    "introduction": "Trigonometric identities are equations true for all allowed angle values. They are not solved like ordinary equations. They help simplify expressions and prove trigonometric results.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The main identity is sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta = 1.\n\nHow It Works: Start with one side. Replace ratios using known identities. Simplify until it matches the other side.\n\nWhy It Works: The main identity comes from Pythagoras theorem on a right triangle or unit circle.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in geometry proofs, constructions, graphs, measurement, and problem solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the result when its conditions are not fully satisfied.\n\nCorrect idea: Check every condition before applying the result. A similar-looking diagram is not proof.\n\nIdentity: sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta = 1; 1 + tan^2 theta = sec^2 theta; 1 + cot^2 theta = cosec^2 theta.\n\nWorked Example: If sin theta = 3/5, then cos^2 theta = 1 - 9/25 = 16/25, so cos theta = 4/5 for an acute angle.\n\nVerification: Substitute a standard angle, such as 30 degrees, to test the identity.\n\nMemory Trick: Unit circle gives sin square plus cos square equals one.\n\n**Key Point:** Trigonometric identities are always-true relationships between trigonometric ratios.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A 3-4-5 triangle gives (3/5)^2 + (4/5)^2 = 1.\nExample 2 - Contrast: sin theta + cos theta = 1 is false for theta = 45 degrees.\nExample 3 - Application: Identities simplify equations in physics, waves, and geometry.\nVisual Understanding: On the unit circle, x = cos theta and y = sin theta, so x^2 + y^2 = 1.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "An identity is always true when the expressions are defined.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Pythagorean identities arise from the unit circle equation x^2+y^2=1 and quotient identities follow by division.",
    "practicePrompt": "Prove 1 + tan^2 theta = sec^2 theta by dividing sin^2 theta + cos^2 theta = 1 by cos^2 theta.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
