  {
    "sourceRow": 731,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__grouped-mean-direct-method",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Grouped mean direct method",
    "introduction": "Grouped mean by direct method estimates the average from a frequency table. It uses class marks as representative values.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Multiply each class mark by its frequency, add these products, then divide by total frequency.\n\nHow It Works: Find xi for each class, compute fixi, add fixi, and divide by sum fi.\n\nWhy It Works: Each class mark represents all observations in that class, so weighted average is used.\n\nWhere We Use It: Grouped marks, heights, and survey data use this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Averaging class marks without using frequencies.\n\nCorrect idea: Classes with more observations must have more weight.\n\nFormula: Mean = sum(fixi) / sum(fi).\n\nWorked Example: For xi 10,20 with frequencies 2,3: mean=(2x10+3x20)/5=16.\n\nVerification: Total weighted value is 80 and total frequency is 5.\n\n**Key Point:** Mean = sum(fixi) / sum(fi).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For xi 10,20 with frequencies 2,3: mean=(2x10+3x20)/5=16.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classes with more observations must have more weight.\nExample 3 - Application: Grouped marks, heights, and survey data use this method.\nVisual Understanding: Use class marks as weighted values.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Class marks are data values with weights.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This is a weighted mean; accuracy depends on class marks representing class data well.",
    "practicePrompt": "A table has xi=5,15,25 and fi=2,4,2. Find the grouped mean.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 732,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__assumed-mean",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Assumed mean",
    "introduction": "Assumed mean is a shortcut for grouped mean. It reduces arithmetic by measuring deviations from a convenient value.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose a central assumed mean A, calculate di=xi-A, then average deviations.\n\nHow It Works: Find sum(fidi), divide by sum(fi), and add the result to A.\n\nWhy It Works: Mean shifts with the origin; adding the average deviation to A restores the true mean.\n\nWhere We Use It: Large class marks become easier to handle with this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Choosing A and then forgetting to add it back.\n\nCorrect idea: The average deviation is measured from A, so final mean is A plus that value.\n\nFormula: Mean = A + sum(fidi)/sum(fi).\n\nWorked Example: Let A=20. If sum(fidi)=30 and sum(fi)=10, mean=20+3=23.\n\nVerification: Direct method should give the same mean.\n\n**Key Point:** Mean = A + sum(fidi)/sum(fi).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Let A=20. If sum(fidi)=30 and sum(fi)=10, mean=20+3=23.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The average deviation is measured from A, so final mean is A plus that value.\nExample 3 - Application: Large class marks become easier to handle with this method.\nVisual Understanding: Move from a chosen centre, then come back.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Assumed mean is a smart temporary centre.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is an origin-shift version of the weighted mean formula.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use A=50, sum(fidi)=120, sum(fi)=24. Find the mean.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 733,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__step-deviation",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Step deviation",
    "introduction": "Step deviation is an efficient grouped-mean method for equal class widths. It divides deviations by the common width.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose A and class width h, then compute ui=(xi-A)/h.\n\nHow It Works: Find mean using A + h x sum(fiui)/sum(fi).\n\nWhy It Works: Scaling deviations by h makes numbers smaller; multiplying by h restores the scale.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is useful when class marks are large and equally spaced.\n\nCommon mistake: Using step deviation when class widths are unequal without care.\n\nCorrect idea: The simple formula needs a common class width h.\n\nFormula: Mean = A + h x sum(fiui)/sum(fi).\n\nWorked Example: If A=50, h=10, sum(fiui)=8, sum(fi)=20, mean=50+10x8/20=54.\n\nVerification: Compare with assumed mean using di=hui.\n\n**Key Point:** Mean = A + h x sum(fiui)/sum(fi).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A=50, h=10, sum(fiui)=8, sum(fi)=20, mean=50+10x8/20=54.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The simple formula needs a common class width h.\nExample 3 - Application: It is useful when class marks are large and equally spaced.\nVisual Understanding: Divide deviations by equal steps.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Step deviation shrinks big deviations.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is a linear transformation of deviations, preserving the weighted mean after rescaling.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is common class width needed in the usual step-deviation formula?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 734,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__grouped-median",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Grouped median",
    "introduction": "Grouped median estimates the middle value from a grouped frequency table. It uses cumulative frequency to locate the median class.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find N/2, locate the class whose cumulative frequency first reaches it, then use the median formula.\n\nHow It Works: Identify L, cf, f, h, and N carefully.\n\nWhy It Works: Cumulative frequency shows how many observations lie before each class.\n\nWhere We Use It: Grouped income, marks, and heights often need median estimates.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the class with highest frequency instead of median class.\n\nCorrect idea: Median class is found by cumulative frequency reaching N/2, not by maximum frequency.\n\nFormula: Median = L + ((N/2 - cf)/f) x h.\n\nWorked Example: If L=20, N=40, cf=12, f=10, h=10, median=20+((20-12)/10)x10=28.\n\nVerification: The answer lies inside the median class.\n\n**Key Point:** Median = L + ((N/2 - cf)/f) x h.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If L=20, N=40, cf=12, f=10, h=10, median=20+((20-12)/10)x10=28.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Median class is found by cumulative frequency reaching N/2, not by maximum frequency.\nExample 3 - Application: Grouped income, marks, and heights often need median estimates.\nVisual Understanding: Use cumulative frequency to find the middle class.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Median is the halfway position in grouped data.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This formula assumes data are spread uniformly inside the median class interval.",
    "practicePrompt": "Given N=60, median class 30-40, cf=22, f=18, h=10. Find median.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 735,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__grouped-mode",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Grouped mode",
    "introduction": "Grouped mode estimates the most common value in grouped data. It uses the modal class and nearby frequencies.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Locate the class with highest frequency, then compare it with previous and next classes.\n\nHow It Works: Use the grouped mode formula with f1, f0, f2, L, and h.\n\nWhy It Works: The formula interpolates where the peak likely lies inside the modal class.\n\nWhere We Use It: It helps summarize common marks, sizes, or measurements.\n\nCommon mistake: Calling the midpoint of modal class always the mode.\n\nCorrect idea: The mode may be inside the class, not necessarily at its midpoint.\n\nFormula: Mode = L + ((f1-f0)/(2f1-f0-f2)) x h.\n\nWorked Example: If L=20,h=10,f1=18,f0=12,f2=10, mode=20+(6/14)x10 approximately 24.29.\n\nVerification: The result lies between 20 and 30.\n\n**Key Point:** Mode = L + ((f1-f0)/(2f1-f0-f2)) x h.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If L=20,h=10,f1=18,f0=12,f2=10, mode=20+(6/14)x10 approximately 24.29.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The mode may be inside the class, not necessarily at its midpoint.\nExample 3 - Application: It helps summarize common marks, sizes, or measurements.\nVisual Understanding: Look for the tallest frequency class.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Mode is the peak of grouped frequency.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Grouped mode is an interpolation estimate and can be unstable if neighbouring frequencies are close.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the modal class first, then compute mode for f0=8,f1=14,f2=10,L=30,h=10.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 736,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__empirical-relation",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Empirical relation",
    "introduction": "The empirical relation connects mean, median, and mode for moderately skewed data. It is an approximation, not a theorem for every data set.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use it only when data are reasonably unimodal and skewed.\n\nHow It Works: Substitute two known averages to estimate the third.\n\nWhy It Works: The relation models how mean is pulled more by the tail than median, while mode stays near the peak.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used for quick estimates in statistics problems.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the relation as always exact.\n\nCorrect idea: It is an empirical approximation and may fail for irregular data.\n\nFormula: Mode approximately equals 3 Median - 2 Mean.\n\nWorked Example: If mean=25 and median=23, mode approximately = 3x23 - 2x25 = 19.\n\nVerification: Check whether 19 is plausible from the data distribution.\n\n**Key Point:** Mode approximately equals 3 Median - 2 Mean.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If mean=25 and median=23, mode approximately = 3x23 - 2x25 = 19.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It is an empirical approximation and may fail for irregular data.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used for quick estimates in statistics problems.\nVisual Understanding: Approximation depends on shape.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "It is a shortcut for a skewed single-peak data set.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The relation is Pearson's empirical mode relation and depends on distribution shape.",
    "practicePrompt": "Mean is 40 and median is 38. Estimate mode and state the limitation.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 737,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__statistics__ogive-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Ogive introduction",
    "introduction": "An ogive is a cumulative frequency graph. It shows how many observations are below or above class boundaries.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Build cumulative frequencies, then plot them against class boundaries.\n\nHow It Works: Join plotted points smoothly or with line segments depending on convention.\n\nWhy It Works: Cumulative totals show running counts, so the curve rises as data accumulate.\n\nWhere We Use It: Medians, percentiles, and distribution comparisons use ogives.\n\nCommon mistake: Plotting ordinary frequencies instead of cumulative frequencies.\n\nCorrect idea: An ogive uses cumulative frequency, not class frequency.\n\nDefinition: An ogive is a graph of cumulative frequency against class boundaries.\n\nWorked Example: If cumulative frequencies are 5,12,20, plot these totals at upper class boundaries.\n\nVerification: The graph should not decrease.\n\n**Key Point:** An ogive is a graph of cumulative frequency against class boundaries.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If cumulative frequencies are 5,12,20, plot these totals at upper class boundaries.\nExample 2 - Contrast: An ogive uses cumulative frequency, not class frequency.\nExample 3 - Application: Medians, percentiles, and distribution comparisons use ogives.\nVisual Understanding: Watch the total grow along class boundaries.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Ogive is a running-total graph.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Ogives estimate quantiles by reading positions such as N/2 from the cumulative curve.",
    "practicePrompt": "How can an ogive help estimate the median of grouped data?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 738,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__probability__classical-probability",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Classical probability",
    "introduction": "Classical probability measures chance when all outcomes are equally likely. It gives an exact fraction for simple random experiments.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Count favourable outcomes and total equally likely outcomes.\n\nHow It Works: Divide favourable outcomes by total outcomes.\n\nWhy It Works: Equal likelihood allows each outcome to have the same weight in the count.\n\nWhere We Use It: Dice, coins, cards, and simple games use classical probability.\n\nCommon mistake: Using this formula when outcomes are not equally likely.\n\nCorrect idea: For a biased coin, experimental or model-based probabilities are needed.\n\nFormula: P(E)=number of favourable outcomes / total number of equally likely outcomes.\n\nWorked Example: For one die, P(even)=3/6=1/2 because even outcomes are 2,4,6.\n\nVerification: The answer lies between 0 and 1.\n\n**Key Point:** P(E)=number of favourable outcomes / total number of equally likely outcomes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For one die, P(even)=3/6=1/2 because even outcomes are 2,4,6.\nExample 2 - Contrast: For a biased coin, experimental or model-based probabilities are needed.\nExample 3 - Application: Dice, coins, cards, and simple games use classical probability.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine every outcome as one equal ticket.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Probability is a fair-outcome fraction.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Classical probability is a finite uniform probability model.",
    "practicePrompt": "A bag has 3 red and 2 blue balls, all equally likely. Find P(red).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 739,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__probability__complement",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Complement",
    "introduction": "The complement of an event is everything in the sample space that is not in the event. It helps find probability by subtracting from certainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find P(E), then subtract it from 1.\n\nHow It Works: Use complement when the unwanted cases are easier to count.\n\nWhy It Works: An event and its complement are disjoint and together cover the whole sample space.\n\nWhere We Use It: At least one, not happening, and failure cases often use complements.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding P(E) and P(E') to more than 1.\n\nCorrect idea: They must add exactly to 1.\n\nRule: P(E') = 1 - P(E).\n\nWorked Example: For one die, P(not 6)=1-P(6)=1-1/6=5/6.\n\nVerification: P(6)+P(not 6)=1.\n\n**Key Point:** P(E') = 1 - P(E).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For one die, P(not 6)=1-P(6)=1-1/6=5/6.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They must add exactly to 1.\nExample 3 - Application: At least one, not happening, and failure cases often use complements.\nVisual Understanding: Shade the whole space, then remove E.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complement means everything except the event.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complementation follows from probability additivity over disjoint exhaustive events.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the probability of not getting a head in one fair coin toss.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 740,
    "id": "class-10__statistics-and-probability__probability__compound-outcomes",
    "classLevel": "Class 10",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Compound outcomes",
    "introduction": "Compound outcomes combine results from more than one stage. They help count possibilities in two-step or multi-step experiments.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: List outcomes as ordered pairs or use a tree diagram.\n\nHow It Works: Multiply counts when each first choice can pair with each second choice.\n\nWhy It Works: The multiplication principle counts all combinations of independent choices in separate stages.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coin-and-die throws, outfits, and passwords use compound outcomes.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding counts when choices combine.\n\nCorrect idea: For 2 coin outcomes and 6 die outcomes, there are 2 x 6 = 12 pairs, not 8.\n\nPrinciple: If one stage has m outcomes and another has n outcomes, together they have mn outcomes.\n\nWorked Example: A coin and die have outcomes like (H,1). Total outcomes = 2 x 6 = 12.\n\nVerification: A complete list has 12 ordered pairs.\n\n**Key Point:** If one stage has m outcomes and another has n outcomes, together they have mn outcomes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A coin and die have outcomes like (H,1). Total outcomes = 2 x 6 = 12.\nExample 2 - Contrast: For 2 coin outcomes and 6 die outcomes, there are 2 x 6 = 12 pairs, not 8.\nExample 3 - Application: Coin-and-die throws, outfits, and passwords use compound outcomes.\nVisual Understanding: A tree branches at each stage.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Compound means stages joined together.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Product sample spaces model multi-stage random experiments.",
    "practicePrompt": "How many outcomes are possible when tossing two coins and rolling one die?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 741,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__set-notation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Set notation",
    "introduction": "Set notation gives a compact language for collections. Symbols like belongs to, subset, and braces make membership precise.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use braces for sets and the membership symbol in words when reading.\n\nHow It Works: Write elements once inside braces, usually separated by commas.\n\nWhy It Works: Notation removes ambiguity about what is inside the collection.\n\nWhere We Use It: Solutions, number groups, and event spaces use set notation.\n\nCommon mistake: Repeating an element and thinking the set changes.\n\nCorrect idea: A set records membership; {1,1,2} is the same set as {1,2}.\n\nConvention: A={1,2,3} means A is the set containing 1, 2, and 3.\n\nWorked Example: If A={2,4,6}, then 4 belongs to A and 5 does not belong to A.\n\nVerification: Check membership by reading the listed elements.\n\n**Key Point:** A={1,2,3} means A is the set containing 1, 2, and 3.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={2,4,6}, then 4 belongs to A and 5 does not belong to A.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A set records membership; {1,1,2} is the same set as {1,2}.\nExample 3 - Application: Solutions, number groups, and event spaces use set notation.\nVisual Understanding: Look inside the braces for membership.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Braces are a container label for members.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Set notation underlies relations, functions, topology, and probability spaces.",
    "practicePrompt": "For A={a,e,i,o,u}, decide whether b belongs to A.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 742,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__roster-form",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Roster form",
    "introduction": "Roster form lists the elements of a set. It is useful when the elements are few or follow an obvious finite list.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write all elements inside braces.\n\nHow It Works: Avoid duplicates and order does not matter unless stated separately.\n\nWhy It Works: A set is defined by its members, not by the order in which they are written.\n\nWhere We Use It: Small solution sets and survey categories use roster form.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing the same element many times to show importance.\n\nCorrect idea: Duplicates do not create new set elements.\n\nDefinition: Roster form represents a set by listing its elements inside braces.\n\nWorked Example: The set of factors of 6 is {1,2,3,6}.\n\nVerification: Each listed number divides 6 exactly.\n\n**Key Point:** Roster form represents a set by listing its elements inside braces.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: The set of factors of 6 is {1,2,3,6}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Duplicates do not create new set elements.\nExample 3 - Application: Small solution sets and survey categories use roster form.\nVisual Understanding: List members once.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Roster form is a visible member list.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Roster notation is extensional: two sets are equal exactly when they have the same elements.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the set of letters in the word MATH in roster form.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 743,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__set-builder-form",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Set-builder form",
    "introduction": "Set-builder form describes a set by a rule. It is useful when listing every element is long or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write a variable, a condition, and the set it comes from.\n\nHow It Works: Read it as all values satisfying the condition.\n\nWhy It Works: The rule decides membership by a test instead of a list.\n\nWhere We Use It: Number sets, intervals, and solution sets use set-builder form.\n\nCommon mistake: Giving a rule that does not clearly define membership.\n\nCorrect idea: Every object should clearly pass or fail the rule.\n\nDefinition: {x : condition on x} means the set of all x satisfying the condition.\n\nWorked Example: {x : x is an even natural number less than 10} = {2,4,6,8}.\n\nVerification: Check each listed value satisfies the rule.\n\n**Key Point:** {x : condition on x} means the set of all x satisfying the condition.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: {x : x is an even natural number less than 10} = {2,4,6,8}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Every object should clearly pass or fail the rule.\nExample 3 - Application: Number sets, intervals, and solution sets use set-builder form.\nVisual Understanding: Use a rule instead of a long list.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Set-builder form is a membership test.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is an intensional description, defining a set by property rather than enumeration.",
    "practicePrompt": "Convert {3,6,9,12} into set-builder form using multiples of 3.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 744,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__empty-finite-infinite-sets",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Empty finite infinite sets",
    "introduction": "Sets may have no elements, a countable ending list, or endlessly many elements. These types describe set size.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Count the elements when possible.\n\nHow It Works: Empty has zero elements; finite has a fixed count; infinite does not end.\n\nWhy It Works: Cardinality measures how many elements a set has.\n\nWhere We Use It: Solutions, number systems, and classification use these types.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking empty set contains zero as an element.\n\nCorrect idea: Empty set has no elements; {0} contains one element, zero.\n\nDefinitions: Empty set has no elements; finite set has limited elements; infinite set has no last element.\n\nWorked Example: Solutions of x+1=x in natural numbers form the empty set. {1,2,3} is finite. Natural numbers are infinite.\n\nVerification: Count elements to verify finite examples.\n\n**Key Point:** Empty set has no elements; finite set has limited elements; infinite set has no last element.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Solutions of x+1=x in natural numbers form the empty set. {1,2,3} is finite. Natural numbers are infinite.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Empty set has no elements; {0} contains one element, zero.\nExample 3 - Application: Solutions, number systems, and classification use these types.\nVisual Understanding: Do not confuse empty set with zero.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Empty is nothing inside; infinite never stops.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Finite and infinite cardinalities are central in set theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "Classify {}, {0}, and the set of all whole numbers.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 745,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__equal-sets",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Equal sets",
    "introduction": "Equal sets contain exactly the same elements. Order and repetition do not matter.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check membership both ways: every element of A is in B and every element of B is in A.\n\nHow It Works: Ignore order and duplicate listings.\n\nWhy It Works: Sets are defined by membership, so same members mean same set.\n\nWhere We Use It: Comparing solution sets and categories uses equal sets.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking {1,2,3} differs from {3,2,1}.\n\nCorrect idea: They are equal because membership is identical.\n\nDefinition: A=B if and only if A and B have exactly the same elements.\n\nWorked Example: {a,b,c} = {c,b,a}.\n\nVerification: Check each member appears in both sets.\n\n**Key Point:** A=B if and only if A and B have exactly the same elements.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: {a,b,c} = {c,b,a}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They are equal because membership is identical.\nExample 3 - Application: Comparing solution sets and categories uses equal sets.\nVisual Understanding: Same members means equal set.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Equal sets have same contents.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Equality of sets is extensional equality and is proved by double inclusion.",
    "practicePrompt": "Are {2,4,6} and {6,4,2,2} equal? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 746,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__equivalent-sets",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Equivalent sets",
    "introduction": "Equivalent sets have the same number of elements. Their actual elements may be different.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Count elements in each set.\n\nHow It Works: If the counts match, the sets are equivalent.\n\nWhy It Works: Equivalence compares size, not membership.\n\nWhere We Use It: Matching students to desks and comparing groups use equivalent sets.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing equal sets with equivalent sets.\n\nCorrect idea: {1,2,3} and {a,b,c} are equivalent but not equal.\n\nDefinition: Two finite sets are equivalent if they have the same cardinality.\n\nWorked Example: A={1,2,3}, B={red,blue,green}; both have 3 elements, so they are equivalent.\n\nVerification: Match each element of A with one element of B.\n\n**Key Point:** Two finite sets are equivalent if they have the same cardinality.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A={1,2,3}, B={red,blue,green}; both have 3 elements, so they are equivalent.\nExample 2 - Contrast: {1,2,3} and {a,b,c} are equivalent but not equal.\nExample 3 - Application: Matching students to desks and comparing groups use equivalent sets.\nVisual Understanding: Count first, compare members second.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Equivalent means same count, not same members.",
    "advancedExplanation": "For infinite sets, equivalence is defined by existence of a bijection.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give two equivalent sets that are not equal.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 747,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__subsets",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Subsets",
    "introduction": "A subset is a set whose every element is inside another set. It describes containment.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check each element of the smaller set against the larger set.\n\nHow It Works: If all are present, it is a subset; if one is missing, it is not.\n\nWhy It Works: Subset relation is based on universal membership inclusion.\n\nWhere We Use It: Number systems and solution sets use subsets.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking a set cannot be a subset of itself.\n\nCorrect idea: Every set is a subset of itself because all its elements are included.\n\nDefinition: A is a subset of B if every element of A is also an element of B.\n\nWorked Example: {1,3} is a subset of {1,2,3,4}.\n\nVerification: Check 1 and 3 are both in the larger set.\n\n**Key Point:** A is a subset of B if every element of A is also an element of B.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: {1,3} is a subset of {1,2,3,4}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Every set is a subset of itself because all its elements are included.\nExample 3 - Application: Number systems and solution sets use subsets.\nVisual Understanding: All elements must fit inside.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Subset means fully inside.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Subset relation is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive, giving a partial order.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is {2,5} a subset of {1,2,3,4}? Give the missing-element reason.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 748,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__power-set",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Power set",
    "introduction": "The power set of a set is the set of all its subsets. It includes the empty set and the set itself.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: List subsets by choosing elements in all possible ways.\n\nHow It Works: For n elements, there are 2^n subsets.\n\nWhy It Works: Each element has two choices: included or not included.\n\nWhere We Use It: Counting choices and binary patterns use power sets.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting the empty set.\n\nCorrect idea: The empty set is a subset of every set, so it belongs in the power set.\n\nDefinition: The power set P(A) is the set of all subsets of A.\n\nWorked Example: If A={a,b}, then P(A)={empty set,{a},{b},{a,b}}.\n\nVerification: There are 4 subsets, and 2^2=4.\n\n**Key Point:** The power set P(A) is the set of all subsets of A.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={a,b}, then P(A)={empty set,{a},{b},{a,b}}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The empty set is a subset of every set, so it belongs in the power set.\nExample 3 - Application: Counting choices and binary patterns use power sets.\nVisual Understanding: Each element has in-or-out choice.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Power set is all possible selections.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Power sets are larger in cardinality than the original set, a key idea in Cantor's theorem.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the power set of {1,2,3}. How many subsets should appear?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 749,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__universal-set",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Universal set",
    "introduction": "The universal set contains all objects currently being considered. It sets the boundary for complements.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose the context first.\n\nHow It Works: List all possible objects for that problem before taking subsets.\n\nWhy It Works: Complement and Venn work depend on what the whole universe contains.\n\nWhere We Use It: Survey groups and number-set questions need a universal set.\n\nCommon mistake: Changing the universal set without noticing.\n\nCorrect idea: Complements change when the universal set changes.\n\nDefinition: A universal set U contains all elements under discussion.\n\nWorked Example: If U={1,2,3,4,5} and A={2,4}, then values outside A but in U are {1,3,5}.\n\nVerification: Together A and its complement make U.\n\n**Key Point:** A universal set U contains all elements under discussion.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If U={1,2,3,4,5} and A={2,4}, then values outside A but in U are {1,3,5}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Complements change when the universal set changes.\nExample 3 - Application: Survey groups and number-set questions need a universal set.\nVisual Understanding: Know the boundary first.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Universal set is the full playground.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Universal sets are context-dependent; modern set theory avoids one set of all sets due to paradoxes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Let U be numbers 1 to 10 and A be even numbers. Find A complement.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 750,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__venn-diagrams",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Venn diagrams",
    "introduction": "Venn diagrams show sets as regions. They make overlap, union, intersection, and complement visible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Draw a rectangle for U and circles for sets.\n\nHow It Works: Place elements in the correct region based on membership.\n\nWhy It Works: Regions represent logical combinations of set membership.\n\nWhere We Use It: Surveys, probability, and classification use Venn diagrams.\n\nCommon mistake: Putting shared elements in only one circle.\n\nCorrect idea: Shared elements belong in the overlap region.\n\nRepresentation: A Venn diagram represents set relationships using regions.\n\nWorked Example: If A={1,2,3} and B={3,4}, place 3 in the overlap.\n\nVerification: Check each element's membership in A and B.\n\n**Key Point:** A Venn diagram represents set relationships using regions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={1,2,3} and B={3,4}, place 3 in the overlap.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Shared elements belong in the overlap region.\nExample 3 - Application: Surveys, probability, and classification use Venn diagrams.\nVisual Understanding: Overlap means belongs to both.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Venn diagrams are membership maps.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Venn diagrams visualize Boolean algebra of sets.",
    "practicePrompt": "Draw a Venn diagram for multiples of 2 and multiples of 3 from 1 to 12.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 751,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__union",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Union",
    "introduction": "Union combines all elements that are in either set. It includes elements from A, from B, or from both.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Collect all members from both sets and remove duplicates.\n\nHow It Works: Use the word 'or' inclusively: in A or B or both.\n\nWhy It Works: Sets record membership once, so duplicates are removed.\n\nWhere We Use It: Combining student groups, search results, and event outcomes uses union.\n\nCommon mistake: Leaving out overlap elements because they are in both.\n\nCorrect idea: Overlap elements are included in the union too.\n\nDefinition: A union B = {x : x is in A or x is in B}.\n\nWorked Example: If A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}, then A union B={1,2,3,4}.\n\nVerification: Every listed element is in at least one original set.\n\n**Key Point:** A union B = {x : x is in A or x is in B}.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}, then A union B={1,2,3,4}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Overlap elements are included in the union too.\nExample 3 - Application: Combining student groups, search results, and event outcomes uses union.\nVisual Understanding: Union gathers all members.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Union means everything from both.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Union is associative, commutative, and idempotent.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find A union B for A={a,b,d} and B={b,c}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 752,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__intersection",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Intersection",
    "introduction": "Intersection contains only elements common to both sets. It shows overlap.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check which elements belong to A and B at the same time.\n\nHow It Works: List only shared elements.\n\nWhy It Works: The word 'and' requires membership in both sets.\n\nWhere We Use It: Common friends, shared factors, and overlapping events use intersection.\n\nCommon mistake: Including elements that are in only one set.\n\nCorrect idea: They belong to the union, not the intersection.\n\nDefinition: A intersection B = {x : x is in A and x is in B}.\n\nWorked Example: A={2,4,6}, B={1,2,3,4}; intersection is {2,4}.\n\nVerification: 2 and 4 appear in both sets.\n\n**Key Point:** A intersection B = {x : x is in A and x is in B}.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A={2,4,6}, B={1,2,3,4}; intersection is {2,4}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They belong to the union, not the intersection.\nExample 3 - Application: Common friends, shared factors, and overlapping events use intersection.\nVisual Understanding: Intersection means common part.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Intersection is the overlap only.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Intersection is associative, commutative, idempotent, and distributes over union.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the intersection of factors of 12 and factors of 18.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 753,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__difference",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Difference",
    "introduction": "Set difference keeps elements in one set after removing elements also in another set. Order matters.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with the first set, then remove members of the second set.\n\nHow It Works: Do not swap A-B and B-A unless checking both.\n\nWhy It Works: The operation is directional, so the starting set controls the answer.\n\nWhere We Use It: Lists, filters, and excluded groups use set difference.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming A-B equals B-A.\n\nCorrect idea: Usually they are different; with A={1,2}, B={2,3}, A-B={1}, B-A={3}.\n\nDefinition: A-B = {x : x is in A and x is not in B}.\n\nWorked Example: If A={1,2,3,4} and B={3,4,5}, A-B={1,2}.\n\nVerification: Elements 1 and 2 are in A but not B.\n\n**Key Point:** A-B = {x : x is in A and x is not in B}.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={1,2,3,4} and B={3,4,5}, A-B={1,2}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Usually they are different; with A={1,2}, B={2,3}, A-B={1}, B-A={3}.\nExample 3 - Application: Lists, filters, and excluded groups use set difference.\nVisual Understanding: Start from the first set.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Difference means remove B from A.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Set difference is not commutative and equals intersection with the complement of B.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find A-B and B-A for A={a,b,c} and B={b,c,d}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 754,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__complement",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Complement",
    "introduction": "Complement explains a specific idea inside Sets. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Sets Relations Functions accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify U first, then remove A from U. How It Works: List all elements in U that are not in A. Why It Works: Complement depends on the chosen universe. Where We Use It: Probability events and Venn diagrams use complements. Common mistake: Finding complement without defining U. Correct idea: Different universal sets give different complements. Definition: A complement = U - A. Worked Example: If U={1,2,3,4,5} and A={2,5}, A complement={1,3,4}. Verification: A and its complement together make U with no overlap. **Key Point:** A complement = U - A. Worked Example: For Complement, write one small example, list the assumptions, apply the rule carefully, and check the answer against the original question. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Complement but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a simple diagram, table, or step-by-step worked example so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Complement becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Complement, write one small example, list the assumptions, apply the rule carefully, and check the answer against the original question.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Complement with classroom examples, simple measurements, games, or organized tables.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a simple diagram, table, or step-by-step worked example and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complement is best learned through one clear example. Name the object, check the condition, apply the rule, and verify the result.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complement becomes deeper when stated with exact assumptions and counterexamples. Advanced mathematics studies which structures preserve the idea and where the rule stops working.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Complement. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 755,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__de-morgan-laws",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "De Morgan laws",
    "introduction": "De Morgan laws connect complements with union and intersection. They show how 'not' changes 'or' and 'and'.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Take the complement of a union or intersection carefully.\n\nHow It Works: Not in A union B means not in A and not in B.\n\nWhy It Works: Logical negation reverses the set operation.\n\nWhere We Use It: Logic, circuits, and probability use these laws.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing complement of union as union of complements.\n\nCorrect idea: The complement of a union is the intersection of complements.\n\nLaws: (A union B)' = A' intersection B' and (A intersection B)' = A' union B'.\n\nWorked Example: Let U={1,2,3,4}, A={1,2}, B={2,3}. (A union B)'={4}; A' intersection B'={4}.\n\nVerification: Both sides match.\n\n**Key Point:** (A union B)' = A' intersection B' and (A intersection B)' = A' union B'.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Let U={1,2,3,4}, A={1,2}, B={2,3}. (A union B)'={4}; A' intersection B'={4}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The complement of a union is the intersection of complements.\nExample 3 - Application: Logic, circuits, and probability use these laws.\nVisual Understanding: Negation swaps union and intersection.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Not-or becomes and-not.",
    "advancedExplanation": "De Morgan laws are Boolean algebra identities for sets and logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Verify one De Morgan law using U={1,2,3,4,5}, A={1,3}, B={3,5}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 756,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__sets__cartesian-product",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Sets",
    "subtopic": "Cartesian product",
    "introduction": "Cartesian product forms ordered pairs from two sets. It is the basis for relations and coordinate grids.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Pair each element of the first set with each element of the second set.\n\nHow It Works: Order matters: first coordinate comes from the first set.\n\nWhy It Works: All possible ordered pair choices are included.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coordinates, tables, and relations use Cartesian products.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking (a,b) equals (b,a) always.\n\nCorrect idea: Ordered pairs depend on position; they are equal only when matching coordinates match.\n\nDefinition: A x B = {(a,b): a in A and b in B}.\n\nWorked Example: If A={1,2}, B={x,y}, A x B={(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\n\nVerification: There are 2 x 2 = 4 pairs.\n\n**Key Point:** A x B = {(a,b): a in A and b in B}.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A={1,2}, B={x,y}, A x B={(1,x),(1,y),(2,x),(2,y)}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Ordered pairs depend on position; they are equal only when matching coordinates match.\nExample 3 - Application: Coordinates, tables, and relations use Cartesian products.\nVisual Understanding: Make a grid of choices.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cartesian product is all first-second pairings.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cartesian products define coordinate spaces and relation domains.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find {a,b} x {1,2,3}. How many ordered pairs are there?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 757,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__ordered-pairs",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Ordered pairs",
    "introduction": "Ordered pairs record two objects in a fixed order. They are used to locate points and define relations.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write the first component first and the second component second.\n\nHow It Works: Compare ordered pairs by matching first with first and second with second.\n\nWhy It Works: Order gives meaning to position, so swapping can change the pair.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coordinates, tables, and input-output rules use ordered pairs.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking (2,5) and (5,2) are the same.\n\nCorrect idea: They are different unless both components match in order.\n\nDefinition: (a,b)=(c,d) exactly when a=c and b=d.\n\nWorked Example: (3,4) is not equal to (4,3).\n\nVerification: On a coordinate plane, they mark different points.\n\n**Key Point:** (a,b)=(c,d) exactly when a=c and b=d.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (3,4) is not equal to (4,3).\nExample 2 - Contrast: They are different unless both components match in order.\nExample 3 - Application: Coordinates, tables, and input-output rules use ordered pairs.\nVisual Understanding: First slot and second slot matter.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Ordered pair means position matters.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Ordered pairs can be defined formally using sets, but their key property is coordinate equality.",
    "practicePrompt": "Are (x,7) and (4,y) equal if x=4 and y=7? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 758,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__cartesian-product",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Cartesian product",
    "introduction": "Cartesian product lists all ordered pairs from two sets. It creates the possible input-output space for relations.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Pair every element of A with every element of B.\n\nHow It Works: Use a table or grid to avoid missing pairs.\n\nWhy It Works: Each first choice can combine with every second choice.\n\nWhere We Use It: Relations, functions, and coordinate geometry use Cartesian products.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing unordered pairs instead of ordered pairs.\n\nCorrect idea: (a,b) must record first set element first.\n\nDefinition: A x B = {(a,b): a in A and b in B}.\n\nWorked Example: A={1,2}, B={3}; A x B={(1,3),(2,3)}.\n\nVerification: Number of pairs is |A|x|B|=2x1=2.\n\n**Key Point:** A x B = {(a,b): a in A and b in B}.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A={1,2}, B={3}; A x B={(1,3),(2,3)}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: (a,b) must record first set element first.\nExample 3 - Application: Relations, functions, and coordinate geometry use Cartesian products.\nVisual Understanding: Use a grid to list pairs.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Make all possible pairings.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cartesian products generalize to higher-dimensional products such as R^n.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find A x B for A={p,q}, B={0,1}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 759,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__relation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Relation",
    "introduction": "A relation from A to B is any subset of A x B. It tells which first-set elements are connected to which second-set elements.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: First form A x B, then choose the ordered pairs that satisfy the rule.\n\nHow It Works: The chosen pairs define the relation.\n\nWhy It Works: A relation is flexible because not every input must connect to exactly one output.\n\nWhere We Use It: Family links, divisibility, and graph connections use relations.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking every relation must be a function.\n\nCorrect idea: A function is a special relation with exactly one output for each input.\n\nDefinition: A relation from A to B is a subset of A x B.\n\nWorked Example: A={1,2}, B={3,4}; R={(1,3),(2,4)} is a relation.\n\nVerification: R is contained in A x B.\n\n**Key Point:** A relation from A to B is a subset of A x B.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A={1,2}, B={3,4}; R={(1,3),(2,4)} is a relation.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A function is a special relation with exactly one output for each input.\nExample 3 - Application: Family links, divisibility, and graph connections use relations.\nVisual Understanding: Choose allowed arrows.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A relation is a selected set of arrows.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Relations are subsets of Cartesian products and can have properties like reflexive or symmetric.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give a relation from {1,2,3} to {a,b} with exactly two ordered pairs.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 760,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__domain",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Domain",
    "introduction": "Domain is the set of all first components used by a relation or all allowed inputs of a function. It tells where inputs come from.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Look at first components of ordered pairs, or read the allowed input set.\n\nHow It Works: For formulas, exclude inputs that break the rule, such as zero denominator.\n\nWhy It Works: The domain controls which inputs are meaningful.\n\nWhere We Use It: Graphs, functions, and equations need domains.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming all real numbers are always allowed.\n\nCorrect idea: Some formulas restrict inputs, such as 1/x excluding x=0.\n\nDefinition: Domain is the set of permitted input values.\n\nWorked Example: For R={(1,a),(2,b),(2,c)}, domain is {1,2}.\n\nVerification: Only first components are collected.\n\n**Key Point:** Domain is the set of permitted input values.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For R={(1,a),(2,b),(2,c)}, domain is {1,2}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Some formulas restrict inputs, such as 1/x excluding x=0.\nExample 3 - Application: Graphs, functions, and equations need domains.\nVisual Understanding: Collect inputs only.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Domain is the input set.",
    "advancedExplanation": "In functions, domain is part of the definition, not only something found later.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the domain of f(x)=1/(x-3). Which input is forbidden?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 761,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__codomain",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Codomain",
    "introduction": "Codomain is the declared target set for outputs. It may include values that the function never actually reaches.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Read the function statement f:A->B; B is the codomain.\n\nHow It Works: Do not confuse codomain with actual outputs.\n\nWhy It Works: The codomain sets the allowed output universe, while range records achieved outputs.\n\nWhere We Use It: Function classification, especially onto, depends on codomain.\n\nCommon mistake: Calling range and codomain always the same.\n\nCorrect idea: They are equal only for onto functions.\n\nDefinition: For f:A->B, B is the codomain.\n\nWorked Example: If f:R->R, f(x)=x^2, the codomain is R but range is non-negative real numbers.\n\nVerification: Negative numbers are in codomain but not range.\n\n**Key Point:** For f:A->B, B is the codomain.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If f:R->R, f(x)=x^2, the codomain is R but range is non-negative real numbers.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They are equal only for onto functions.\nExample 3 - Application: Function classification, especially onto, depends on codomain.\nVisual Understanding: Target set is declared.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Codomain is the target board.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Changing codomain can change whether a function is onto.",
    "practicePrompt": "For f:N->N, f(n)=n+1, identify codomain and range.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 762,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__range",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Range",
    "introduction": "Range is the set of actual output values produced. It shows what the rule really reaches.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Apply the relation or function to allowed inputs and collect outputs.\n\nHow It Works: For ordered pairs, collect second components that appear.\n\nWhy It Works: Range depends on both the rule and the domain.\n\nWhere We Use It: Graphs, equations, and onto tests use range.\n\nCommon mistake: Listing all codomain elements as range.\n\nCorrect idea: Only outputs actually produced belong to the range.\n\nDefinition: Range is the set of actual outputs.\n\nWorked Example: For R={(1,a),(2,a),(3,b)}, range is {a,b}.\n\nVerification: Only a and b appear as second components.\n\n**Key Point:** Range is the set of actual outputs.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For R={(1,a),(2,a),(3,b)}, range is {a,b}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Only outputs actually produced belong to the range.\nExample 3 - Application: Graphs, equations, and onto tests use range.\nVisual Understanding: Collect hit outputs.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Range is what the arrows hit.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Range is the image of the domain under a relation or function.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the range of f(x)=x^2 for x in {-2,-1,0,1,2}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 763,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__types-of-relations",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Types of relations",
    "introduction": "Types of relations classify relations by special properties. Common types include reflexive, symmetric, transitive, and equivalence relations.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check the required property against every relevant ordered pair.\n\nHow It Works: Use counterexamples when one required pair is missing.\n\nWhy It Works: Relation properties describe patterns of connection inside a set.\n\nWhere We Use It: Number divisibility, equality, and similarity use relation types.\n\nCommon mistake: Checking only one example and declaring the type.\n\nCorrect idea: A property must hold for all required elements or pairs.\n\nDefinitions: Reflexive needs (a,a); symmetric needs reverse pairs; transitive needs linked pairs to imply the shortcut pair.\n\nWorked Example: On {1,2}, equality relation {(1,1),(2,2)} is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\n\nVerification: Every element relates to itself, and no false reverse or chain is missing.\n\n**Key Point:** Reflexive needs (a,a); symmetric needs reverse pairs; transitive needs linked pairs to imply the shortcut pair.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: On {1,2}, equality relation {(1,1),(2,2)} is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A property must hold for all required elements or pairs.\nExample 3 - Application: Number divisibility, equality, and similarity use relation types.\nVisual Understanding: Check pairs like a rulebook.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Relation type is a checklist of pair rules.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Equivalence relations partition a set into equivalence classes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is relation {(1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(2,1)} on {1,2} symmetric? Is it transitive?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 764,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__function",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Function",
    "introduction": "A function assigns each allowed input exactly one output. It is a relation with a strict uniqueness rule.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check every input in the domain.\n\nHow It Works: Each input must appear and must not point to two different outputs.\n\nWhy It Works: Uniqueness makes the rule predictable and graphable.\n\nWhere We Use It: Calculators, formulas, and input-output tables use functions.\n\nCommon mistake: Allowing one input to have two outputs.\n\nCorrect idea: That breaks the definition of function.\n\nDefinition: A function from A to B assigns every element of A exactly one element of B.\n\nWorked Example: {(1,2),(2,3)} is a function from {1,2} to {2,3}; {(1,2),(1,3)} is not.\n\nVerification: Input 1 has two outputs in the second relation.\n\n**Key Point:** A function from A to B assigns every element of A exactly one element of B.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: {(1,2),(2,3)} is a function from {1,2} to {2,3}; {(1,2),(1,3)} is not.\nExample 2 - Contrast: That breaks the definition of function.\nExample 3 - Application: Calculators, formulas, and input-output tables use functions.\nVisual Understanding: One input, one output.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Function means one output per input.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Functions are single-valued relations with specified domain and codomain.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is {(1,a),(2,a),(3,b)} a function? Explain using inputs.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 765,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__one-one",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "One-one",
    "introduction": "A one-one function sends different inputs to different outputs. No two inputs collide at the same output.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Compare outputs of different inputs.\n\nHow It Works: If f(a)=f(b) always forces a=b, the function is one-one.\n\nWhy It Works: Unique outputs allow the function to be reversed on its range.\n\nWhere We Use It: IDs, roll numbers, and inverse functions use one-one rules.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking every increasing-looking table is one-one without checking all rows.\n\nCorrect idea: Find whether any two different inputs share an output.\n\nDefinition: A function is one-one if different inputs have different outputs.\n\nWorked Example: f(x)=2x+1 is one-one because 2a+1=2b+1 gives a=b.\n\nVerification: Algebra proves no collision occurs.\n\n**Key Point:** A function is one-one if different inputs have different outputs.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(x)=2x+1 is one-one because 2a+1=2b+1 gives a=b.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Find whether any two different inputs share an output.\nExample 3 - Application: IDs, roll numbers, and inverse functions use one-one rules.\nVisual Understanding: No two arrows land together.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "One-one means no shared landing point.",
    "advancedExplanation": "One-one functions are injective maps and have left inverses on their images.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is f:R->R, f(x)=x^2 one-one? Give a counterexample.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 766,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__many-one",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Many-one",
    "introduction": "A many-one function lets two or more different inputs have the same output. It is still a function if each input has only one output.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Look for output collisions among different inputs.\n\nHow It Works: If two inputs share one output, it is many-one.\n\nWhy It Works: The function rule remains valid because each input still has a single output.\n\nWhere We Use It: Squaring, grouping, and classifications often create many-one maps.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking many-one means one input has many outputs.\n\nCorrect idea: That would not be a function; many-one means many inputs to one output.\n\nDefinition: A function is many-one if at least two different inputs have the same output.\n\nWorked Example: f:R->R, f(x)=x^2 is many-one because f(2)=f(-2)=4.\n\nVerification: Both inputs have one output each.\n\n**Key Point:** A function is many-one if at least two different inputs have the same output.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f:R->R, f(x)=x^2 is many-one because f(2)=f(-2)=4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: That would not be a function; many-one means many inputs to one output.\nExample 3 - Application: Squaring, grouping, and classifications often create many-one maps.\nVisual Understanding: Many arrows may land together.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Many-one means different inputs can meet.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Many-one functions are not injective and cannot have a two-sided inverse on the original domain.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give two different inputs with the same output for f(x)=|x|.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 767,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__into",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Into",
    "introduction": "An into function does not use every element of its codomain. Some target values are left unused.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Compare range with codomain.\n\nHow It Works: If range is a proper subset of codomain, the function is into.\n\nWhy It Works: The codomain may be larger than the actual outputs reached.\n\nWhere We Use It: Function classification and inverse questions use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Ignoring codomain and looking only at formula.\n\nCorrect idea: Into or onto depends on the declared codomain.\n\nDefinition: A function f:A->B is into if its range is a proper subset of B.\n\nWorked Example: f:R->R, f(x)=x^2 is into because negative real numbers are not outputs.\n\nVerification: Range is [0,infinity), not all R.\n\n**Key Point:** A function f:A->B is into if its range is a proper subset of B.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f:R->R, f(x)=x^2 is into because negative real numbers are not outputs.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Into or onto depends on the declared codomain.\nExample 3 - Application: Function classification and inverse questions use this idea.\nVisual Understanding: Look for unused targets.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Into means some target spots are empty.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Into functions fail surjectivity; changing codomain to the range makes them onto.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is f:N->N, f(n)=n+1 into? Which codomain value is missed?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 768,
    "id": "class-11__sets-relations-functions__relations-and-functions__onto",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Sets Relations Functions",
    "topic": "Relations and Functions",
    "subtopic": "Onto",
    "introduction": "An onto function reaches every element of its codomain. Every target value has at least one input.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Take an arbitrary codomain value y and find an input x with f(x)=y.\n\nHow It Works: If this works for all y, the function is onto.\n\nWhy It Works: Onto means the range equals the codomain.\n\nWhere We Use It: Solving equations and inverse-function checks use onto functions.\n\nCommon mistake: Saying a function is onto because it has many outputs.\n\nCorrect idea: It must hit every codomain element, not merely many of them.\n\nDefinition: A function f:A->B is onto if range equals B.\n\nWorked Example: f:R->R, f(x)=x+3 is onto because for any y, x=y-3 gives f(x)=y.\n\nVerification: Substitute x=y-3 to get y.\n\n**Key Point:** A function f:A->B is onto if range equals B.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f:R->R, f(x)=x+3 is onto because for any y, x=y-3 gives f(x)=y.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It must hit every codomain element, not merely many of them.\nExample 3 - Application: Solving equations and inverse-function checks use onto functions.\nVisual Understanding: Every target must be hit.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Onto means no target is missed.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Onto functions are surjective maps and have right inverses under suitable choice assumptions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is f:R->R, f(x)=e^x onto? Name a missed output.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
