  {
    "sourceRow": 772,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__imaginary-unit",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Imaginary unit",
    "introduction": "Imaginary unit explains a specific idea inside Complex Numbers. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Algebra accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use i whenever a square root of -1 appears. How It Works: Rewrite sqrt(-a) as sqrt(a)i for positive a. Why It Works: No real number squares to -1, so a new unit extends the number system. Where We Use It: Quadratic equations and electrical mathematics use i. Common mistake: Thinking i is a real number. Correct idea: i is not real; it belongs to complex numbers. Definition: i^2 = -1. Worked Example: sqrt(-25)=5i because (5i)^2=25i^2=-25. Verification: Squaring 5i gives -25. **Key Point:** i^2 = -1. Worked Example: For Imaginary unit, choose a simple value or equation. Work one step at a time, keep both sides balanced, and substitute the answer back to check that the original statement is true. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Imaginary unit but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a balance scale, table of values, expression tree, or graph so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Imaginary unit becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Imaginary unit, choose a simple value or equation. Work one step at a time, keep both sides balanced, and substitute the answer back to check that the original statement is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Imaginary unit with finding an unknown price, comparing mobile plans, balancing scores, or changing a recipe.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a balance scale, table of values, expression tree, or graph and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Imaginary unit is like keeping a balance. Each step must preserve the same value or relationship, so the final answer still fits the original problem.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Imaginary unit connects symbols with structure-preserving operations. In higher algebra, the same reasoning appears through equations, functions, matrices, rings, vector spaces, or abstract maps.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Imaginary unit. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 773,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__complex-numbers",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Complex numbers",
    "introduction": "A complex number has a real part and an imaginary part. It is written as a+bi.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify a as the real part and b as the coefficient of i.\n\nHow It Works: Keep real and imaginary parts separate when reading or operating.\n\nWhy It Works: The form a+bi gives every complex number a unique pair of real coordinates.\n\nWhere We Use It: Quadratics, rotations, signals, and AC circuits use complex numbers.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting that b can be zero.\n\nCorrect idea: Real numbers are complex numbers with imaginary part 0.\n\nDefinition: A complex number has form a+bi, where a and b are real and i^2=-1.\n\nWorked Example: 7-3i has real part 7 and imaginary part -3.\n\nVerification: Write it as ordered pair (7,-3).\n\n**Key Point:** A complex number has form a+bi, where a and b are real and i^2=-1.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 7-3i has real part 7 and imaginary part -3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Real numbers are complex numbers with imaginary part 0.\nExample 3 - Application: Quadratics, rotations, signals, and AC circuits use complex numbers.\nVisual Understanding: See a+bi as a point on a plane.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complex numbers are two-part numbers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complex numbers form a field extending real numbers.",
    "practicePrompt": "Identify real and imaginary parts of -4+9i.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 774,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__equality",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Equality",
    "introduction": "Equality of complex numbers means both parts match. The real parts must be equal and the imaginary coefficients must be equal.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Compare real parts and imaginary parts separately.\n\nHow It Works: Set corresponding parts equal to form equations.\n\nWhy It Works: The representation a+bi is unique, so matching value requires both coordinates to match.\n\nWhere We Use It: Solving equations with complex numbers uses this rule.\n\nCommon mistake: Equating only the real parts.\n\nCorrect idea: Both real and imaginary parts must match.\n\nCriterion: a+bi=c+di exactly when a=c and b=d.\n\nWorked Example: If x+2i=5+yi, then x=5 and y=2.\n\nVerification: Substitute x and y back into the equality.\n\n**Key Point:** a+bi=c+di exactly when a=c and b=d.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If x+2i=5+yi, then x=5 and y=2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Both real and imaginary parts must match.\nExample 3 - Application: Solving equations with complex numbers uses this rule.\nVisual Understanding: Match real with real, imaginary with imaginary.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complex equality is coordinate equality.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This follows from linear independence of 1 and i over the real numbers.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find x and y if 3x+4i=12+yi.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 775,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__addition",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Addition",
    "introduction": "Adding complex numbers combines real parts with real parts and imaginary parts with imaginary parts. It works like vector addition.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Group the real parts and the i-parts.\n\nHow It Works: Add coefficients of i just like like terms in algebra.\n\nWhy It Works: Real and imaginary parts are independent directions, so they add separately.\n\nWhere We Use It: Forces, signals, and algebraic expressions can use complex addition.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding real part to imaginary coefficient.\n\nCorrect idea: 3 and 4i are unlike parts, so they do not combine into 7i.\n\nRule: (a+bi)+(c+di)=(a+c)+(b+d)i.\n\nWorked Example: (3+2i)+(5-7i)=8-5i.\n\nVerification: Subtract the second addend from the sum to recover the first.\n\n**Key Point:** (a+bi)+(c+di)=(a+c)+(b+d)i.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (3+2i)+(5-7i)=8-5i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: 3 and 4i are unlike parts, so they do not combine into 7i.\nExample 3 - Application: Forces, signals, and algebraic expressions can use complex addition.\nVisual Understanding: Use two perpendicular directions.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Add horizontal parts and vertical parts.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complex addition is vector addition in R^2.",
    "practicePrompt": "Add -2+6i and 9-4i.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 776,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__subtraction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Subtraction",
    "introduction": "Subtracting complex numbers takes away both the real and imaginary parts. Signs inside brackets matter.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Distribute the minus sign to both parts of the second number.\n\nHow It Works: Then combine real parts and i-parts.\n\nWhy It Works: Subtraction is addition of the opposite complex number.\n\nWhere We Use It: Differences of signals, roots, and points use complex subtraction.\n\nCommon mistake: Changing only the real part sign.\n\nCorrect idea: Both real and imaginary parts of the subtracted number change sign.\n\nRule: (a+bi)-(c+di)=(a-c)+(b-d)i.\n\nWorked Example: (7+3i)-(2+8i)=5-5i.\n\nVerification: Add 2+8i to 5-5i to get 7+3i.\n\n**Key Point:** (a+bi)-(c+di)=(a-c)+(b-d)i.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (7+3i)-(2+8i)=5-5i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Both real and imaginary parts of the subtracted number change sign.\nExample 3 - Application: Differences of signals, roots, and points use complex subtraction.\nVisual Understanding: Minus before brackets flips both parts.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Subtract each direction separately.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complex subtraction is vector displacement in the Argand plane.",
    "practicePrompt": "Simplify (4-9i)-(-3+2i).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 777,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__multiplication",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Multiplication",
    "introduction": "Multiplying complex numbers uses ordinary algebra plus i^2=-1. The imaginary unit changes squared i terms into real negatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Expand using distributive property.\n\nHow It Works: Replace i^2 with -1 and combine real and imaginary parts.\n\nWhy It Works: The rule works because complex multiplication extends real algebra while keeping i^2=-1.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rotations, scaling, and quadratic roots use multiplication.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting to replace i^2.\n\nCorrect idea: Leaving i^2 in the answer is incomplete; use i^2=-1.\n\nRule: (a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i.\n\nWorked Example: (2+3i)(4-i)=8-2i+12i-3i^2=11+10i.\n\nVerification: Use formula: ac-bd=8-(-3)=11 and ad+bc=-2+12=10.\n\n**Key Point:** (a+bi)(c+di)=(ac-bd)+(ad+bc)i.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (2+3i)(4-i)=8-2i+12i-3i^2=11+10i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Leaving i^2 in the answer is incomplete; use i^2=-1.\nExample 3 - Application: Rotations, scaling, and quadratic roots use multiplication.\nVisual Understanding: Area-like expansion with i^2 replaced.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "FOIL, then turn i squared into -1.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complex multiplication combines scaling and rotation in the plane.",
    "practicePrompt": "Multiply (1+2i)(3+4i) and simplify fully.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 778,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__conjugate",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Conjugate",
    "introduction": "The conjugate of a complex number changes the sign of the imaginary part. It is useful for division and modulus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Keep the real part same and reverse the sign before i.\n\nHow It Works: Multiply a complex number by its conjugate to get a real number.\n\nWhy It Works: The imaginary middle terms cancel because they are opposites.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rationalising denominators and finding modulus use conjugates.\n\nCommon mistake: Changing the sign of the real part too.\n\nCorrect idea: Only the imaginary part changes sign.\n\nDefinition: The conjugate of a+bi is a-bi.\n\nWorked Example: The conjugate of 5-2i is 5+2i; product is 25+4=29.\n\nVerification: Multiply to confirm no i term remains.\n\n**Key Point:** The conjugate of a+bi is a-bi.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: The conjugate of 5-2i is 5+2i; product is 25+4=29.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Only the imaginary part changes sign.\nExample 3 - Application: Rationalising denominators and finding modulus use conjugates.\nVisual Understanding: Flip only the vertical sign.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Conjugate is mirror across the real axis.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Conjugation is an automorphism of complex numbers fixing real numbers.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the conjugate of -3+7i and multiply them.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 779,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__modulus",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Modulus",
    "introduction": "Modulus explains a specific idea inside Complex Numbers. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Algebra accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use the real and imaginary parts as perpendicular legs. How It Works: Apply the Pythagorean theorem. Why It Works: Distance from (0,0) to (a,b) is sqrt(a^2+b^2). Where We Use It: Magnitude of signals and distances of complex roots use modulus. Common mistake: Writing modulus as a+b. Correct idea: Distance needs squares and square root, not direct addition. Formula: |a+bi| = sqrt(a^2+b^2). Worked Example: |3+4i|=sqrt(9+16)=5. Verification: A 3-4-5 right triangle confirms it. **Key Point:** |a+bi| = sqrt(a^2+b^2). Worked Example: For Modulus, choose a simple value or equation. Work one step at a time, keep both sides balanced, and substitute the answer back to check that the original statement is true. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Modulus but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a balance scale, table of values, expression tree, or graph so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Modulus becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Modulus, choose a simple value or equation. Work one step at a time, keep both sides balanced, and substitute the answer back to check that the original statement is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Modulus with finding an unknown price, comparing mobile plans, balancing scores, or changing a recipe.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a balance scale, table of values, expression tree, or graph and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Modulus is like keeping a balance. Each step must preserve the same value or relationship, so the final answer still fits the original problem.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Modulus connects symbols with structure-preserving operations. In higher algebra, the same reasoning appears through equations, functions, matrices, rings, vector spaces, or abstract maps.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Modulus. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 780,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__argand-plane",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Argand plane",
    "introduction": "The Argand plane represents complex numbers as points. The horizontal axis is real and the vertical axis is imaginary.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Plot a+bi as the point (a,b).\n\nHow It Works: Move a units horizontally and b units vertically.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex numbers have two coordinates, so a plane represents them naturally.\n\nWhere We Use It: Graphs, roots, and transformations use the Argand plane.\n\nCommon mistake: Putting b on the horizontal axis.\n\nCorrect idea: The real part is horizontal; imaginary coefficient is vertical.\n\nRepresentation: a+bi corresponds to point (a,b).\n\nWorked Example: -2+3i is plotted at (-2,3).\n\nVerification: Read back the point as -2+3i.\n\n**Key Point:** a+bi corresponds to point (a,b).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: -2+3i is plotted at (-2,3).\nExample 2 - Contrast: The real part is horizontal; imaginary coefficient is vertical.\nExample 3 - Application: Graphs, roots, and transformations use the Argand plane.\nVisual Understanding: Horizontal real, vertical imaginary.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complex numbers become map points.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The Argand plane identifies complex numbers with R^2 while multiplication adds rotational structure.",
    "practicePrompt": "Plot 4-5i and name its quadrant.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 781,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__polar-form-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Polar form introduction",
    "introduction": "Polar form writes a complex number using distance and angle. It is helpful for multiplication and powers.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find modulus r and argument theta.\n\nHow It Works: Write z = r(cos theta + i sin theta).\n\nWhy It Works: A point can be described by rectangular coordinates or by distance and direction.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rotations, roots, and trigonometric form use polar notation.\n\nCommon mistake: Using any angle without matching the quadrant.\n\nCorrect idea: The argument must point to the correct quadrant of the complex number.\n\nDefinition: z = r(cos theta + i sin theta), where r=|z|.\n\nWorked Example: For z=1+i, r=sqrt(2) and theta=45 degrees, so z=sqrt(2)(cos45+i sin45).\n\nVerification: Convert back: sqrt(2)cos45=1 and sqrt(2)sin45=1.\n\n**Key Point:** z = r(cos theta + i sin theta), where r=|z|.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For z=1+i, r=sqrt(2) and theta=45 degrees, so z=sqrt(2)(cos45+i sin45).\nExample 2 - Contrast: The argument must point to the correct quadrant of the complex number.\nExample 3 - Application: Rotations, roots, and trigonometric form use polar notation.\nVisual Understanding: Think compass direction from the origin.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Polar form is distance plus direction.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Euler form re^(i theta) is the advanced polar representation.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write 1-i in polar form using its quadrant.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 782,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__complex-numbers__quadratics-with-complex-roots",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Complex Numbers",
    "subtopic": "Quadratics with complex roots",
    "introduction": "Some quadratic equations have no real roots but have complex roots. The discriminant tells when this happens.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Compute b^2-4ac.\n\nHow It Works: If it is negative, use i while applying the quadratic formula.\n\nWhy It Works: Square roots of negative numbers are represented using the imaginary unit.\n\nWhere We Use It: Algebra, signals, and polynomial theory use complex roots.\n\nCommon mistake: Saying the equation has no solution at all.\n\nCorrect idea: It has no real solution, but it has complex solutions.\n\nTheorem: A quadratic with real coefficients and negative discriminant has two complex conjugate roots.\n\nWorked Example: x^2+4=0 gives x^2=-4, so x=2i or -2i.\n\nVerification: Substitute 2i: (2i)^2+4=-4+4=0.\n\n**Key Point:** A quadratic with real coefficients and negative discriminant has two complex conjugate roots.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: x^2+4=0 gives x^2=-4, so x=2i or -2i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It has no real solution, but it has complex solutions.\nExample 3 - Application: Algebra, signals, and polynomial theory use complex roots.\nVisual Understanding: Negative discriminant means complex pair.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complex roots complete quadratic solving.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The fundamental theorem of algebra guarantees complex roots for non-constant polynomials.",
    "practicePrompt": "Solve x^2+2x+5=0 and identify the conjugate pair.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
