  {
    "sourceRow": 801,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__sequences",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Sequences",
    "introduction": "A sequence is an ordered list of numbers following a rule. Each position has a term.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use n to name the position of a term.\n\nHow It Works: Look for a pattern, then write or apply the nth-term rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Order matters because the same numbers in a different order form a different sequence.\n\nWhere We Use It: Savings plans, patterns, and repeated measurements use sequences.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating a sequence like an unordered set.\n\nCorrect idea: In a sequence, first, second, and third positions matter.\n\nDefinition: A sequence is a function whose domain is usually positive integers.\n\nWorked Example: 2,4,6,8 has nth term 2n.\n\nVerification: For n=4, 2n=8, matching the fourth term.\n\n**Key Point:** A sequence is a function whose domain is usually positive integers.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 2,4,6,8 has nth term 2n.\nExample 2 - Contrast: In a sequence, first, second, and third positions matter.\nExample 3 - Application: Savings plans, patterns, and repeated measurements use sequences.\nVisual Understanding: Place terms in numbered boxes.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Sequence means ordered number pattern.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Sequences are functions on natural-number domains and can converge, diverge, or be recursively defined.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the nth term of 5,10,15,20.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 802,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__ap",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "AP",
    "introduction": "An arithmetic progression has a constant difference between consecutive terms. It grows by equal steps.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find the common difference d.\n\nHow It Works: Subtract a term from the next term; use a_n=a+(n-1)d.\n\nWhy It Works: Adding the same amount each time creates a linear pattern.\n\nWhere We Use It: Monthly savings, stair heights, and seating rows can form APs.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a ratio instead of a difference.\n\nCorrect idea: AP checks repeated addition, not repeated multiplication.\n\nFormula: a_n = a + (n-1)d.\n\nWorked Example: For 3,7,11,..., a=3,d=4, so a_10=3+9x4=39.\n\nVerification: Terms increase by 4 each time.\n\n**Key Point:** a_n = a + (n-1)d.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 3,7,11,..., a=3,d=4, so a_10=3+9x4=39.\nExample 2 - Contrast: AP checks repeated addition, not repeated multiplication.\nExample 3 - Application: Monthly savings, stair heights, and seating rows can form APs.\nVisual Understanding: A number line with equal steps shows an AP.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "AP is equal jumps.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Arithmetic sequences are linear functions of n.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the 15th term of AP 8, 11, 14, ...",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 803,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__arithmetic-mean",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Arithmetic mean",
    "introduction": "Arithmetic mean between two numbers is the number halfway between them. In an AP, it sits equally from both ends.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Add the two numbers and divide by 2.\n\nHow It Works: Check equal differences from the mean to each number.\n\nWhy It Works: The midpoint balances the two values by equal distance.\n\nWhere We Use It: Average marks, temperatures, and AP missing terms use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Using geometric mean for numbers in an AP.\n\nCorrect idea: Arithmetic mean uses addition and equal differences.\n\nFormula: AM of a and b = (a+b)/2.\n\nWorked Example: AM of 10 and 18 is 14 because (10+18)/2=14.\n\nVerification: 14-10=4 and 18-14=4.\n\n**Key Point:** AM of a and b = (a+b)/2.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: AM of 10 and 18 is 14 because (10+18)/2=14.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Arithmetic mean uses addition and equal differences.\nExample 3 - Application: Average marks, temperatures, and AP missing terms use it.\nVisual Understanding: Put it halfway on a number line.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "AM is the middle by addition.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The arithmetic mean minimizes the sum of squared deviations for two points.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the arithmetic mean between 7 and 23.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 804,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__gp",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "GP",
    "introduction": "GP explains a specific idea inside Sequences and Series. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Algebra accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find the common ratio r. How It Works: Divide a term by the previous term; use a_n=ar^(n-1). Why It Works: Multiplying by the same factor each time creates exponential-style growth. Where We Use It: Compound interest, population growth, and scale changes can form GPs. Common mistake: Checking difference instead of ratio. Correct idea: GP uses a common ratio, not a common difference. Formula: a_n = ar^(n-1). Worked Example: For 2,6,18,..., a=2,r=3, so a_5=2x3^4=162. Verification: Each term is previous term times 3. **Key Point:** a_n = ar^(n-1). Worked Example: For GP, choose a simple value or equation. Work one step at a time, keep both sides balanced, and substitute the answer back to check that the original statement is true. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of GP but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a balance scale, table of values, expression tree, or graph so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: GP becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 2,6,18,..., a=2,r=3, so a_5=2x3^4=162.\nExample 2 - Contrast: GP uses a common ratio, not a common difference.\nExample 3 - Application: Compound interest, population growth, and scale changes can form GPs.\nVisual Understanding: A tree of repeated multiplication shows a GP.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "GP is like keeping a balance. Each step must preserve the same value or relationship, so the final answer still fits the original problem.",
    "advancedExplanation": "GP connects symbols with structure-preserving operations. In higher algebra, the same reasoning appears through equations, functions, matrices, rings, vector spaces, or abstract maps.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for GP. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 805,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__geometric-mean",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Geometric mean",
    "introduction": "Geometric mean between positive numbers balances them by ratio. It is useful in geometric progressions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Multiply the numbers and take the square root.\n\nHow It Works: For positive a and b, GM=sqrt(ab).\n\nWhy It Works: If a, G, b are in GP, then G/a=b/G, so G^2=ab.\n\nWhere We Use It: Growth factors, ratios, and GP missing terms use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Using GM with negative values at this level without care.\n\nCorrect idea: The simple square-root formula is taught for positive numbers.\n\nFormula: GM of positive a and b = sqrt(ab).\n\nWorked Example: GM of 4 and 9 is sqrt(36)=6.\n\nVerification: Ratios match: 6/4 = 9/6 = 1.5.\n\n**Key Point:** GM of positive a and b = sqrt(ab).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: GM of 4 and 9 is sqrt(36)=6.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The simple square-root formula is taught for positive numbers.\nExample 3 - Application: Growth factors, ratios, and GP missing terms use it.\nVisual Understanding: Balance ratios on both sides.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "GM is the middle by multiplication.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Geometric mean is natural for multiplicative data and log averages.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the geometric mean between 8 and 18.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 806,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__finite-gp-sum",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Finite GP sum",
    "introduction": "Finite GP sum adds a fixed number of geometric terms. It avoids adding many terms one by one.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify first term a, ratio r, and number of terms n.\n\nHow It Works: Use the formula according to whether r is 1 or not.\n\nWhy It Works: Multiplying the sum by r makes most terms cancel after subtraction.\n\nWhere We Use It: Savings, repeated discounts, and growth totals use GP sums.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the infinite formula for a finite number of terms.\n\nCorrect idea: Finite sums need n; infinite sums need a convergence condition.\n\nFormula: S_n = a(r^n-1)/(r-1), for r != 1.\n\nWorked Example: For 2+6+18+54, a=2,r=3,n=4, S=2(81-1)/2=80.\n\nVerification: Direct addition gives 80.\n\n**Key Point:** S_n = a(r^n-1)/(r-1), for r != 1.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 2+6+18+54, a=2,r=3,n=4, S=2(81-1)/2=80.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Finite sums need n; infinite sums need a convergence condition.\nExample 3 - Application: Savings, repeated discounts, and growth totals use GP sums.\nVisual Understanding: Write the sum and shifted sum below it.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Finite GP sum is a shortcut for repeated ratio terms.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The formula comes from subtracting rS_n from S_n to create cancellation.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the sum of 3+6+12+24+48.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 807,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__infinite-gp-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Infinite GP introduction",
    "introduction": "An infinite GP has endlessly many terms. Its sum is finite only when the ratio's absolute value is less than 1.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check |r|<1 before using the sum formula.\n\nHow It Works: If the condition holds, use S=a/(1-r).\n\nWhy It Works: Terms shrink toward zero, so the endless tail has a finite total.\n\nWhere We Use It: Repeating decimals and decreasing bounces use infinite GP ideas.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the formula when r is 2 or -2.\n\nCorrect idea: If |r|>=1, the terms do not shrink to zero enough for this sum.\n\nFormula: For |r|<1, S_infinity = a/(1-r).\n\nWorked Example: 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... has sum 1/(1-1/2)=2.\n\nVerification: Partial sums get closer to 2.\n\n**Key Point:** For |r|<1, S_infinity = a/(1-r).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + ... has sum 1/(1-1/2)=2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If |r|>=1, the terms do not shrink to zero enough for this sum.\nExample 3 - Application: Repeating decimals and decreasing bounces use infinite GP ideas.\nVisual Understanding: Watch shrinking pieces fill a whole.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Infinite sum works only when terms shrink.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Convergence of a geometric series depends exactly on |r|<1.",
    "practicePrompt": "Does 5+10+20+... have a finite infinite sum? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 808,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__arithmetic-geometric-sequence-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Arithmetic-geometric sequence introduction",
    "introduction": "An arithmetic-geometric sequence mixes an arithmetic pattern with a geometric pattern. Terms often look like n times a power.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify the arithmetic part and the geometric part.\n\nHow It Works: Write the nth term as a product, then use suitable sum methods later.\n\nWhy It Works: One factor changes by addition while another changes by multiplication.\n\nWhere We Use It: Advanced series problems and recurrence patterns use this type.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating it as purely AP or purely GP.\n\nCorrect idea: Check both difference and ratio; usually neither stays constant alone.\n\nDefinition: An arithmetic-geometric term often has form (linear in n) times r^n.\n\nWorked Example: Sequence 1,4,12,32 has terms n x 2^(n-1) for n=1,2,3,4.\n\nVerification: Substitute n=4 to get 4x8=32.\n\n**Key Point:** An arithmetic-geometric term often has form (linear in n) times r^n.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Sequence 1,4,12,32 has terms n x 2^(n-1) for n=1,2,3,4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check both difference and ratio; usually neither stays constant alone.\nExample 3 - Application: Advanced series problems and recurrence patterns use this type.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine two patterns multiplied term by term.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "It is AP thinking times GP thinking.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Arithmetic-geometric series can be summed using shifted subtraction or generating functions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the next term if a_n=n x 3^(n-1).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 809,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__sum-n",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Sum n",
    "introduction": "The sum of first n natural numbers has a simple formula. It adds 1+2+3 up to n quickly.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Pair terms from the beginning and end.\n\nHow It Works: Each pair sums to n+1, and there are n/2 pairs in effect.\n\nWhy It Works: Pairing shows the total without adding every number individually.\n\nWhere We Use It: Counting handshakes, dots, and rows uses this sum.\n\nCommon mistake: Using n^2 instead of n(n+1)/2.\n\nCorrect idea: n^2 counts an n by n square, not the triangular total.\n\nFormula: 1+2+...+n = n(n+1)/2.\n\nWorked Example: 1+2+...+10 = 10x11/2 = 55.\n\nVerification: Pair 1+10, 2+9, and so on.\n\n**Key Point:** 1+2+...+n = n(n+1)/2.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 1+2+...+10 = 10x11/2 = 55.\nExample 2 - Contrast: n^2 counts an n by n square, not the triangular total.\nExample 3 - Application: Counting handshakes, dots, and rows uses this sum.\nVisual Understanding: Two identical triangles make a rectangle.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "The sum makes a triangle of dots.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This is the nth triangular number.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find 1+2+...+50 using the formula.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 810,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__sum-n-2",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Sum n^2",
    "introduction": "The sum of squares adds 1^2+2^2 up to n^2. It counts stacked square layers.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use the standard square-sum formula.\n\nHow It Works: Substitute n carefully and simplify.\n\nWhy It Works: The formula comes from polynomial summation and can be proved by induction.\n\nWhere We Use It: Counting square arrays and discrete models uses this sum.\n\nCommon mistake: Squaring the sum 1+2+...+n.\n\nCorrect idea: Sum of squares is not the square of the sum.\n\nFormula: 1^2+2^2+...+n^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.\n\nWorked Example: For n=3, sum=1+4+9=14; formula gives 3x4x7/6=14.\n\nVerification: Both direct addition and formula match.\n\n**Key Point:** 1^2+2^2+...+n^2 = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For n=3, sum=1+4+9=14; formula gives 3x4x7/6=14.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Sum of squares is not the square of the sum.\nExample 3 - Application: Counting square arrays and discrete models uses this sum.\nVisual Understanding: Picture growing square tiles.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Add each square, not the final total squared.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Square sums are degree-three polynomial sums and appear in variance formulas.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find 1^2+2^2+...+5^2 using the formula.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 811,
    "id": "class-11__algebra__sequences-and-series__sum-n-3",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Algebra",
    "topic": "Sequences and Series",
    "subtopic": "Sum n^3",
    "introduction": "The sum of cubes adds 1^3+2^3 up to n^3. It has a beautiful link with triangular numbers.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find the sum of first n natural numbers, then square it.\n\nHow It Works: Use [n(n+1)/2]^2.\n\nWhy It Works: Cubes add to the square of the triangular-number sum.\n\nWhere We Use It: Algebra identities and counting patterns use this formula.\n\nCommon mistake: Using n^3 as the whole sum.\n\nCorrect idea: n^3 is only the last term, not the sum of all cubes.\n\nFormula: 1^3+2^3+...+n^3 = [n(n+1)/2]^2.\n\nWorked Example: For n=3, 1+8+27=36; [3x4/2]^2=6^2=36.\n\nVerification: Direct addition confirms the formula.\n\n**Key Point:** 1^3+2^3+...+n^3 = [n(n+1)/2]^2.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For n=3, 1+8+27=36; [3x4/2]^2=6^2=36.\nExample 2 - Contrast: n^3 is only the last term, not the sum of all cubes.\nExample 3 - Application: Algebra identities and counting patterns use this formula.\nVisual Understanding: Build a square from cumulative cube layers.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cube sum is triangular sum squared.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This identity can be proved by induction or combinatorial arguments.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find 1^3+2^3+...+4^3.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
