    "id": "class-11__coordinate-geometry__straight-lines__slope",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Coordinate Geometry",
    "topic": "Straight Lines",
    "subtopic": "Slope",
    "introduction": "Slope is a specific idea in Straight Lines. Slope studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Slope studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Slope. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of geometry and topology, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Coordinate Geometry when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of confusing a local picture with a global property.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For Slope, use a circle, sphere, torus, or simple surface. Identify the local object and the global invariant being studied.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Slope studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Slope, use a circle, sphere, torus, or simple surface. Identify the local object and the global invariant being studied.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Slope may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Coordinate Geometry, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Slope.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Slope is easiest when you attach it to one small example. Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Slope separates local coordinates from global invariants. Advanced results often measure obstruction, curvature, or homotopy information.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Slope, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
