  {
    "sourceRow": 830,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__intuitive-limits",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Intuitive limits",
    "introduction": "A limit describes the value a function approaches as input gets close to a point. The function need not equal that value at the point.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Watch nearby input values, not only the point itself.\n\nHow It Works: Approach from both sides and compare the outputs.\n\nWhy It Works: A limit captures approaching behavior, so it can exist even with a hole.\n\nWhere We Use It: Motion, graphs, and approximation use limits.\n\nCommon mistake: Substituting the point and stopping when the function is undefined.\n\nCorrect idea: A limit may still exist if nearby values approach one number.\n\nDefinition: lim x->a f(x)=L means f(x) can be made close to L when x is close to a.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1), x near 1 gives values near 2.\n\nVerification: Simplify to x+1 for x not equal to 1.\n\n**Key Point:** lim x->a f(x)=L means f(x) can be made close to L when x is close to a.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1), x near 1 gives values near 2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A limit may still exist if nearby values approach one number.\nExample 3 - Application: Motion, graphs, and approximation use limits.\nVisual Understanding: Trace the graph near, not only at, the point.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Limit is the value approached.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Epsilon-delta definitions make this idea precise.",
    "practicePrompt": "Estimate lim x->2 (x^2-4)/(x-2).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 831,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__algebra-of-limits",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Algebra of limits",
    "introduction": "Algebra of limits lets us combine limits of sums, products, and quotients. It saves work when individual limits are known.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find simple limits first, then apply limit laws.\n\nHow It Works: For quotients, check the denominator limit is not zero.\n\nWhy It Works: Limit laws preserve approaching behavior under ordinary arithmetic operations.\n\nWhere We Use It: Polynomial and rational limit calculations use these laws.\n\nCommon mistake: Dividing by a limit of zero.\n\nCorrect idea: The quotient law requires denominator limit not equal to zero.\n\nTheorem: If limits exist, limit of sum/product follows sum/product of limits; quotient needs non-zero denominator limit.\n\nWorked Example: lim x->2 (x^2+3x)=4+6=10.\n\nVerification: Substitution works for polynomials because they are continuous.\n\n**Key Point:** If limits exist, limit of sum/product follows sum/product of limits; quotient needs non-zero denominator limit.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: lim x->2 (x^2+3x)=4+6=10.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The quotient law requires denominator limit not equal to zero.\nExample 3 - Application: Polynomial and rational limit calculations use these laws.\nVisual Understanding: Break a hard expression into simpler parts.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Limits follow arithmetic when conditions hold.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Limit laws are foundational for continuity and derivative rules.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find lim x->1 (x^2+5)/(x+1). State the quotient condition.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 832,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__standard-trigonometric-limits",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Standard trigonometric limits",
    "introduction": "Standard trigonometric limits are key limits used to derive trig derivatives. They must use radians.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use known limits near zero.\n\nHow It Works: Convert forms to sin x / x or related expressions.\n\nWhy It Works: The unit-circle geometry behind these limits works with radian measure.\n\nWhere We Use It: Trig derivatives, waves, and small-angle approximations use them.\n\nCommon mistake: Using degrees in the standard limits.\n\nCorrect idea: These limits are valid when angles are measured in radians.\n\nLimits: lim x->0 sin x / x = 1 and lim x->0 (1-cos x)/x = 0.\n\nWorked Example: lim x->0 sin(3x)/x = 3 because sin(3x)/(3x) tends to 1.\n\nVerification: Rewrite as 3[sin(3x)/(3x)].\n\n**Key Point:** lim x->0 sin x / x = 1 and lim x->0 (1-cos x)/x = 0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: lim x->0 sin(3x)/x = 3 because sin(3x)/(3x) tends to 1.\nExample 2 - Contrast: These limits are valid when angles are measured in radians.\nExample 3 - Application: Trig derivatives, waves, and small-angle approximations use them.\nVisual Understanding: Zoom near zero on the sine graph.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Small radian angles make sin x close to x.",
    "advancedExplanation": "These limits can be proved by squeeze theorem using unit-circle areas.",
    "practicePrompt": "Evaluate lim x->0 sin(5x)/(2x).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 833,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__derivative-as-rate",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Derivative as rate",
    "introduction": "A derivative measures instantaneous rate of change. It tells how fast one quantity changes at a moment.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start from average rate over a small interval.\n\nHow It Works: Let the interval shrink toward zero.\n\nWhy It Works: The limiting average rate becomes the rate at one instant.\n\nWhere We Use It: Speed, growth, cooling, and cost changes use derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing average rate over a trip with speed at one instant.\n\nCorrect idea: Instantaneous rate is a limit of average rates.\n\nDefinition: f'(a)=lim h->0 [f(a+h)-f(a)]/h.\n\nWorked Example: If s(t)=t^2, average rate from 3 to 3+h is 6+h, so instant rate is 6.\n\nVerification: Derivative s'(t)=2t gives s'(3)=6.\n\n**Key Point:** f'(a)=lim h->0 [f(a+h)-f(a)]/h.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If s(t)=t^2, average rate from 3 to 3+h is 6+h, so instant rate is 6.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Instantaneous rate is a limit of average rates.\nExample 3 - Application: Speed, growth, cooling, and cost changes use derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Shrink the time window around the moment.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Derivative is speed at an instant.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Derivatives define local linear approximation of functions.",
    "practicePrompt": "For s(t)=t^2+1, find instantaneous rate at t=4.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 834,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__derivative-as-slope",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Derivative as slope",
    "introduction": "Derivative as slope gives the slope of the tangent line to a curve. It measures local steepness.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find f'(a), then use it as tangent slope.\n\nHow It Works: A secant slope between two points becomes tangent slope as points merge.\n\nWhy It Works: The tangent is the limiting position of nearby secant lines.\n\nWhere We Use It: Graphs, optimization, and motion diagrams use tangent slopes.\n\nCommon mistake: Using slope between far points as tangent slope.\n\nCorrect idea: Tangent slope is local at one point.\n\nDefinition: The tangent slope at x=a is f'(a).\n\nWorked Example: For y=x^2 at x=2, slope is f'(2)=4.\n\nVerification: Nearby secant slopes approach 4.\n\n**Key Point:** The tangent slope at x=a is f'(a).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For y=x^2 at x=2, slope is f'(2)=4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Tangent slope is local at one point.\nExample 3 - Application: Graphs, optimization, and motion diagrams use tangent slopes.\nVisual Understanding: Watch secant lines settle into one tangent.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Derivative is graph steepness.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Differentiability means the tangent slope is uniquely defined.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find tangent slope of y=x^3 at x=1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 835,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__first-principles",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "First principles",
    "introduction": "First principles define the derivative from the limit of difference quotients. It shows where derivative rules come from.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use [f(x+h)-f(x)]/h.\n\nHow It Works: Simplify, cancel h if possible, then let h approach zero.\n\nWhy It Works: This computes the limiting slope of secant lines.\n\nWhere We Use It: It proves derivative formulas instead of memorising them.\n\nCommon mistake: Substituting h=0 before simplifying.\n\nCorrect idea: That causes division by zero; simplify first.\n\nDefinition: f'(x)=lim h->0 [f(x+h)-f(x)]/h.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2, quotient = ((x+h)^2-x^2)/h = 2x+h, so f'(x)=2x.\n\nVerification: Let h approach 0 after cancellation.\n\n**Key Point:** f'(x)=lim h->0 [f(x+h)-f(x)]/h.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2, quotient = ((x+h)^2-x^2)/h = 2x+h, so f'(x)=2x.\nExample 2 - Contrast: That causes division by zero; simplify first.\nExample 3 - Application: It proves derivative formulas instead of memorising them.\nVisual Understanding: Secant slope becomes tangent slope.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "First principles means slope from scratch.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The definition is the Frechet derivative idea in one dimension.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use first principles to differentiate f(x)=3x+1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 836,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__polynomial-derivatives",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Polynomial derivatives",
    "introduction": "Polynomial derivatives use the power rule term by term. They are among the most reliable derivative calculations.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate each term separately.\n\nHow It Works: Bring the power down and reduce the exponent by one.\n\nWhy It Works: The power rule follows from expanding (x+h)^n in first principles.\n\nWhere We Use It: Curve slopes, optimization, and approximations use polynomial derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting that constant derivative is zero.\n\nCorrect idea: A constant does not change, so its rate is zero.\n\nRule: d/dx x^n = n x^(n-1) for positive integers n.\n\nWorked Example: d/dx(4x^3-2x+7)=12x^2-2.\n\nVerification: Differentiate term by term and check constant vanishes.\n\n**Key Point:** d/dx x^n = n x^(n-1) for positive integers n.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d/dx(4x^3-2x+7)=12x^2-2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A constant does not change, so its rate is zero.\nExample 3 - Application: Curve slopes, optimization, and approximations use polynomial derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Each term has its own slope contribution.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Power drops down, exponent steps down.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Polynomial differentiation is linear and preserves polynomial structure.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate 5x^4 - 3x^2 + 9.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 837,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__trig-derivatives",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Trig derivatives",
    "introduction": "Trigonometric derivatives give rates of sine, cosine, tangent, and related functions. They are built from standard trig limits.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use standard derivative formulas and chain rule when needed.\n\nHow It Works: Match the function exactly before applying the formula.\n\nWhy It Works: The sine and cosine derivative cycle comes from unit-circle motion.\n\nWhere We Use It: Waves, rotation, and oscillation models use trig derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing derivative of cos x as sin x.\n\nCorrect idea: d/dx cos x = -sin x; the negative sign matters.\n\nFormulas: d/dx sin x=cos x, d/dx cos x=-sin x, d/dx tan x=sec^2 x.\n\nWorked Example: d/dx sin(2x)=2cos(2x).\n\nVerification: Chain rule gives factor 2.\n\n**Key Point:** d/dx sin x=cos x, d/dx cos x=-sin x, d/dx tan x=sec^2 x.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d/dx sin(2x)=2cos(2x).\nExample 2 - Contrast: d/dx cos x = -sin x; the negative sign matters.\nExample 3 - Application: Waves, rotation, and oscillation models use trig derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Watch sine and cosine slopes cycle.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Trig slopes follow wave motion.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Trig derivatives are periodic and connected through phase shifts.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate y=cos(3x)-tan x.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 838,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__sum-rule",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Sum rule",
    "introduction": "Sum rule explains a specific idea inside Limits and Derivatives. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Calculus accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate each addend separately. How It Works: Add the derivative results. Why It Works: Limits distribute over sums when the needed limits exist. Where We Use It: Polynomials and combined rate models use the sum rule. Common mistake: Differentiating only the first term. Correct idea: Every term contributes to the total rate of change. Rule: (f+g)' = f' + g'. Worked Example: If y=x^2+sin x, then y'=2x+cos x. Verification: Differentiate each part separately. **Key Point:** (f+g)' = f' + g'. Worked Example: For Sum rule, use a simple function such as f(x)=x^2 or f(x)=2x+1. Apply the definition step by step, then compare the answer with the graph. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Sum rule but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a number line, curve, tangent, area strip, or limit picture so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Sum rule becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Sum rule, use a simple function such as f(x)=x^2 or f(x)=2x+1. Apply the definition step by step, then compare the answer with the graph.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Sum rule with speed changes, filling water, area under a graph, or motion along a road.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a number line, curve, tangent, area strip, or limit picture and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Sum rule studies change, closeness, or accumulated value. A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Sum rule connects local change, limiting behavior, approximation, or accumulation. Advanced analysis makes this precise using functions, convergence, continuity, differentiability, or integration conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Sum rule. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 839,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__product-rule",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Product rule",
    "introduction": "Product rule explains a specific idea inside Limits and Derivatives. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Calculus accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate first times second, plus first times derivative of second. How It Works: Do not simply multiply the derivatives. Why It Works: A small change in a product comes from change in each factor separately. Where We Use It: Area with changing sides and physics formulas use product rule. Common mistake: Using (fg)'=f'g'. Correct idea: That is false; use f'g + fg'. Rule: (fg)' = f'g + fg'. Worked Example: For y=x^2 sin x, y'=2x sin x + x^2 cos x. Verification: Each term changes one factor at a time. **Key Point:** (fg)' = f'g + fg'. Worked Example: For Product rule, use a simple function such as f(x)=x^2 or f(x)=2x+1. Apply the definition step by step, then compare the answer with the graph. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Product rule but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a number line, curve, tangent, area strip, or limit picture so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Product rule becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For y=x^2 sin x, y'=2x sin x + x^2 cos x.\nExample 2 - Contrast: That is false; use f'g + fg'.\nExample 3 - Application: Area with changing sides and physics formulas use product rule.\nVisual Understanding: Two moving parts create two terms.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Product rule studies change, closeness, or accumulated value. A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Product rule connects local change, limiting behavior, approximation, or accumulation. Advanced analysis makes this precise using functions, convergence, continuity, differentiability, or integration conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Product rule. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 840,
    "id": "class-11__calculus__limits-and-derivatives__quotient-rule",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Limits and Derivatives",
    "subtopic": "Quotient rule",
    "introduction": "The quotient rule differentiates one function divided by another. The denominator must not be zero.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use denominator times derivative of numerator minus numerator times derivative of denominator, over denominator squared.\n\nHow It Works: Keep the order in the numerator carefully.\n\nWhy It Works: It follows from product rule applied to f times 1/g.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rates, rational functions, and slopes use quotient rule.\n\nCommon mistake: Reversing the subtraction order.\n\nCorrect idea: Use low d-high minus high d-low only if you remember which is numerator and denominator.\n\nRule: (f/g)' = (gf' - fg')/g^2, where g != 0.\n\nWorked Example: For y=x/(x+1), y'=[(x+1)1 - x(1)]/(x+1)^2 = 1/(x+1)^2.\n\nVerification: Denominator cannot be -1.\n\n**Key Point:** (f/g)' = (gf' - fg')/g^2, where g != 0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For y=x/(x+1), y'=[(x+1)1 - x(1)]/(x+1)^2 = 1/(x+1)^2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use low d-high minus high d-low only if you remember which is numerator and denominator.\nExample 3 - Application: Rates, rational functions, and slopes use quotient rule.\nVisual Understanding: Mark numerator and denominator before applying.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Quotient rule protects the changing bottom.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It can be derived from product and chain rules using g^-1.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate (x^2+1)/(x-3).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 841,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__statements",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Statements",
    "introduction": "A mathematical statement is a sentence that is definitely true or false. It is the basic unit of logic.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Ask whether the sentence has a clear truth value.\n\nHow It Works: Questions, commands, and vague sentences are not mathematical statements.\n\nWhy It Works: Logic needs clear truth values before reasoning can begin.\n\nWhere We Use It: Proofs, definitions, and computer conditions use statements.\n\nCommon mistake: Calling a question a statement.\n\nCorrect idea: A question has no truth value until answered as a claim.\n\nDefinition: A statement is a declarative sentence that is true or false, not both.\n\nWorked Example: '7 is prime' is a statement; 'Is 7 prime?' is not.\n\nVerification: The first sentence is true.\n\n**Key Point:** A statement is a declarative sentence that is true or false, not both.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: '7 is prime' is a statement; 'Is 7 prime?' is not.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A question has no truth value until answered as a claim.\nExample 3 - Application: Proofs, definitions, and computer conditions use statements.\nVisual Understanding: Truth value is the test.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Statement means truth-checkable sentence.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Statements are propositions in formal logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Decide whether 'x+2=5' is a statement before x is specified.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 842,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__negation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Negation",
    "introduction": "Negation changes a statement to its opposite truth value. It says 'not' the original claim.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write the exact denial of the statement.\n\nHow It Works: If the original is true, the negation is false, and reverse.\n\nWhy It Works: Negation flips truth values while keeping the same situation.\n\nWhere We Use It: Proof by contradiction and error checking use negation.\n\nCommon mistake: Negating 'all' by saying 'none'.\n\nCorrect idea: The negation of 'all' is 'at least one not'.\n\nDefinition: The negation of p is not p.\n\nWorked Example: If p is '5 is even', not p is '5 is not even', which is true.\n\nVerification: Exactly one of p and not p is true.\n\n**Key Point:** The negation of p is not p.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If p is '5 is even', not p is '5 is not even', which is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The negation of 'all' is 'at least one not'.\nExample 3 - Application: Proof by contradiction and error checking use negation.\nVisual Understanding: Turn the claim into its exact denial.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Negation flips true and false.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Negation is a unary logical connective with truth value reversal.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the negation of 'All squares are rectangles'.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 843,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__compound-statements",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Compound statements",
    "introduction": "Compound statements are made by joining simpler statements. Connectives such as and, or, and if-then control truth.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify the smaller statements and the connective.\n\nHow It Works: Use the truth rule for that connective.\n\nWhy It Works: The compound truth depends on the truth values of its parts.\n\nWhere We Use It: Proofs, search filters, and programming conditions use compound statements.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging the whole sentence without checking the connective.\n\nCorrect idea: Break it into parts first.\n\nDefinition: A compound statement combines statements using logical connectives.\n\nWorked Example: 'p and q' is true only when both p and q are true.\n\nVerification: Let p true and q false; p and q is false.\n\n**Key Point:** A compound statement combines statements using logical connectives.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 'p and q' is true only when both p and q are true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Break it into parts first.\nExample 3 - Application: Proofs, search filters, and programming conditions use compound statements.\nVisual Understanding: Use a truth table.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Compound means joined logic parts.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Compound statements are formulas in propositional logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "For p true and q false, find truth of p or q and p and q.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 844,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__and",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "AND",
    "introduction": "AND joins two statements and requires both to be true. It is strict because one false part makes the whole false.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check each part separately.\n\nHow It Works: Mark the AND statement true only if both parts are true.\n\nWhy It Works: AND represents intersection of truth conditions.\n\nWhere We Use It: Rules, filters, and geometry conditions use AND.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking one true part is enough.\n\nCorrect idea: That is OR thinking; AND needs both.\n\nTruth Rule: p AND q is true exactly when p and q are both true.\n\nWorked Example: '2 is even AND 3 is odd' is true.\n\nVerification: Both component statements are true.\n\n**Key Point:** p AND q is true exactly when p and q are both true.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: '2 is even AND 3 is odd' is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: That is OR thinking; AND needs both.\nExample 3 - Application: Rules, filters, and geometry conditions use AND.\nVisual Understanding: Overlap region represents AND.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "AND means both conditions pass.",
    "advancedExplanation": "AND is conjunction and corresponds to intersection in set logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is '6 is even AND 6 is prime' true or false? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 845,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__or",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "OR",
    "introduction": "OR joins statements where at least one part may be true. In mathematics, OR usually includes the case where both are true.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check whether p is true, q is true, or both.\n\nHow It Works: The OR statement is false only when both parts are false.\n\nWhy It Works: Inclusive OR covers union of truth conditions.\n\nWhere We Use It: Eligibility rules, events, and cases use OR.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking mathematical OR always excludes both.\n\nCorrect idea: Unless stated 'either but not both', OR includes both.\n\nTruth Rule: p OR q is true when at least one of p, q is true.\n\nWorked Example: '4 is even OR 4 is prime' is true because 4 is even.\n\nVerification: At least one part is true.\n\n**Key Point:** p OR q is true when at least one of p, q is true.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: '4 is even OR 4 is prime' is true because 4 is even.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Unless stated 'either but not both', OR includes both.\nExample 3 - Application: Eligibility rules, events, and cases use OR.\nVisual Understanding: Union region represents OR.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "OR means one or both.",
    "advancedExplanation": "OR is disjunction and corresponds to union in set logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is '9 is even OR 9 is a square' true? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 846,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__implication",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Implication",
    "introduction": "An implication has the form if p, then q. It claims q follows whenever p is true.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify hypothesis p and conclusion q.\n\nHow It Works: The implication is false only when p is true and q is false.\n\nWhy It Works: It is a rule about cases where the hypothesis happens.\n\nWhere We Use It: Theorems and conditional instructions use implications.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking p implies q also means q implies p.\n\nCorrect idea: The reverse statement is the converse and may be false.\n\nTruth Rule: p -> q is false only when p is true and q is false.\n\nWorked Example: If a number is divisible by 4, then it is even is true.\n\nVerification: Every multiple of 4 has factor 2.\n\n**Key Point:** p -> q is false only when p is true and q is false.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a number is divisible by 4, then it is even is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The reverse statement is the converse and may be false.\nExample 3 - Application: Theorems and conditional instructions use implications.\nVisual Understanding: Arrow points from condition to result.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Implication means a promise from p to q.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Material implication differs from everyday cause-and-effect language.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give the converse of 'If a shape is a square, then it is a rectangle'. Is it true?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 847,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__converse",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Converse",
    "introduction": "The converse of an implication swaps hypothesis and conclusion. It may have a different truth value.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with if p then q.\n\nHow It Works: Write if q then p.\n\nWhy It Works: Swapping changes the logical direction of the claim.\n\nWhere We Use It: Geometry converses and theorem checking use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming a true statement has a true converse.\n\nCorrect idea: A true implication can have a false converse.\n\nDefinition: The converse of p -> q is q -> p.\n\nWorked Example: Original: If square, then rectangle. Converse: If rectangle, then square, which is false.\n\nVerification: A non-square rectangle disproves the converse.\n\n**Key Point:** The converse of p -> q is q -> p.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Original: If square, then rectangle. Converse: If rectangle, then square, which is false.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A true implication can have a false converse.\nExample 3 - Application: Geometry converses and theorem checking use this idea.\nVisual Understanding: Turn the arrow around.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Converse reverses the arrow.",
    "advancedExplanation": "A theorem and its converse are logically independent unless both are proved.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the converse of 'If angles are vertical, then they are equal'.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 848,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__inverse",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Inverse",
    "introduction": "The inverse of an implication negates both hypothesis and conclusion. It keeps the order but changes each part to 'not'.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with if p then q.\n\nHow It Works: Write if not p then not q.\n\nWhy It Works: Negating both parts does not preserve truth in general.\n\nWhere We Use It: Logic comparisons and proof analysis use inverse statements.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking inverse is always equal to the original implication.\n\nCorrect idea: The inverse is logically equivalent to the converse, not to the original.\n\nDefinition: The inverse of p -> q is not p -> not q.\n\nWorked Example: If square then rectangle. Inverse: If not square then not rectangle, false because many rectangles are not squares.\n\nVerification: A non-square rectangle is a counterexample.\n\n**Key Point:** The inverse of p -> q is not p -> not q.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If square then rectangle. Inverse: If not square then not rectangle, false because many rectangles are not squares.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The inverse is logically equivalent to the converse, not to the original.\nExample 3 - Application: Logic comparisons and proof analysis use inverse statements.\nVisual Understanding: Put NOT on both sides.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Inverse keeps direction but negates both parts.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Inverse and converse share truth value because both are contrapositives of each other.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the inverse of 'If n is divisible by 6, then n is divisible by 3'.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 849,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__contrapositive",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Contrapositive",
    "introduction": "The contrapositive reverses and negates an implication. It is logically equivalent to the original statement.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with if p then q.\n\nHow It Works: Write if not q then not p.\n\nWhy It Works: The only false case is preserved by reversing and negating.\n\nWhere We Use It: Proofs often use contrapositive when direct proof is hard.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing contrapositive with converse.\n\nCorrect idea: Converse only reverses; contrapositive reverses and negates.\n\nTheorem: p -> q is logically equivalent to not q -> not p.\n\nWorked Example: If square then rectangle. Contrapositive: If not rectangle, then not square, true.\n\nVerification: Any square must be a rectangle.\n\n**Key Point:** p -> q is logically equivalent to not q -> not p.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If square then rectangle. Contrapositive: If not rectangle, then not square, true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Converse only reverses; contrapositive reverses and negates.\nExample 3 - Application: Proofs often use contrapositive when direct proof is hard.\nVisual Understanding: Reverse the arrow and negate both parts.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Contrapositive is the reliable reverse-not form.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Contrapositive equivalence is a tautology in classical logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the contrapositive of 'If a number is odd, then its square is odd'.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 850,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__quantifiers-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Quantifiers introduction",
    "introduction": "Quantifiers tell how many objects a statement talks about. Common ones are 'for all' and 'there exists'.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify the set being discussed.\n\nHow It Works: Decide whether the claim is universal or existential.\n\nWhy It Works: Truth depends on checking all elements or finding at least one example.\n\nWhere We Use It: Definitions, proofs, and counterexamples use quantifiers.\n\nCommon mistake: Disproving 'for all' by many examples instead of one counterexample.\n\nCorrect idea: One counterexample is enough to disprove a universal claim.\n\nDefinitions: 'For all' means every element; 'there exists' means at least one element.\n\nWorked Example: 'For all even numbers n, n is divisible by 2' is true.\n\nVerification: Each even number has form 2k.\n\n**Key Point:** 'For all' means every element; 'there exists' means at least one element.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 'For all even numbers n, n is divisible by 2' is true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: One counterexample is enough to disprove a universal claim.\nExample 3 - Application: Definitions, proofs, and counterexamples use quantifiers.\nVisual Understanding: Universal means every point in the set.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Quantifiers say all or at least one.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Quantifiers belong to predicate logic and control variable scope.",
    "practicePrompt": "Negate: 'For all students, x is true'.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 851,
    "id": "class-11__mathematical-reasoning__logic__validate-statements",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Mathematical Reasoning",
    "topic": "Logic",
    "subtopic": "Validate statements",
    "introduction": "Validating statements means deciding whether a mathematical claim is true or false with reasoning. It needs proof or counterexample.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Read the quantifiers, conditions, and conclusion.\n\nHow It Works: Prove true claims generally; disprove false claims with a counterexample.\n\nWhy It Works: Examples can support a pattern but do not prove a universal statement unless all cases are covered.\n\nWhere We Use It: Proof writing, exams, and computer logic use validation.\n\nCommon mistake: Checking only one example and calling a universal statement true.\n\nCorrect idea: One example is not proof; one counterexample can disprove.\n\nMethod: A statement is validated by proof when true or counterexample when false.\n\nWorked Example: 'All prime numbers are odd' is false because 2 is prime and even.\n\nVerification: 2 satisfies prime but breaks odd.\n\n**Key Point:** A statement is validated by proof when true or counterexample when false.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 'All prime numbers are odd' is false because 2 is prime and even.\nExample 2 - Contrast: One example is not proof; one counterexample can disprove.\nExample 3 - Application: Proof writing, exams, and computer logic use validation.\nVisual Understanding: Search for a counterexample before accepting 'all'.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Truth needs proof; false needs counterexample.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Validation separates semantic truth from syntactic form in logic.",
    "practicePrompt": "Decide whether 'All rectangles are squares' is true, and justify.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 852,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__range",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Range",
    "introduction": "Range explains a specific idea inside Statistics. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Statistics and Probability accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Range is a precise idea in Statistics.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the given objects, choose the correct rule or definition, and solve step by step.\n\nWhy It Works: The method works because the rule matches the structure of the problem, not just the topic name.\n\nWhere We Use It: We use it in school marks, cricket scores, weather data, survey answers, or game results.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying the rule without checking conditions, units, or meaning.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the condition first, then solve and verify.\n\nWorked Example: For Range, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6. Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.\n\nVerification: Test the answer with a small example or reverse step.\n\n**Key Point:** Range needs the correct rule, condition, and check.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 12, 18, 25, 30, range = 30 - 12 = 18.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Here range means maximum minus minimum in data.\nExample 3 - Application: Scores, temperatures, and heights can use range.\nVisual Understanding: Distance from smallest to largest.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Range is about reading information from data. Start with a small table, compute carefully, then say what the number means.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Range connects sample information with uncertainty, modelling, and inference. More advanced statistics studies assumptions, variability, bias, and how conclusions change when the data model changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the range of 4, 9, 10, 10, 22. How does 100 change it?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 853,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__mean-deviation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Mean deviation",
    "introduction": "Mean deviation measures average distance from a central value, often mean or median. It shows spread without signs cancelling.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find the centre, find absolute deviations, then average them.\n\nHow It Works: Use absolute values so distances are non-negative.\n\nWhy It Works: Positive and negative deviations would cancel, hiding spread.\n\nWhere We Use It: Comparing consistency of marks or production quality uses mean deviation.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding signed deviations around the mean and getting zero.\n\nCorrect idea: Use absolute deviations, not signed deviations.\n\nFormula: Mean deviation about mean = sum |xi - mean| / n for ungrouped data.\n\nWorked Example: For 2,4,6, mean=4; deviations are 2,0,2; mean deviation=4/3.\n\nVerification: Distances are all non-negative.\n\n**Key Point:** Mean deviation about mean = sum |xi - mean| / n for ungrouped data.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 2,4,6, mean=4; deviations are 2,0,2; mean deviation=4/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use absolute deviations, not signed deviations.\nExample 3 - Application: Comparing consistency of marks or production quality uses mean deviation.\nVisual Understanding: Average the distances, not the signs.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Mean deviation is average distance from centre.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Mean deviation uses L1 distance and is less sensitive to outliers than variance in some contexts.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find mean deviation about mean for 3, 3, 9.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 854,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__variance",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Variance",
    "introduction": "Variance measures average squared distance from the mean. Squaring makes all deviations non-negative and gives more weight to large deviations.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find the mean, square each deviation, then average the squares.\n\nHow It Works: Use population or sample formula according to the problem.\n\nWhy It Works: Squaring prevents cancellation and emphasizes larger spread.\n\nWhere We Use It: Statistics, risk, and data comparison use variance.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting to square deviations.\n\nCorrect idea: Without squaring, deviations from the mean sum to zero.\n\nFormula: Population variance = sum(xi - mean)^2 / n.\n\nWorked Example: For 2,4,6, mean=4; squared deviations are 4,0,4; variance=8/3.\n\nVerification: Variance is non-negative.\n\n**Key Point:** Population variance = sum(xi - mean)^2 / n.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For 2,4,6, mean=4; squared deviations are 4,0,4; variance=8/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Without squaring, deviations from the mean sum to zero.\nExample 3 - Application: Statistics, risk, and data comparison use variance.\nVisual Understanding: Square the distances from mean.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Variance is spread measured in squared units.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Variance is the second central moment of a distribution.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the variance of 1, 3, 5 using population formula.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 855,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__standard-deviation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Standard deviation",
    "introduction": "Standard deviation explains a specific idea inside Statistics. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Statistics and Probability accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Standard deviation is a precise idea in Statistics.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the given objects, choose the correct rule or definition, and solve step by step.\n\nWhy It Works: The method works because the rule matches the structure of the problem, not just the topic name.\n\nWhere We Use It: We use it in school marks, cricket scores, weather data, survey answers, or game results.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying the rule without checking conditions, units, or meaning.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the condition first, then solve and verify.\n\nWorked Example: For Standard deviation, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6. Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.\n\nVerification: Test the answer with a small example or reverse step.\n\n**Key Point:** Standard deviation needs the correct rule, condition, and check.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If variance is 25 square marks, standard deviation is 5 marks.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Standard deviation is sqrt(variance), so it has original units.\nExample 3 - Application: Marks, finance, science, and quality control use standard deviation.\nVisual Understanding: Undo variance's square.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Standard deviation is about reading information from data. Start with a small table, compute carefully, then say what the number means.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Standard deviation connects sample information with uncertainty, modelling, and inference. More advanced statistics studies assumptions, variability, bias, and how conclusions change when the data model changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Standard deviation. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 856,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__coefficient-of-variation",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Coefficient of variation",
    "introduction": "Coefficient of variation compares spread relative to the mean. It helps compare variability of data sets with different scales.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Divide standard deviation by mean and multiply by 100.\n\nHow It Works: Use it only when the mean is positive and meaningful.\n\nWhy It Works: Relative spread is fairer than raw spread when averages differ.\n\nWhere We Use It: Comparing consistency of teams, machines, or investments uses CV.\n\nCommon mistake: Choosing the smaller standard deviation without considering the mean.\n\nCorrect idea: A larger mean can make the same spread relatively smaller.\n\nFormula: CV = standard deviation / mean x 100%.\n\nWorked Example: Data set A has mean 50, SD 5, so CV=10%. Data set B has mean 20, SD 4, so CV=20%.\n\nVerification: A is more consistent by CV.\n\n**Key Point:** CV = standard deviation / mean x 100%.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Data set A has mean 50, SD 5, so CV=10%. Data set B has mean 20, SD 4, so CV=20%.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A larger mean can make the same spread relatively smaller.\nExample 3 - Application: Comparing consistency of teams, machines, or investments uses CV.\nVisual Understanding: Compare spread relative to average.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "CV is spread as a percent of average.",
    "advancedExplanation": "CV is dimensionless but unstable when the mean is near zero.",
    "practicePrompt": "Which is more consistent: mean 80 SD 8, or mean 30 SD 6? Use CV.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 857,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__statistics__grouped-variance",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Statistics",
    "subtopic": "Grouped variance",
    "introduction": "Grouped variance summarises data using organised values or class intervals. It helps compare large data sets without reading every entry separately. It is useful for marks, surveys, sports data, and measurements.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Grouped variance = sum(f*(x-mean)^2)/sum(f).\n\nHow It Works: Make a clear frequency table. Compute class marks or cumulative frequencies if needed. Substitute carefully.\n\nWhy It Works: Frequencies show how many times each value or interval contributes to the summary.\n\nWhere We Use It: School results, population tables, and grouped measurements use these methods.\n\nCommon mistake: Using class limits instead of class marks when a class mark is required.\n\nCorrect idea: For grouped mean and variance, use the midpoint of each class interval.\n\nFormula: Grouped variance = sum(f*(x-mean)^2)/sum(f).\n\nWorked Example: Use class marks x and frequencies f, then divide by total frequency.\n\nVerification: Check that the answer lies in a reasonable range for the data.\n\nMemory Trick: Frequency means repeated weight.\n\n**Key Point:** Grouped variance uses frequencies to summarise grouped data accurately.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Marks grouped as 0-10, 10-20, and 20-30 form a frequency table.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A histogram shows frequency bars; an ogive shows cumulative frequency.\nExample 3 - Application: A teacher can compare two class performances using mean and variation.\nVisual Understanding: Use a table with columns for class mark, frequency, and the needed product.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Grouped statistics compress many values into a useful summary.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Grouped formulas approximate raw-data summaries by representing each interval with its class mark.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use a small grouped table to calculate or draw Grouped variance.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 858,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__sample-space",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Sample space",
    "introduction": "A sample space is the set of all possible outcomes of a random experiment. It is the starting point for probability.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: List every possible outcome once.\n\nHow It Works: Use braces, tables, or tree diagrams depending on the experiment.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability of events is measured relative to all possible outcomes in the sample space.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coins, dice, cards, and surveys use sample spaces.\n\nCommon mistake: Leaving out an outcome.\n\nCorrect idea: A missing outcome changes every probability calculation.\n\nDefinition: Sample space S is the set of all possible outcomes.\n\nWorked Example: For one die, S={1,2,3,4,5,6}.\n\nVerification: The six outcomes cover every die result.\n\n**Key Point:** Sample space S is the set of all possible outcomes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For one die, S={1,2,3,4,5,6}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A missing outcome changes every probability calculation.\nExample 3 - Application: Coins, dice, cards, and surveys use sample spaces.\nVisual Understanding: List all possible results first.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Sample space is the full outcome list.",
    "advancedExplanation": "In probability theory, events are subsets of the sample space.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the sample space for tossing two coins.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 859,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__events",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Events",
    "introduction": "An event is a set of outcomes from the sample space. It is something whose probability can be measured.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose outcomes that satisfy the event description.\n\nHow It Works: Write them as a subset of the sample space.\n\nWhy It Works: Because events are sets, set operations can combine them.\n\nWhere We Use It: Game wins, even numbers, and rainy days can be events.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing an event outside the sample space.\n\nCorrect idea: Every event outcome must belong to S.\n\nDefinition: An event is any subset of the sample space.\n\nWorked Example: For a die, event E='even number' is {2,4,6}.\n\nVerification: E is contained in S={1,2,3,4,5,6}.\n\n**Key Point:** An event is any subset of the sample space.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a die, event E='even number' is {2,4,6}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Every event outcome must belong to S.\nExample 3 - Application: Game wins, even numbers, and rainy days can be events.\nVisual Understanding: Circle the outcomes that match the condition.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "An event is a selected part of S.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Events form a set algebra or sigma-algebra in formal probability.",
    "practicePrompt": "For one die, write the event of getting a number greater than 4.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 860,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__event-algebra",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Event algebra",
    "introduction": "Event algebra uses set operations on events. It lets us describe 'or', 'and', and 'not' in probability.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use union for or, intersection for and, complement for not.\n\nHow It Works: Translate the words in the question into set operations.\n\nWhy It Works: Events are sets, so set laws apply to probability events.\n\nWhere We Use It: Combined conditions in games, surveys, and risk use event algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing union and intersection.\n\nCorrect idea: 'A or B' is union; 'A and B' is intersection.\n\nRules: A union B means A or B; A intersection B means A and B; A' means not A.\n\nWorked Example: For die events A={2,4,6}, B={4,5,6}, A intersection B={4,6}.\n\nVerification: Only 4 and 6 are in both sets.\n\n**Key Point:** A union B means A or B; A intersection B means A and B; A' means not A.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For die events A={2,4,6}, B={4,5,6}, A intersection B={4,6}.\nExample 2 - Contrast: 'A or B' is union; 'A and B' is intersection.\nExample 3 - Application: Combined conditions in games, surveys, and risk use event algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use Venn regions for event operations.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Event algebra is set language for chance.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Probability spaces use event algebras closed under complement and countable unions.",
    "practicePrompt": "For A={1,2,3}, B={3,4}, find A union B and A intersection B.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 861,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__mutually-exclusive",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Mutually exclusive",
    "introduction": "Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together. Their intersection is empty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check whether the events share any outcome.\n\nHow It Works: If they share none, they are mutually exclusive.\n\nWhy It Works: No overlap means both events cannot occur in the same trial.\n\nWhere We Use It: Single die results and one-choice survey answers use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking opposite events are the only mutually exclusive events.\n\nCorrect idea: Many event pairs can be mutually exclusive without being complements.\n\nDefinition: A and B are mutually exclusive if A intersection B is empty.\n\nWorked Example: On one die, getting 2 and getting 5 are mutually exclusive.\n\nVerification: No die roll can be both 2 and 5.\n\n**Key Point:** A and B are mutually exclusive if A intersection B is empty.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: On one die, getting 2 and getting 5 are mutually exclusive.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Many event pairs can be mutually exclusive without being complements.\nExample 3 - Application: Single die results and one-choice survey answers use this idea.\nVisual Understanding: Check overlap region.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Mutually exclusive means no overlap.",
    "advancedExplanation": "For mutually exclusive events, P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B).",
    "practicePrompt": "Are 'even number' and 'number greater than 4' mutually exclusive on a die? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 862,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__exhaustive-events",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Exhaustive events",
    "introduction": "Exhaustive events cover the whole sample space. At least one of them must happen.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Take the union of the events.\n\nHow It Works: If the union equals S, the events are exhaustive.\n\nWhy It Works: Covering S means no possible outcome is left outside all events.\n\nWhere We Use It: Casework and probability partitions use exhaustive events.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking exhaustive events must be mutually exclusive.\n\nCorrect idea: They may overlap and still cover the sample space.\n\nDefinition: Events are exhaustive if their union is the sample space.\n\nWorked Example: For a die, A={1,2,3}, B={3,4,5,6}; A union B=S, so they are exhaustive.\n\nVerification: Every die outcome is covered.\n\n**Key Point:** Events are exhaustive if their union is the sample space.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a die, A={1,2,3}, B={3,4,5,6}; A union B=S, so they are exhaustive.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They may overlap and still cover the sample space.\nExample 3 - Application: Casework and probability partitions use exhaustive events.\nVisual Understanding: Cover the whole rectangle of S.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Exhaustive means nothing is missed.",
    "advancedExplanation": "A partition is a stronger case: events are exhaustive and pairwise disjoint.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give two exhaustive events for one coin toss.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 863,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__probability-axioms-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Probability axioms introduction",
    "introduction": "Probability axioms are basic rules every probability model must follow. They keep chance calculations consistent.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check non-negativity, total probability, and additivity for disjoint events.\n\nHow It Works: Use the axioms before applying formulas.\n\nWhy It Works: These rules define probability mathematically beyond simple counting.\n\nWhere We Use It: Advanced probability, statistics, and modelling use axioms.\n\nCommon mistake: Allowing a probability greater than 1.\n\nCorrect idea: Probabilities must lie between 0 and 1.\n\nAxioms: P(E)>=0, P(S)=1, and disjoint events add: P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B).\n\nWorked Example: If P(A)=0.3 and P(B)=0.4 for disjoint events, P(A union B)=0.7.\n\nVerification: 0.7 is between 0 and 1.\n\n**Key Point:** P(E)>=0, P(S)=1, and disjoint events add: P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If P(A)=0.3 and P(B)=0.4 for disjoint events, P(A union B)=0.7.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Probabilities must lie between 0 and 1.\nExample 3 - Application: Advanced probability, statistics, and modelling use axioms.\nVisual Understanding: All chance lives between impossible and certain.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Axioms are the rules of chance numbers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Kolmogorov axioms define probability measures on event spaces.",
    "practicePrompt": "Can a probability be -0.2 or 1.3? Which axiom rejects it?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 864,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__addition-rule",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Addition rule",
    "introduction": "The addition rule finds the probability that A or B happens. It must avoid double-counting overlap.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Add P(A) and P(B), then subtract P(A intersection B).\n\nHow It Works: If events are mutually exclusive, the overlap term is zero.\n\nWhy It Works: Outcomes in both events are counted twice unless the intersection is subtracted.\n\nWhere We Use It: Cards, dice, surveys, and risk events use this rule.\n\nCommon mistake: Using P(A)+P(B) even when events overlap.\n\nCorrect idea: Subtract the overlap once.\n\nFormula: P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A intersection B).\n\nWorked Example: If P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4, overlap=0.1, then union=0.8.\n\nVerification: A Venn diagram shows overlap counted once.\n\n**Key Point:** P(A union B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A intersection B).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4, overlap=0.1, then union=0.8.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Subtract the overlap once.\nExample 3 - Application: Cards, dice, surveys, and risk events use this rule.\nVisual Understanding: Shade both events and subtract overlap.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Add both circles, remove double count.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The addition rule follows from finite additivity of disjoint pieces.",
    "practicePrompt": "For a die, find P(even or greater than 4).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 865,
    "id": "class-11__statistics-and-probability__probability__conditional-probability-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 11",
    "chapter": "Statistics and Probability",
    "topic": "Probability",
    "subtopic": "Conditional probability introduction",
    "introduction": "Conditional probability measures chance after some information is known. It changes the sample space to the condition.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Restrict attention to outcomes where B happened.\n\nHow It Works: Then find the fraction of those outcomes where A also happened.\n\nWhy It Works: Knowing B removes outcomes outside B from consideration.\n\nWhere We Use It: Cards, medical tests, and data filters use conditional probability.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the original sample space after a condition is given.\n\nCorrect idea: After 'given B', count only inside B.\n\nFormula: P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0.\n\nWorked Example: For a die, P(even | greater than 3). Outcomes greater than 3 are {4,5,6}; even ones are {4,6}; probability=2/3.\n\nVerification: P(B)>0 because the condition must be possible.\n\n**Key Point:** P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a die, P(even | greater than 3). Outcomes greater than 3 are {4,5,6}; even ones are {4,6}; probability=2/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: After 'given B', count only inside B.\nExample 3 - Application: Cards, medical tests, and data filters use conditional probability.\nVisual Understanding: Look only inside the condition first.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Given means shrink the sample space.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Conditional probability is the basis for independence and Bayes' theorem.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find P(number greater than 4 | even) for one die.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
