    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cramer's rule is easiest when you attach it to one small example. Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cramer's rule is about structure preserved by maps. Advanced use studies invariants, universal properties, or classification under isomorphism.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Cramer's rule, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 901,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__continuity-at-point",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Continuity at point",
    "introduction": "Continuity at a point means the function value matches the limiting value there. The graph has no break at that exact point.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check left limit, right limit, and function value.\n\nHow It Works: All three must exist and be equal.\n\nWhy It Works: A point is continuous only when nearby values approach the actual value.\n\nWhere We Use It: Graphs, motion, and model validity use point continuity.\n\nCommon mistake: Checking only that f(a) exists.\n\nCorrect idea: A defined value alone is not enough; the limit must equal it.\n\nDefinition: f is continuous at x=a if lim x->a f(x)=f(a).\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2 at a=2, limit is 4 and f(2)=4, so continuous.\n\nVerification: Both sides approach 4.\n\n**Key Point:** f is continuous at x=a if lim x->a f(x)=f(a).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2 at a=2, limit is 4 and f(2)=4, so continuous.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A defined value alone is not enough; the limit must equal it.\nExample 3 - Application: Graphs, motion, and model validity use point continuity.\nVisual Understanding: Zoom near the point and compare the hole or value.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "No jump between approach and value.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Point continuity is a local property and can fail by removable, jump, or infinite discontinuity.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1) continuous at x=1 as written? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 902,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__continuity-on-interval",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Continuity on interval",
    "introduction": "Continuity on an interval means the function is continuous at every point of that interval. Endpoints use one-sided checks.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check each point inside the interval and endpoints if included.\n\nHow It Works: Use known continuity rules or inspect possible break points.\n\nWhy It Works: An interval has many points, so every point must pass the continuity condition.\n\nWhere We Use It: Curves over time, distance, and temperature models use interval continuity.\n\nCommon mistake: Checking only a few sample points.\n\nCorrect idea: One unchecked break can make the function discontinuous on the interval.\n\nDefinition: A function is continuous on an interval if it is continuous at every point of that interval.\n\nWorked Example: f(x)=x^2 is continuous on [0,3].\n\nVerification: Polynomials are continuous everywhere, so every point in [0,3] passes.\n\n**Key Point:** A function is continuous on an interval if it is continuous at every point of that interval.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(x)=x^2 is continuous on [0,3].\nExample 2 - Contrast: One unchecked break can make the function discontinuous on the interval.\nExample 3 - Application: Curves over time, distance, and temperature models use interval continuity.\nVisual Understanding: Trace the whole interval, not one point.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Continuous interval means no break anywhere in the stretch.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Continuity on compact intervals supports the extreme value and intermediate value theorems.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is 1/x continuous on [1,4]? What about [-1,1]?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 903,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__algebra-continuous-functions",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Algebra continuous functions",
    "introduction": "Algebra of continuous functions tells how sums, products, quotients, and composites behave. It helps prove continuity quickly.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with known continuous functions.\n\nHow It Works: Apply allowed operations, checking denominator restrictions for quotients.\n\nWhy It Works: Limits obey algebraic laws, so continuity is preserved under these operations when defined.\n\nWhere We Use It: Polynomial, rational, and composite functions use this shortcut.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting the denominator condition in quotients.\n\nCorrect idea: A quotient is continuous only where the denominator is not zero.\n\nTheorem: If f and g are continuous at a, then f+g, f-g, and fg are continuous at a; f/g is continuous if g(a)!=0.\n\nWorked Example: (x^2+1)/(x-3) is continuous at x=2 but not at x=3.\n\nVerification: At x=3 the denominator is zero.\n\n**Key Point:** If f and g are continuous at a, then f+g, f-g, and fg are continuous at a; f/g is continuous if g(a)!=0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (x^2+1)/(x-3) is continuous at x=2 but not at x=3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A quotient is continuous only where the denominator is not zero.\nExample 3 - Application: Polynomial, rational, and composite functions use this shortcut.\nVisual Understanding: Mark forbidden denominator zeros first.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Build continuous functions from safe pieces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This theorem follows from limit laws for algebraic operations.",
    "practicePrompt": "Where is f(x)=(x+1)/(x^2-4) continuous?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 904,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__differentiability",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Differentiability",
    "introduction": "Differentiability means a function has a well-defined derivative at a point. Geometrically, it has one clear tangent slope there.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Compute the derivative limit or use known derivative rules.\n\nHow It Works: Check for corners, cusps, vertical tangents, or discontinuities.\n\nWhy It Works: The derivative is a limit of average rates of change; it must approach one value.\n\nWhere We Use It: Velocity, slope, and optimization use differentiability.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming continuity guarantees differentiability.\n\nCorrect idea: |x| is continuous at 0 but not differentiable there.\n\nDefinition: f is differentiable at a if lim h->0 [f(a+h)-f(a)]/h exists.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2, derivative at 3 is lim h->0 ((3+h)^2-9)/h = 6.\n\nVerification: Derivative formula gives f'(x)=2x, so f'(3)=6.\n\n**Key Point:** f is differentiable at a if lim h->0 [f(a+h)-f(a)]/h exists.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2, derivative at 3 is lim h->0 ((3+h)^2-9)/h = 6.\nExample 2 - Contrast: |x| is continuous at 0 but not differentiable there.\nExample 3 - Application: Velocity, slope, and optimization use differentiability.\nVisual Understanding: A corner has two possible tangent directions.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Differentiability means one smooth slope.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Differentiability implies continuity, but continuity does not imply differentiability.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is |x| not differentiable at 0? Compare left and right slopes.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 905,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__chain-rule",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Chain rule",
    "introduction": "The chain rule differentiates a function inside another function. It handles composite functions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate the outside function, keep the inside, then multiply by derivative of inside.\n\nHow It Works: Identify outer and inner functions before differentiating.\n\nWhy It Works: A small input change first changes the inside, then the outside; rates multiply.\n\nWhere We Use It: Motion, growth models, and nested formulas use the chain rule.\n\nCommon mistake: Differentiating only the outside part.\n\nCorrect idea: The inside derivative must also multiply.\n\nRule: If y=f(g(x)), then dy/dx=f'(g(x))g'(x).\n\nWorked Example: For y=(3x+1)^5, derivative is 5(3x+1)^4 x 3 = 15(3x+1)^4.\n\nVerification: Differentiate by expanding small cases or checking with a CAS later.\n\n**Key Point:** If y=f(g(x)), then dy/dx=f'(g(x))g'(x).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For y=(3x+1)^5, derivative is 5(3x+1)^4 x 3 = 15(3x+1)^4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The inside derivative must also multiply.\nExample 3 - Application: Motion, growth models, and nested formulas use the chain rule.\nVisual Understanding: Peel layers from outside inward.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Outside derivative times inside derivative.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The chain rule is derivative composition for differentiable maps.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate y=(x^2+4)^6 and name inner and outer functions.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 906,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__implicit-differentiation",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Implicit differentiation",
    "introduction": "Implicit differentiation finds derivatives when x and y are mixed in one equation. It treats y as a function of x.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate both sides with respect to x.\n\nHow It Works: Whenever differentiating y, multiply by dy/dx.\n\nWhy It Works: Since y depends on x, the chain rule applies to y terms.\n\nWhere We Use It: Circles, ellipses, and relations not solved for y use this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Differentiating y^2 as only 2y.\n\nCorrect idea: It should be 2y dy/dx because y is a function of x.\n\nMethod: Differentiate both sides and solve for dy/dx.\n\nWorked Example: For x^2+y^2=25, 2x+2y dy/dx=0, so dy/dx=-x/y.\n\nVerification: At (3,4), slope=-3/4.\n\n**Key Point:** Differentiate both sides and solve for dy/dx.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For x^2+y^2=25, 2x+2y dy/dx=0, so dy/dx=-x/y.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It should be 2y dy/dx because y is a function of x.\nExample 3 - Application: Circles, ellipses, and relations not solved for y use this method.\nVisual Understanding: Every y term carries dy/dx when needed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Treat y as moving with x.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Implicit differentiation generalizes to level curves F(x,y)=0 with slope -Fx/Fy when Fy != 0.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find dy/dx for xy=10.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 907,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__logarithmic-differentiation",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Logarithmic differentiation",
    "introduction": "Logarithmic differentiation uses logarithms before differentiating. It is useful for products, quotients, powers, or variable exponents.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Take natural log on both sides, simplify using log laws, then differentiate implicitly.\n\nHow It Works: Finally multiply by y if solving y'/y.\n\nWhy It Works: Log laws turn multiplication into addition and powers into products.\n\nWhere We Use It: Functions like x^x or long products use this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting to multiply back by y.\n\nCorrect idea: After finding y'/y, multiply by y to get y'.\n\nMethod: If y>0, take ln y, differentiate, then solve for y'.\n\nWorked Example: For y=x^x, ln y=x ln x. Then y'/y=ln x+1, so y'=x^x(ln x+1).\n\nVerification: Differentiate x^x numerically for a positive x to check sign.\n\n**Key Point:** If y>0, take ln y, differentiate, then solve for y'.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For y=x^x, ln y=x ln x. Then y'/y=ln x+1, so y'=x^x(ln x+1).\nExample 2 - Contrast: After finding y'/y, multiply by y to get y'.\nExample 3 - Application: Functions like x^x or long products use this method.\nVisual Understanding: Log first, differentiate second.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Logs turn powers into simpler products.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The method depends on domains where logarithms are defined, often handled with ln|y| in broader contexts.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate y=(x^2+1)^x for x>0 using logarithms.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 908,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__inverse-trig-derivatives",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Inverse trig derivatives",
    "introduction": "Inverse trigonometric derivatives give rates for inverse sine, cosine, tangent, and related functions. Domain restrictions matter.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use the standard derivative formula with its valid interval.\n\nHow It Works: Check whether the expression inside needs the chain rule.\n\nWhy It Works: The formulas come from implicit differentiation of trig identities and principal branches.\n\nWhere We Use It: Calculus integrals, motion angles, and geometry rates use them.\n\nCommon mistake: Ignoring domain restrictions like |x|<1 for arcsin derivative.\n\nCorrect idea: The derivative of sin^(-1)x needs -1<x<1.\n\nFormulas: d/dx sin^(-1)x = 1/sqrt(1-x^2); d/dx tan^(-1)x = 1/(1+x^2).\n\nWorked Example: d/dx sin^(-1)(3x)=3/sqrt(1-9x^2).\n\nVerification: Chain rule adds the factor 3.\n\n**Key Point:** d/dx sin^(-1)x = 1/sqrt(1-x^2); d/dx tan^(-1)x = 1/(1+x^2).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d/dx sin^(-1)(3x)=3/sqrt(1-9x^2).\nExample 2 - Contrast: The derivative of sin^(-1)x needs -1<x<1.\nExample 3 - Application: Calculus integrals, motion angles, and geometry rates use them.\nVisual Understanding: Picture angle changing as ratio changes.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Inverse trig rates need branch and domain care.",
    "advancedExplanation": "These derivatives follow by differentiating y=sin^(-1)x as x=sin y.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate tan^(-1)(2x) and state the chain-rule factor.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 909,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__exponential-derivatives",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Exponential derivatives",
    "introduction": "Exponential derivatives measure how exponential functions change. The natural base e has the simplest derivative.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use d/dx e^x = e^x and apply chain rule for e^(g(x)).\n\nHow It Works: For a^x, multiply by ln a.\n\nWhy It Works: The number e is defined so its exponential growth rate equals its value.\n\nWhere We Use It: Growth, decay, finance, and differential equations use exponential derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating d/dx 2^x as 2^x.\n\nCorrect idea: It is 2^x ln 2, not just 2^x.\n\nFormulas: d/dx e^x=e^x; d/dx a^x=a^x ln a for a>0.\n\nWorked Example: d/dx e^(3x)=3e^(3x).\n\nVerification: Chain rule gives inner derivative 3.\n\n**Key Point:** d/dx e^x=e^x; d/dx a^x=a^x ln a for a>0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d/dx e^(3x)=3e^(3x).\nExample 2 - Contrast: It is 2^x ln 2, not just 2^x.\nExample 3 - Application: Growth, decay, finance, and differential equations use exponential derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Exponential slope is proportional to its height.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "e^x keeps its shape under differentiation.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Exponential functions are eigenfunctions of the derivative operator.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate 5^x and e^(x^2).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 910,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__log-derivatives",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Log derivatives",
    "introduction": "Log derivatives describe rates of logarithmic functions. They convert multiplication patterns into simpler derivative forms.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use d/dx ln x = 1/x for x>0, with chain rule for ln g(x).\n\nHow It Works: For ln|g(x)|, derivative is g'(x)/g(x) where g(x)!=0.\n\nWhy It Works: The logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, leading to reciprocal derivative.\n\nWhere We Use It: Growth scales, information, and integration use log derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing derivative of ln(3x) as 1/(3x).\n\nCorrect idea: Chain rule gives 3/(3x)=1/x.\n\nFormula: d/dx ln x = 1/x for x>0; d/dx ln|g(x)| = g'(x)/g(x).\n\nWorked Example: d/dx ln(x^2+1)=2x/(x^2+1).\n\nVerification: Differentiate by chain rule: g'=2x.\n\n**Key Point:** d/dx ln x = 1/x for x>0; d/dx ln|g(x)| = g'(x)/g(x).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d/dx ln(x^2+1)=2x/(x^2+1).\nExample 2 - Contrast: Chain rule gives 3/(3x)=1/x.\nExample 3 - Application: Growth scales, information, and integration use log derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Log slope shrinks as x grows.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Log derivative is inner derivative over inside.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The logarithmic derivative f'/f is important for products and differential equations.",
    "practicePrompt": "Differentiate ln(5x-2) and state where it is valid.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 911,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__parametric-differentiation",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Parametric differentiation",
    "introduction": "Parametric differentiation finds dy/dx when x and y are both given using a parameter. The curve is traced by changing the parameter.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate x and y with respect to the parameter.\n\nHow It Works: Divide dy/dt by dx/dt when dx/dt is not zero.\n\nWhy It Works: Both coordinates change with t, so slope compares their rates of change.\n\nWhere We Use It: Projectile paths, curves, and motion use parametric equations.\n\nCommon mistake: Using dy/dt directly as dy/dx.\n\nCorrect idea: dy/dt is change in y per parameter, not per x.\n\nFormula: dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt), provided dx/dt != 0.\n\nWorked Example: If x=t^2 and y=t^3, dy/dx=(3t^2)/(2t)=3t/2 for t != 0.\n\nVerification: At t=2, slope=3.\n\n**Key Point:** dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt), provided dx/dt != 0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If x=t^2 and y=t^3, dy/dx=(3t^2)/(2t)=3t/2 for t != 0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: dy/dt is change in y per parameter, not per x.\nExample 3 - Application: Projectile paths, curves, and motion use parametric equations.\nVisual Understanding: Track how x and y move together.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Slope compares vertical and horizontal rates.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Parametric differentiation extends to higher derivatives and vector-valued curves.",
    "practicePrompt": "For x=t+1 and y=t^2, find dy/dx at t=3.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 912,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__continuity-and-differentiability__second-derivative",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Continuity and Differentiability",
    "subtopic": "Second derivative",
    "introduction": "The second derivative is the derivative of the first derivative. It measures how the rate of change itself changes.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate once to get f'(x), then differentiate again to get f''(x).\n\nHow It Works: Interpret sign and value according to the context.\n\nWhy It Works: If f' is slope, f'' tells whether slope is increasing or decreasing.\n\nWhere We Use It: Acceleration, curve concavity, and optimization use second derivatives.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking second derivative is the square of the first derivative.\n\nCorrect idea: It means differentiate twice, not square f'.\n\nDefinition: f''(x)=d/dx[f'(x)].\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^3, f'(x)=3x^2 and f''(x)=6x.\n\nVerification: Differentiate 3x^2 again to get 6x.\n\n**Key Point:** f''(x)=d/dx[f'(x)].",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^3, f'(x)=3x^2 and f''(x)=6x.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It means differentiate twice, not square f'.\nExample 3 - Application: Acceleration, curve concavity, and optimization use second derivatives.\nVisual Understanding: Slope of slope shows bending.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Second derivative is rate of the rate.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Second derivatives describe concavity and appear in Taylor expansions and differential equations.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find f''(x) for f(x)=4x^3-x^2+7.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 913,
