    "id": "class-12__calculus__integrals__fundamental-theorem-of-calculus",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Integrals",
    "subtopic": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus",
    "introduction": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus is a specific idea in Integrals. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of analysis, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Calculus when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using an integral, limit, or operator theorem without checking continuity, convergence, boundedness, or domain conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: If F(x)=x^3/3, then integral from 0 to 2 of x^2 dx is F(2)-F(0)=8/3.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If F(x)=x^3/3, then integral from 0 to 2 of x^2 dx is F(2)-F(0)=8/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fundamental Theorem of Calculus may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Calculus, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Fundamental Theorem of Calculus is about one main check: The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus links differentiation and integration as inverse processes. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Part I differentiates an accumulation function. Part II evaluates definite integrals using antiderivatives under continuity assumptions. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Integrals.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
