  {
    "sourceRow": 934,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__order",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Order",
    "introduction": "Order of a differential equation tells the highest derivative present. It helps choose the method needed to solve the equation.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Look for the derivative with the greatest order.\n\nHow It Works: Inspect every derivative, such as dy/dx, d2y/dx2, and d3y/dx3.\n\nWhy It Works: Higher derivatives describe deeper rates of change, so the largest one controls order.\n\nWhere We Use It: Motion, growth, cooling, and curve families use differential equations.\n\nCommon mistake: Calling the power of a derivative the order.\n\nCorrect idea: Order is about derivative level, not exponent.\n\nDefinition: Order is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nWorked Example: d2y/dx2 + 3dy/dx + y = 0 has order 2.\n\nVerification: The highest derivative is d2y/dx2.\n\n**Key Point:** Order is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: d2y/dx2 + 3dy/dx + y = 0 has order 2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Order is about derivative level, not exponent.\nExample 3 - Application: Motion, growth, cooling, and curve families use differential equations.\nVisual Understanding: Circle the tallest derivative ladder.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Order is the first classification before degree or solution method.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Order connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the order of d3y/dx3 + (dy/dx)^2 = x.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 935,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__degree",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Degree",
    "introduction": "Degree is the power of the highest-order derivative after the equation is polynomial in derivatives. It is not always defined.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: First remove radicals or fractions involving derivatives if possible.\n\nHow It Works: Check that derivatives occur polynomially, then read the power of the highest-order derivative.\n\nWhy It Works: Degree needs polynomial form because powers must be clear and whole-number based.\n\nWhere We Use It: It classifies differential equations in school-level solving.\n\nCommon mistake: Finding degree before clearing a radical on a derivative.\n\nCorrect idea: Degree is undefined if the equation cannot be made polynomial in derivatives.\n\nDefinition: Degree is the exponent of the highest-order derivative when defined.\n\nWorked Example: (d2y/dx2)^3 + dy/dx = 0 has order 2 and degree 3.\n\nVerification: The highest derivative d2y/dx2 has power 3.\n\n**Key Point:** Degree is the exponent of the highest-order derivative when defined.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (d2y/dx2)^3 + dy/dx = 0 has order 2 and degree 3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Degree is undefined if the equation cannot be made polynomial in derivatives.\nExample 3 - Application: It classifies differential equations in school-level solving.\nVisual Understanding: Look at the highest derivative after cleaning the equation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Degree is less central than order in advanced differential-equation theory.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Degree connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the degree of (dy/dx)^2 + y = 0.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 936,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__general-solution",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "General solution",
    "introduction": "A general solution contains arbitrary constants. It represents the whole family of curves satisfying the differential equation.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Solve the equation and keep the constants that appear from integration.\n\nHow It Works: The number of independent constants usually matches the order.\n\nWhy It Works: Integrating reverses differentiation, and constants are lost during differentiation.\n\nWhere We Use It: Growth models and curve families use general solutions.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting arbitrary constants after integration.\n\nCorrect idea: Every indefinite integration should include a constant.\n\nDefinition: A general solution contains arbitrary constants and satisfies the differential equation.\n\nWorked Example: dy/dx = 2x gives y = x^2 + C.\n\nVerification: Differentiate y=x^2+C to get dy/dx=2x.\n\n**Key Point:** A general solution contains arbitrary constants and satisfies the differential equation.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: dy/dx = 2x gives y = x^2 + C.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Every indefinite integration should include a constant.\nExample 3 - Application: Growth models and curve families use general solutions.\nVisual Understanding: A general solution is a family of answers.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Existence and uniqueness theorems decide when a general family becomes one curve under conditions.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, General solution connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Solve dy/dx = 3x^2 and write the general solution.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 937,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__particular-solution",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Particular solution",
    "introduction": "A particular solution is one member of the general solution chosen by a given condition. It gives one exact curve.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Start with the general solution, then substitute the condition.\n\nHow It Works: Solve for the arbitrary constant.\n\nWhy It Works: The condition fixes the unknown constant left by integration.\n\nWhere We Use It: Initial value problems in motion, cooling, and population use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Stopping at the general solution when a condition is given.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the condition to find the constant.\n\nDefinition: A particular solution satisfies both the differential equation and the given condition.\n\nWorked Example: If y=x^2+C and y(1)=5, then 5=1+C, so C=4 and y=x^2+4.\n\nVerification: Substitute x=1 to get y=5.\n\n**Key Point:** A particular solution satisfies both the differential equation and the given condition.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If y=x^2+C and y(1)=5, then 5=1+C, so C=4 and y=x^2+4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the condition to find the constant.\nExample 3 - Application: Initial value problems in motion, cooling, and population use it.\nVisual Understanding: The condition picks one curve from the family.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A well-posed initial value problem can have a unique particular solution.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Particular solution connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "For dy/dx=2x with y(0)=3, find the particular solution.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 938,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__formation",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Formation",
    "introduction": "Formation of a differential equation removes arbitrary constants from a family of curves. It creates an equation for the whole family.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Differentiate enough times to get equations involving the constants.\n\nHow It Works: Eliminate the constants using the original relation and derivatives.\n\nWhy It Works: Differentiation gives new relations that help remove fixed constants.\n\nWhere We Use It: It connects curve families with differential equations.\n\nCommon mistake: Differentiating too few times.\n\nCorrect idea: Differentiate as many times as the number of arbitrary constants.\n\nMethod: A family with n arbitrary constants usually gives a differential equation of order n.\n\nWorked Example: From y=mx+c, dy/dx=m and d2y/dx2=0, so the differential equation is d2y/dx2=0.\n\nVerification: All straight lines satisfy second derivative zero.\n\n**Key Point:** A family with n arbitrary constants usually gives a differential equation of order n.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: From y=mx+c, dy/dx=m and d2y/dx2=0, so the differential equation is d2y/dx2=0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Differentiate as many times as the number of arbitrary constants.\nExample 3 - Application: It connects curve families with differential equations.\nVisual Understanding: Remove constants by differentiating.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "This process describes families as solution sets of differential equations.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Formation connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Form the differential equation for y=ax^2.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 939,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__variable-separable",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Variable separable",
    "introduction": "A variable separable equation can be rearranged so x terms and y terms are on different sides. Then each side is integrated.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Move all y terms with dy and all x terms with dx.\n\nHow It Works: Integrate both sides and add a constant.\n\nWhy It Works: Separation works because the equation expresses the derivative as a product or quotient of single-variable parts.\n\nWhere We Use It: Growth, decay, and simple rate laws often separate.\n\nCommon mistake: Dividing by a factor that might be zero without noting it.\n\nCorrect idea: Check whether special constant solutions are lost when dividing.\n\nMethod: dy/dx = g(x)h(y) can be written dy/h(y)=g(x)dx when allowed.\n\nWorked Example: dy/dx=xy gives dy/y = x dx, so ln|y| = x^2/2 + C.\n\nVerification: Differentiate implicitly to recover dy/dx=xy.\n\n**Key Point:** dy/dx = g(x)h(y) can be written dy/h(y)=g(x)dx when allowed.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: dy/dx=xy gives dy/y = x dx, so ln|y| = x^2/2 + C.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check whether special constant solutions are lost when dividing.\nExample 3 - Application: Growth, decay, and simple rate laws often separate.\nVisual Understanding: Put x with dx and y with dy.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Separable equations are first-order equations reducible by direct integration.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Variable separable connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Solve dy/dx = 3x y for y not equal to zero.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 940,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__homogeneous-first-order",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Homogeneous first order",
    "introduction": "A homogeneous first-order differential equation has dy/dx as a function of y/x. Substitution y=vx makes it separable.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Check whether the right side depends on y/x.\n\nHow It Works: Put y=vx, so dy/dx=v+x dv/dx.\n\nWhy It Works: The substitution converts the ratio y/x into v, reducing two variables to one ratio variable.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used for slope fields that scale from the origin.\n\nCommon mistake: Using this method for any first-order equation.\n\nCorrect idea: The equation must depend on y/x or be homogeneous in x and y.\n\nMethod: For dy/dx = F(y/x), use y=vx.\n\nWorked Example: If dy/dx = y/x, then y=vx gives v+x dv/dx=v, so dv/dx=0 and y=Cx.\n\nVerification: Substitute y=Cx to get dy/dx=C=y/x.\n\n**Key Point:** For dy/dx = F(y/x), use y=vx.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If dy/dx = y/x, then y=vx gives v+x dv/dx=v, so dv/dx=0 and y=Cx.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The equation must depend on y/x or be homogeneous in x and y.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used for slope fields that scale from the origin.\nVisual Understanding: Use the ratio as the new variable.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Homogeneous equations are invariant under scaling (x,y)->(kx,ky).",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Homogeneous first order connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Solve dy/dx=(x+y)/x using y=vx.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 941,
    "id": "class-12__calculus__differential-equations__linear-first-order",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Calculus",
    "topic": "Differential Equations",
    "subtopic": "Linear first order",
    "introduction": "A linear first-order equation has y and dy/dx only to the first power. It is solved using an integrating factor.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write it as dy/dx + P(x)y = Q(x).\n\nHow It Works: Find IF = e^(integral P dx), multiply through, then integrate.\n\nWhy It Works: The integrating factor turns the left side into the derivative of IF times y.\n\nWhere We Use It: Mixture, growth, circuits, and cooling models use linear equations.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting to put the equation in standard form first.\n\nCorrect idea: Coefficient of dy/dx must be 1 before finding P(x).\n\nFormula: For dy/dx+P(x)y=Q(x), integrating factor = e^(integral P(x) dx).\n\nWorked Example: dy/dx + y = e^x has IF=e^x, so d(e^x y)/dx=e^(2x), giving e^x y=e^(2x)/2+C.\n\nVerification: Differentiate e^x y to check the product rule.\n\n**Key Point:** For dy/dx+P(x)y=Q(x), integrating factor = e^(integral P(x) dx).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: dy/dx + y = e^x has IF=e^x, so d(e^x y)/dx=e^(2x), giving e^x y=e^(2x)/2+C.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Coefficient of dy/dx must be 1 before finding P(x).\nExample 3 - Application: Mixture, growth, circuits, and cooling models use linear equations.\nVisual Understanding: The integrating factor makes the product rule appear.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Linear equations are solved by converting them into exact derivative form.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Linear first order connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Solve dy/dx + 2y = x by finding the integrating factor.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 942,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__scalars",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Scalars",
    "introduction": "A scalar has magnitude only, not direction. It is useful for quantities measured by size alone.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Identify whether direction matters for the quantity.\n\nHow It Works: If only size is needed, treat it as a scalar.\n\nWhy It Works: Scalars stay the same when axes are rotated, unlike vector components.\n\nWhere We Use It: Mass, time, temperature, and distance are scalars.\n\nCommon mistake: Calling speed and velocity the same thing.\n\nCorrect idea: Speed is scalar; velocity includes direction.\n\nDefinition: A scalar is a quantity with magnitude but no direction.\n\nWorked Example: A car speed of 60 km/h is scalar; east is not part of it.\n\nVerification: Changing direction does not change speed.\n\n**Key Point:** A scalar is a quantity with magnitude but no direction.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A car speed of 60 km/h is scalar; east is not part of it.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Speed is scalar; velocity includes direction.\nExample 3 - Application: Mass, time, temperature, and distance are scalars.\nVisual Understanding: A scalar is just a number with size.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Scalars are elements of the field over which vector spaces are defined.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Scalars connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Classify mass, displacement, speed, and force as scalar or vector.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 943,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__vectors",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Vectors",
    "introduction": "A vector has magnitude and direction. It is used when direction changes the meaning of a quantity.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Represent it by an arrow or components.\n\nHow It Works: State both size and direction, or write component form.\n\nWhy It Works: Two quantities with the same size can differ if directions differ.\n\nWhere We Use It: Displacement, velocity, force, and acceleration use vectors.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking any number with a unit is a vector.\n\nCorrect idea: Direction is required for a vector.\n\nDefinition: A vector is a quantity with magnitude and direction.\n\nWorked Example: A displacement of 5 km east is a vector.\n\nVerification: A 5 km west displacement is different.\n\n**Key Point:** A vector is a quantity with magnitude and direction.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A displacement of 5 km east is a vector.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Direction is required for a vector.\nExample 3 - Application: Displacement, velocity, force, and acceleration use vectors.\nVisual Understanding: A vector is an arrow with length and direction.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Vectors are elements of vector spaces, where addition and scalar multiplication obey axioms.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Vectors connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give two vectors with equal magnitude but different directions.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 944,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__magnitude",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Magnitude",
    "introduction": "Magnitude is the length or size of a vector. It ignores direction but measures how large the vector is.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use distance formula for components.\n\nHow It Works: For vector ai+bj+ck, compute square root of a2+b2+c2.\n\nWhy It Works: Components form a right triangle or rectangular box, so Pythagoras gives length.\n\nWhere We Use It: Speed from velocity and force size use magnitude.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding components without squaring.\n\nCorrect idea: Magnitude is distance, so use squares and square root.\n\nFormula: |a i + b j + c k| = sqrt(a^2+b^2+c^2).\n\nWorked Example: For vector 3i+4j, magnitude = sqrt(9+16)=5.\n\nVerification: A 3-4-5 right triangle confirms it.\n\n**Key Point:** |a i + b j + c k| = sqrt(a^2+b^2+c^2).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For vector 3i+4j, magnitude = sqrt(9+16)=5.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Magnitude is distance, so use squares and square root.\nExample 3 - Application: Speed from velocity and force size use magnitude.\nVisual Understanding: Magnitude is arrow length.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Norms generalize magnitude in vector spaces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Magnitude connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the magnitude of 2i-3j+6k.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 945,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__direction",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Direction",
    "introduction": "Direction tells where a vector points. It completes the meaning that magnitude alone cannot give.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use angle, unit vector, or direction cosines.\n\nHow It Works: Compare the vector with coordinate axes or another reference line.\n\nWhy It Works: Direction is determined by the ratios of components, not by size alone.\n\nWhere We Use It: Navigation, forces, and velocity need direction.\n\nCommon mistake: Saying two parallel opposite vectors have the same direction.\n\nCorrect idea: Opposite vectors have opposite directions even if their lines are parallel.\n\nDefinition: Direction of a vector is its orientation in space.\n\nWorked Example: Vector 2i points along positive x-axis; -2i points along negative x-axis.\n\nVerification: Their magnitudes match but directions differ.\n\n**Key Point:** Direction of a vector is its orientation in space.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Vector 2i points along positive x-axis; -2i points along negative x-axis.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Opposite vectors have opposite directions even if their lines are parallel.\nExample 3 - Application: Navigation, forces, and velocity need direction.\nVisual Understanding: Direction is the arrow's facing.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Direction can be represented by a unit vector v/|v| for non-zero v.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Direction connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the unit direction of vector 3i+4j.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 946,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__direction-cosines",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Direction cosines",
    "introduction": "Direction cosines are cosines of the angles a line or vector makes with the coordinate axes. They describe direction in 3D.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find angles with x, y, z axes or divide components by magnitude.\n\nHow It Works: For vector ai+bj+ck, direction cosines are a/r, b/r, c/r.\n\nWhy It Works: Projection of the vector on each axis divided by length gives cosine of the angle.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D lines, planes, and vectors use them.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting that their squares add to 1.\n\nCorrect idea: For a non-zero vector, l2+m2+n2=1.\n\nFormula: If l,m,n are direction cosines, then l^2+m^2+n^2=1.\n\nWorked Example: For vector (2,3,6), r=7, so direction cosines are 2/7,3/7,6/7.\n\nVerification: Squares sum to 4/49+9/49+36/49=1.\n\n**Key Point:** If l,m,n are direction cosines, then l^2+m^2+n^2=1.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For vector (2,3,6), r=7, so direction cosines are 2/7,3/7,6/7.\nExample 2 - Contrast: For a non-zero vector, l2+m2+n2=1.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D lines, planes, and vectors use them.\nVisual Understanding: They are direction fingerprints.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Direction cosines are coordinates of the unit vector in that direction.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Direction cosines connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find direction cosines of vector i+2j+2k.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 947,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__vector-addition",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Vector addition",
    "introduction": "Vector addition combines displacements or directed quantities. The result is called the resultant vector.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Place arrows head-to-tail or add components.\n\nHow It Works: Add matching components: i with i, j with j, k with k.\n\nWhy It Works: Moving one vector after another gives the same final displacement as the resultant.\n\nWhere We Use It: Displacement, forces, and velocities use vector addition.\n\nCommon mistake: Adding magnitudes even when directions differ.\n\nCorrect idea: Magnitude of sum depends on directions, not only sizes.\n\nRule: (a,b,c)+(d,e,f)=(a+d,b+e,c+f).\n\nWorked Example: (2i+3j)+(-i+4j)=i+7j.\n\nVerification: Subtract the second vector to recover the first.\n\n**Key Point:** (a,b,c)+(d,e,f)=(a+d,b+e,c+f).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (2i+3j)+(-i+4j)=i+7j.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Magnitude of sum depends on directions, not only sizes.\nExample 3 - Application: Displacement, forces, and velocities use vector addition.\nVisual Understanding: Add arrows by head-to-tail movement.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Vector addition is commutative and associative in vector spaces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Vector addition connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Add vectors 3i-j+2k and -i+5j+k.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 948,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__scalar-multiplication",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Scalar multiplication",
    "introduction": "Scalar multiplication explains a specific idea inside Vectors. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Vectors and 3D Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Multiply every component by the scalar. How It Works: Positive scalars keep direction; negative scalars reverse direction. Why It Works: Each component scales equally, so the vector remains parallel to the original. Where We Use It: Scaling forces, velocities, and position vectors uses this idea. Common mistake: Multiplying only one component. Correct idea: A scalar multiplies every component of the vector. Rule: k(a,b,c)=(ka,kb,kc). Worked Example: -2(3i-j)= -6i+2j. Verification: The new vector is parallel but opposite in direction. **Key Point:** k(a,b,c)=(ka,kb,kc). Worked Example: For Scalar multiplication, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Scalar multiplication but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Scalar multiplication becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Scalar multiplication, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Scalar multiplication with measuring a room, reading a map, checking a tile pattern, or drawing a bridge part.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Scalar multiplication is easiest when you draw it. Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Scalar multiplication connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations. In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Scalar multiplication. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 949,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__section-formula",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Section formula",
    "introduction": "Section formula finds the point dividing a line segment in a given ratio. It works with position vectors or coordinates.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use the coordinates of endpoints and the ratio.\n\nHow It Works: For internal division m:n, weight the opposite endpoint coordinates.\n\nWhy It Works: The point is a weighted average of endpoint positions.\n\nWhere We Use It: Maps, coordinate geometry, and center-of-mass ideas use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Putting weights with the same endpoint instead of opposite endpoint.\n\nCorrect idea: For AP:PB=m:n, use (nx1+mx2)/(m+n).\n\nFormula: Internal section point = ((mx2+nx1)/(m+n), (my2+ny1)/(m+n), (mz2+nz1)/(m+n)).\n\nWorked Example: Point dividing A(1,2) and B(4,8) in ratio 1:2 is ((1x4+2x1)/3,(1x8+2x2)/3)=(2,4).\n\nVerification: Distances AP and PB are in ratio 1:2.\n\n**Key Point:** Internal section point = ((mx2+nx1)/(m+n), (my2+ny1)/(m+n), (mz2+nz1)/(m+n)).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Point dividing A(1,2) and B(4,8) in ratio 1:2 is ((1x4+2x1)/3,(1x8+2x2)/3)=(2,4).\nExample 2 - Contrast: For AP:PB=m:n, use (nx1+mx2)/(m+n).\nExample 3 - Application: Maps, coordinate geometry, and center-of-mass ideas use it.\nVisual Understanding: A ratio point is a weighted average.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Section formula is affine combination of position vectors.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Section formula connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the midpoint of (2,5,1) and (6,1,3) using section formula.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 950,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__dot-product",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Dot product",
    "introduction": "Dot product multiplies vectors to give a scalar. It measures how much one vector points along another.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Multiply matching components and add, or use magnitudes and angle.\n\nHow It Works: Use a dot b = |a||b|cos theta.\n\nWhy It Works: Cosine measures the aligned part of one vector along the other.\n\nWhere We Use It: Work done, projections, and angle between vectors use dot product.\n\nCommon mistake: Thinking dot product gives a vector.\n\nCorrect idea: Dot product gives a scalar number.\n\nFormula: a dot b = a1b1+a2b2+a3b3 = |a||b|cos theta.\n\nWorked Example: (1,2,3) dot (4,0,-1)=4+0-3=1.\n\nVerification: Component formula and angle formula must match.\n\n**Key Point:** a dot b = a1b1+a2b2+a3b3 = |a||b|cos theta.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: (1,2,3) dot (4,0,-1)=4+0-3=1.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Dot product gives a scalar number.\nExample 3 - Application: Work done, projections, and angle between vectors use dot product.\nVisual Understanding: Dot product measures alignment.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Dot product defines inner products and orthogonality in Euclidean spaces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Dot product connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the dot product of 2i+j and i-3j.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 951,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__projection",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Projection",
    "introduction": "Projection is the shadow of one vector on another direction. It measures the component along that direction.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use dot product with a unit vector in the target direction.\n\nHow It Works: Scalar projection of a on b is (a dot b)/|b|.\n\nWhy It Works: Dot product keeps only the aligned part because it uses cosine.\n\nWhere We Use It: Work, shadows, and resolving forces use projections.\n\nCommon mistake: Dividing by |b|^2 when asking for scalar projection.\n\nCorrect idea: Scalar projection divides by |b|; vector projection divides by |b|^2 and multiplies by b.\n\nFormula: Scalar projection of a on b = (a dot b)/|b|.\n\nWorked Example: For a=(3,4), b=(1,0), projection is 3.\n\nVerification: The shadow on x-axis has length 3.\n\n**Key Point:** Scalar projection of a on b = (a dot b)/|b|.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a=(3,4), b=(1,0), projection is 3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Scalar projection divides by |b|; vector projection divides by |b|^2 and multiplies by b.\nExample 3 - Application: Work, shadows, and resolving forces use projections.\nVisual Understanding: Projection is a vector shadow.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Orthogonal projection is central in least squares and Hilbert spaces.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Projection connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find scalar projection of (2,2) on (1,1).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 952,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__cross-product",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Cross product",
    "introduction": "Cross product explains a specific idea inside Vectors. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Vectors and 3D Geometry accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use determinant form or component formula. How It Works: Follow right-hand rule for direction. Why It Works: The sine factor measures the part perpendicular to the other vector. Where We Use It: Torque, area, and normals to planes use cross products. Common mistake: Reversing order without changing sign. Correct idea: a x b = -(b x a). Formula: |a x b| = |a||b|sin theta. Worked Example: i x j = k, but j x i = -k. Verification: Right-hand rule confirms direction. **Key Point:** |a x b| = |a||b|sin theta. Worked Example: For Cross product, draw a small labelled figure first. Mark the given sides, angles, or units. Then apply the correct definition or formula and check that the final unit matches the quantity. Verification: Re-read the question, check the required condition, and test the answer with a small example. Common mistake: Students may remember the name of Cross product but skip the condition or unit. Correct idea: Say what is given, what rule applies, and why that rule fits this exact problem. Visual Understanding: Use a labelled shape, grid, angle mark, or coordinate picture so the symbols connect to something visible. Key Point: Cross product becomes easier when the definition, method, and check are used together.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: i x j = k, but j x i = -k.\nExample 2 - Contrast: a x b = -(b x a).\nExample 3 - Application: Torque, area, and normals to planes use cross products.\nVisual Understanding: Cross product makes a perpendicular vector.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cross product is easiest when you draw it. Mark the known parts first, then use the shape rule that matches those labels.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cross product connects visual shape facts with measurement, congruence, similarity, coordinates, or transformations. In advanced work, the same idea becomes a statement about invariants under allowed geometric changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Compute i x (j+k), then explain why the answer is perpendicular to i and to (j+k).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 953,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__vectors__area-by-cross-product",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Vectors",
    "subtopic": "Area by cross product",
    "introduction": "Area by cross product uses vector multiplication to measure parallelogram or triangle area. It is powerful in coordinate geometry.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Build two side vectors from a common point.\n\nHow It Works: Parallelogram area is |a x b|; triangle area is half of that.\n\nWhy It Works: The cross product magnitude equals base times perpendicular height.\n\nWhere We Use It: Coordinate geometry, physics, and 3D geometry use it.\n\nCommon mistake: Forgetting the half for triangle area.\n\nCorrect idea: Cross product gives parallelogram area, not triangle area.\n\nFormula: Triangle area = 1/2 |a x b|.\n\nWorked Example: For a=i and b=j, |a x b|=1, so triangle area=1/2.\n\nVerification: The right triangle has base 1 and height 1.\n\n**Key Point:** Triangle area = 1/2 |a x b|.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a=i and b=j, |a x b|=1, so triangle area=1/2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Cross product gives parallelogram area, not triangle area.\nExample 3 - Application: Coordinate geometry, physics, and 3D geometry use it.\nVisual Understanding: Cross product area is the size of the tilted parallelogram.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "This is an exterior-product idea in vector geometry.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Area by cross product connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find area of triangle with vertices O(0,0,0), A(2,0,0), B(0,3,0).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
