  {
    "sourceRow": 954,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__direction-ratios",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Direction ratios",
    "introduction": "Direction ratios are three numbers proportional to a line's direction components. They describe direction without needing unit length.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use any vector parallel to the line.\n\nHow It Works: The components of that vector are direction ratios.\n\nWhy It Works: Multiplying all ratios by the same non-zero number keeps the same direction.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D line equations and angles use direction ratios.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating direction ratios as unique.\n\nCorrect idea: They can be scaled; 1,2,3 and 2,4,6 describe the same direction.\n\nDefinition: Direction ratios are proportional to components of a direction vector.\n\nWorked Example: Line through A(1,2,3) and B(3,5,7) has direction ratios 2,3,4.\n\nVerification: Vector AB = (2,3,4).\n\n**Key Point:** Direction ratios are proportional to components of a direction vector.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Line through A(1,2,3) and B(3,5,7) has direction ratios 2,3,4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: They can be scaled; 1,2,3 and 2,4,6 describe the same direction.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D line equations and angles use direction ratios.\nVisual Understanding: Direction ratios are unsimplified direction coordinates.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Direction ratios represent a projective direction class in space.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Direction ratios connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find direction ratios of the line through (0,1,2) and (4,3,1).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 955,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__direction-cosines",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
