  {
    "sourceRow": 956,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__line-in-space",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Line in space",
    "introduction": "A line in space can be described by a point and a direction vector. This tells every point on the line.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Choose one point and direction ratios or vector.\n\nHow It Works: Write vector or parametric form using a parameter.\n\nWhy It Works: Starting point plus all scalar multiples of direction gives the whole line.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D geometry, motion paths, and modelling use space lines.\n\nCommon mistake: Using only one point to define a line.\n\nCorrect idea: A point alone is not enough; direction is also needed.\n\nDefinition: A line through point a with direction b is r = a + lambda b.\n\nWorked Example: Through (1,2,3) with direction (2,0,1): (x,y,z)=(1+2t,2,3+t).\n\nVerification: At t=0 the point is (1,2,3).\n\n**Key Point:** A line through point a with direction b is r = a + lambda b.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Through (1,2,3) with direction (2,0,1): (x,y,z)=(1+2t,2,3+t).\nExample 2 - Contrast: A point alone is not enough; direction is also needed.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D geometry, motion paths, and modelling use space lines.\nVisual Understanding: A space line is point plus direction.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Lines are one-dimensional affine subspaces of R3.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Line in space connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write parametric equations for line through (0,0,1) with direction (1,2,3).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 957,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__angle-between-lines",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Angle between lines",
    "introduction": "Angle between lines in space is found from their direction vectors. The lines need not meet.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Take direction vectors of both lines.\n\nHow It Works: Use dot product formula with absolute value for acute angle if required.\n\nWhy It Works: The angle between lines equals the angle between their directions.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D geometry and engineering alignment use this idea.\n\nCommon mistake: Using points on the lines instead of direction vectors.\n\nCorrect idea: Angle depends on direction vectors, not location.\n\nFormula: cos theta = |a dot b|/(|a||b|) for the acute angle between lines.\n\nWorked Example: For directions (1,0,0) and (0,1,0), dot product is 0, so theta=90 degrees.\n\nVerification: Perpendicular axes confirm it.\n\n**Key Point:** cos theta = |a dot b|/(|a||b|) for the acute angle between lines.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For directions (1,0,0) and (0,1,0), dot product is 0, so theta=90 degrees.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Angle depends on direction vectors, not location.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D geometry and engineering alignment use this idea.\nVisual Understanding: Compare arrows, not positions.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "This extends inner-product angle ideas to affine lines.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Angle between lines connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find angle between lines with directions (1,1,0) and (1,-1,0).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 958,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__shortest-distance-skew-lines",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Shortest distance skew lines",
    "introduction": "Skew lines are non-parallel lines in space that do not meet. Their shortest distance is measured along a common perpendicular.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use direction vectors and a vector joining one point on each line.\n\nHow It Works: Project the joining vector onto the normal direction a x b.\n\nWhy It Works: The shortest segment must be perpendicular to both line directions.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D geometry and spatial modelling use skew-line distance.\n\nCommon mistake: Using 2D intersection ideas for skew lines.\n\nCorrect idea: Skew lines may not intersect even when not parallel.\n\nFormula: Distance = |(b1-b2) dot (a1 x a2)| / |a1 x a2| for non-parallel lines.\n\nWorked Example: For lines along x-axis through origin and y-axis through (0,0,1), distance is 1.\n\nVerification: The z-separation is perpendicular to both directions.\n\n**Key Point:** Distance = |(b1-b2) dot (a1 x a2)| / |a1 x a2| for non-parallel lines.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For lines along x-axis through origin and y-axis through (0,0,1), distance is 1.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Skew lines may not intersect even when not parallel.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D geometry and spatial modelling use skew-line distance.\nVisual Understanding: Shortest distance is the common perpendicular length.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "The formula is scalar triple product volume divided by base area.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Shortest distance skew lines connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why skew lines cannot exist in a plane.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 959,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__plane-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Plane introduction",
    "introduction": "A plane is a flat surface extending infinitely in two directions. In 3D, a point and normal vector can define it.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Use a point on the plane and a normal vector perpendicular to it.\n\nHow It Works: Write n dot (r-a)=0.\n\nWhy It Works: All points in the plane have displacement from a perpendicular to the normal.\n\nWhere We Use It: Walls, floors, and coordinate geometry use planes.\n\nCommon mistake: Confusing a line direction with a plane normal.\n\nCorrect idea: A normal is perpendicular to the plane, not lying in it.\n\nDefinition: Plane with normal n through point a satisfies n dot (r-a)=0.\n\nWorked Example: Normal (1,2,3) through (1,0,0) gives (x-1)+2y+3z=0.\n\nVerification: Point (1,0,0) satisfies the equation.\n\n**Key Point:** Plane with normal n through point a satisfies n dot (r-a)=0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Normal (1,2,3) through (1,0,0) gives (x-1)+2y+3z=0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A normal is perpendicular to the plane, not lying in it.\nExample 3 - Application: Walls, floors, and coordinate geometry use planes.\nVisual Understanding: A plane is a flat sheet with a perpendicular arrow.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Planes are two-dimensional affine subspaces of R3.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Plane introduction connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find equation of plane through origin with normal (2,-1,3).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 960,
    "id": "class-12__vectors-and-3d-geometry__three-dimensional-geometry__angle-line-plane-introduction",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Vectors and 3D Geometry",
    "topic": "Three-Dimensional Geometry",
    "subtopic": "Angle line-plane introduction",
    "introduction": "Angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its projection on the plane. It relates to the plane normal.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find angle phi between line direction and plane normal.\n\nHow It Works: Then line-plane angle theta satisfies theta = 90 degrees - phi.\n\nWhy It Works: A line perpendicular to the normal lies in a parallel direction to the plane.\n\nWhere We Use It: 3D geometry, optics, and engineering use this angle.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the angle with the normal as the answer.\n\nCorrect idea: The line-plane angle is complementary to the line-normal angle.\n\nFormula: sin theta = |b dot n|/(|b||n|), where b is line direction and n is plane normal.\n\nWorked Example: If b=(1,0,0) and n=(1,0,0), sin theta=1, so theta=90 degrees.\n\nVerification: The line is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n**Key Point:** sin theta = |b dot n|/(|b||n|), where b is line direction and n is plane normal.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If b=(1,0,0) and n=(1,0,0), sin theta=1, so theta=90 degrees.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The line-plane angle is complementary to the line-normal angle.\nExample 3 - Application: 3D geometry, optics, and engineering use this angle.\nVisual Understanding: Compare the line with its shadow on the plane.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "This formula comes from orthogonal projection onto the normal direction.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Angle line-plane introduction connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the angle between a line direction (0,0,1) and plane z=0.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 961,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__linear-inequalities",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Linear inequalities",
    "introduction": "Linear inequalities describe half-planes or regions instead of single boundary lines.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Graph the boundary line, then test one point to choose the correct side.\n\nHow It Works: The inequality keeps all points whose coordinates make the statement true.\n\nWhy It Works: Constraints in linear programming are written as linear inequalities.\n\nWhere We Use It: Shading the wrong side of the boundary.\n\nCommon mistake: Test a point not on the line before shading.\n\nCorrect idea: Rule\n\nA linear inequality in two variables represents a half-plane.: For x+y<=4, point (0,0) works, so shade the side containing the origin.\n\nWorked Example: Substitute a point from the shaded side.\n\nVerification: An inequality shades a side of a line.\n\n**Key Point:** For x+y<=4, point (0,0) works, so shade the side containing the origin.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Substitute a point from the shaded side.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Rule\nExample 3 - Application: Shading the wrong side of the boundary.\nVisual Understanding: Feasible sets in linear programming are intersections of half-planes.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Graph x+2y<=6 and test (0,0).",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Linear inequalities connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example involving Linear inequalities and explain the required condition.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 962,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__feasible-region",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Feasible region",
    "introduction": "The feasible region is the set of all points satisfying every constraint. It contains all allowed solutions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Graph each constraint and overlap the shaded regions.\n\nHow It Works: Include boundary lines when inequalities use <= or >=.\n\nWhy It Works: Only points satisfying all constraints can be considered for optimization.\n\nWhere We Use It: Resource allocation and planning use feasible regions.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing before finding the feasible region.\n\nCorrect idea: The objective is checked only inside the feasible region.\n\nDefinition: Feasible region is the common solution set of all constraints.\n\nWorked Example: If x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=5, the feasible region is a triangle.\n\nVerification: Test a point such as (1,1) in all inequalities.\n\n**Key Point:** Feasible region is the common solution set of all constraints.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=5, the feasible region is a triangle.\nExample 2 - Contrast: The objective is checked only inside the feasible region.\nExample 3 - Application: Resource allocation and planning use feasible regions.\nVisual Understanding: Feasible means allowed by every rule.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A feasible region is a convex polygon or unbounded convex set for linear constraints.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Feasible region connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find whether (2,3) is feasible for x+y<=6, x>=0, y>=0.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 963,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__objective-function",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Objective function",
    "introduction": "The objective function is the quantity to maximize or minimize. It converts each feasible point into a value.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Write the profit, cost, or target expression in variables.\n\nHow It Works: Evaluate it at candidate feasible points.\n\nWhy It Works: Linear objectives reach optimum at boundary corners when an optimum exists.\n\nWhere We Use It: Business planning and resource use need objective functions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating a constraint as the objective.\n\nCorrect idea: Constraints limit choices; objective ranks choices.\n\nDefinition: An objective function is the expression to be optimized.\n\nWorked Example: If profit P=40x+30y, at (2,3), P=80+90=170.\n\nVerification: Check by substituting coordinates.\n\n**Key Point:** An objective function is the expression to be optimized.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If profit P=40x+30y, at (2,3), P=80+90=170.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Constraints limit choices; objective ranks choices.\nExample 3 - Application: Business planning and resource use need objective functions.\nVisual Understanding: Objective is the score of each allowed point.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Linear functionals attain extrema on compact polygonal feasible regions at extreme points.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Objective function connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "For P=5x+8y, compare values at (0,4), (3,2), and (6,0).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 964,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__corner-point-method",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Corner point method",
    "introduction": "Corner point method checks objective values at vertices of the feasible region. It works for linear programming problems with bounded polygonal regions.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Find all corner points of the feasible region.\n\nHow It Works: Evaluate the objective function at each corner and choose best value.\n\nWhy It Works: A linear objective cannot have a strict interior maximum or minimum over a polygon unless constant along a region.\n\nWhere We Use It: Production planning and diet problems use this method.\n\nCommon mistake: Checking only one convenient corner.\n\nCorrect idea: Every corner must be tested unless a theorem or graph removes it.\n\nMethod: For bounded linear programming, an optimum occurs at a corner point.\n\nWorked Example: For P=x+y on triangle (0,0),(4,0),(0,3), values are 0,4,3; maximum is 4.\n\nVerification: Compare all listed values.\n\n**Key Point:** For bounded linear programming, an optimum occurs at a corner point.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For P=x+y on triangle (0,0),(4,0),(0,3), values are 0,4,3; maximum is 4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Every corner must be tested unless a theorem or graph removes it.\nExample 3 - Application: Production planning and diet problems use this method.\nVisual Understanding: Corners are decision candidates.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "This follows from convexity and the extreme point theorem.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Corner point method connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use corner point method for P=2x+3y at (0,0),(5,0),(0,4).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 965,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__bounded-region",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Bounded region",
    "introduction": "A bounded region is enclosed and fits inside a large circle. In linear programming, it usually gives finite maximum and minimum values.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Inspect whether the feasible region closes on all sides.\n\nHow It Works: If it has no endless direction, it is bounded.\n\nWhy It Works: A closed bounded polygon has finitely many corners to test.\n\nWhere We Use It: Planning problems with upper resource limits often produce bounded regions.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming every feasible region is bounded.\n\nCorrect idea: Some constraints leave an open direction continuing forever.\n\nDefinition: A bounded feasible region has finite extent in every direction.\n\nWorked Example: x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=5 forms a bounded triangle.\n\nVerification: All points lie inside a square from 0 to 5.\n\n**Key Point:** A bounded feasible region has finite extent in every direction.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=5 forms a bounded triangle.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Some constraints leave an open direction continuing forever.\nExample 3 - Application: Planning problems with upper resource limits often produce bounded regions.\nVisual Understanding: Bounded means fenced in.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Compact feasible regions guarantee continuous objectives attain extrema.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Bounded region connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is x>=0, y>=0, x+y<=10 bounded? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 966,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__unbounded-region",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Unbounded region",
    "introduction": "An unbounded region extends forever in at least one direction. Optimization may still have an optimum, but not always.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Graph the constraints and look for an endless shaded direction.\n\nHow It Works: Check objective values along that direction if needed.\n\nWhy It Works: If the objective can keep improving forever, no finite optimum exists.\n\nWhere We Use It: Open-ended resource problems and inequalities can create unbounded regions.\n\nCommon mistake: Saying unbounded means no solution.\n\nCorrect idea: It may have many feasible points, but optimum may or may not exist.\n\nDefinition: An unbounded region is not contained in any finite circle.\n\nWorked Example: x>=0, y>=0, x+y>=2 is unbounded.\n\nVerification: Points like (100,100) still satisfy it.\n\n**Key Point:** An unbounded region is not contained in any finite circle.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: x>=0, y>=0, x+y>=2 is unbounded.\nExample 2 - Contrast: It may have many feasible points, but optimum may or may not exist.\nExample 3 - Application: Open-ended resource problems and inequalities can create unbounded regions.\nVisual Understanding: Unbounded means no far fence.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Linear objectives on unbounded feasible sets can be finite, infinite, or unattained.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Unbounded region connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give one point far away in x>=0, y>=0, x+y>=5.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 967,
    "id": "class-12__linear-programming__optimization__multiple-optima",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Linear Programming",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Multiple optima",
    "introduction": "Multiple optima occur when more than one feasible point gives the same best objective value. Often an entire edge is optimal.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Evaluate the objective at corners.\n\nHow It Works: If two adjacent corners give the same optimum, every point on the edge between them is also optimal.\n\nWhy It Works: A linear objective has constant value along lines parallel to its level lines.\n\nWhere We Use It: Production choices may have equal profit at different mixes.\n\nCommon mistake: Choosing only one optimum and ignoring the edge.\n\nCorrect idea: State all optimal points when an edge is optimal.\n\nPrinciple: If adjacent optimal corners have equal objective value, every point on their connecting edge is optimal.\n\nWorked Example: For P=x+y on edge x+y=5, every point on that edge gives P=5.\n\nVerification: Substitute two different edge points such as (2,3) and (4,1).\n\n**Key Point:** If adjacent optimal corners have equal objective value, every point on their connecting edge is optimal.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For P=x+y on edge x+y=5, every point on that edge gives P=5.\nExample 2 - Contrast: State all optimal points when an edge is optimal.\nExample 3 - Application: Production choices may have equal profit at different mixes.\nVisual Understanding: A flat score line touches along an edge.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Multiple optima reflect non-unique maximizers of a linear functional on a convex set.",
    "advancedExplanation": "At a deeper level, Multiple optima connects definitions with proof conditions, solution methods, and structural classification.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find two different points with same value P=x+y on x+y=6.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 968,
    "id": "class-12__probability__advanced-probability__conditional-probability",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Probability",
    "topic": "Advanced Probability",
    "subtopic": "Conditional probability",
    "introduction": "Conditional probability explains a specific idea inside Advanced Probability. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Probability accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: State exactly what Conditional probability means before using it. How It Works: Write the given information, check the required conditions, then apply the matching rule or definition. Why It Works: The method works because each step follows from the definition and preserves the mathematical meaning. Where We Use It: It appears in games, risk, statistics, data science, and decision problems. Common mistake: Using a familiar rule without checking its conditions. Correct idea: Check the definition, allowed values, and assumptions before applying the rule. Formula: P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0. Worked Example: If P(A intersection B)=0.2 and P(B)=0.5, then P(A|B)=0.4. Verification: The denominator is non-zero and the result lies between 0 and 1. **Key Point:** Conditional probability depends on the correct sample space and conditions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Conditional probability, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6. Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Conditional probability with school marks, cricket scores, weather data, survey answers, or game results.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a small table, bar graph, probability tree, or sample list and explain what each part represents.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Conditional probability is about reading information from data. Start with a small table, compute carefully, then say what the number means.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Conditional probability connects sample information with uncertainty, modelling, and inference. More advanced statistics studies assumptions, variability, bias, and how conclusions change when the data model changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Create one example for Conditional probability. Solve it step by step, show a quick check, and explain one common mistake a student might make.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 969,
    "id": "class-12__probability__advanced-probability__multiplication-theorem",
    "classLevel": "Class 12",
    "chapter": "Probability",
    "topic": "Advanced Probability",
    "subtopic": "Multiplication theorem",
    "introduction": "Multiplication theorem explains a specific idea inside Advanced Probability. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Probability accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: State exactly what Multiplication theorem means before using it. How It Works: Write the given information, check the required conditions, then apply the matching rule or definition. Why It Works: The method works because each step follows from the definition and preserves the mathematical meaning. Where We Use It: It appears in games, risk, statistics, data science, and decision problems. Common mistake: Using a familiar rule without checking its conditions. Correct idea: Check the definition, allowed values, and assumptions before applying the rule. Rule: P(A intersection B)=P(A)P(B|A), where P(A)>0. Worked Example: If P(A)=1/3 and P(B|A)=1/2, then P(A and B)=1/6. Verification: The result cannot exceed P(A). **Key Point:** Multiplication theorem depends on the correct sample space and conditions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Multiplication theorem, make a tiny data set such as 2, 4, 4, 6. Compute the required value from the data, then explain what the answer says about the group.\nExample 2 - Contrast: A similar-looking problem can be wrong if it breaks the condition, unit, order, or definition.\nExample 3 - Application: Use Multiplication theorem with school marks, cricket scores, weather data, survey answers, or game results.\nVisual Model: Show the idea using a small table, bar graph, probability tree, or sample list and explain what each part represents.",
