  {
    "sourceRow": 983,
    "id": "school-enrichment-olympiad__number-theory__divisibility-and-congruences__fermat-s-little-theorem",
    "classLevel": "School Enrichment / Olympiad",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Divisibility and Congruences",
    "subtopic": "Fermat's Little Theorem",
    "introduction": "Fermat's Little Theorem is a standard result about powers modulo a prime. It says that prime moduli create a clean repeating structure for nonzero residues. This is useful in Olympiad number theory, modular arithmetic, and cryptography.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: If p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.\n\nHow It Works: First check that p is prime. Then check that a is not divisible by p. After that, raise a to p-1 and reduce modulo p.\n\nWhy It Works: The nonzero residues modulo p form a multiplicative group of size p-1. Powers of a must obey the group structure.\n\nWhere We Use It: It helps simplify large powers and prove divisibility results.\n\nCommon mistake: Using the theorem when the modulus is not prime.\n\nCorrect idea: For composite modulus, use Euler's theorem with gcd(a,n)=1 instead.\n\nWorked Example: For p=7 and a=3, 3^6=729. Since 729 leaves remainder 1 when divided by 7, 3^6 is congruent to 1 modulo 7.\n\nVerification: Divide 729 by 7. The remainder is 1.\n\n**Key Point:** Fermat's Little Theorem needs a prime modulus and a base not divisible by that prime.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 2^4=16 is congruent to 1 modulo 5.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Modulo 6 is not prime, so Fermat's Little Theorem does not apply directly.\nExample 3 - Application: To simplify 3^100 modulo 7, reduce the exponent using cycles of length 6.\nVisual Model: Make a table of powers and remainders to see the repeating cycle.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Use Fermat's Little Theorem only with a prime modulus. Check the prime first, then check that the base is not a multiple of that prime.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This theorem is Lagrange's theorem applied to the multiplicative group of nonzero residues modulo p. It is a special prime-modulus case of Euler's theorem.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use Fermat's Little Theorem to simplify 2^20 modulo 5. State why the theorem applies before calculating.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
