    "subtopic": "Symmetry",
    "introduction": "Symmetry is a specific idea in Strategies. Symmetry is a shape or measurement idea. A labelled diagram shows which lengths, angles, or symmetries control the answer. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Symmetry is a shape or measurement idea. A labelled diagram shows which lengths, angles, or symmetries control the answer.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Symmetry. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of geometry, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Problem Solving when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of remembering the topic name but skipping the condition that makes the rule valid.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For Symmetry, draw a labelled figure and mark the known lengths, angles, or symmetry line. Then apply the shape rule and check the unit.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Symmetry is a shape or measurement idea. A labelled diagram shows which lengths, angles, or symmetries control the answer.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Symmetry, draw a labelled figure and mark the known lengths, angles, or symmetry line. Then apply the shape rule and check the unit.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Symmetry may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Problem Solving, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Symmetry.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Symmetry is easiest when you attach it to one small example. Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Symmetry becomes deeper when examples, counterexamples, and hypotheses are studied together rather than as a memorized rule.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Symmetry, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
