    "id": "ug-year-1__discrete-mathematics__graph-theory__degree",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Degree",
    "introduction": "Degree studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident with it; loops count twice in standard graph theory.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident with it; loops count twice in standard graph theory.\n\nWorked Example: In a path A-B-C, vertex B has degree 2.\n\nVerification: Two edges touch B.\n\n**Key Point:** The degree of a vertex is the number of edges incident with it; loops count twice in standard graph theory.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In a path A-B-C, vertex B has degree 2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Degree counts edge touches.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The handshaking lemma says the sum of degrees equals twice the number of edges.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find degrees in a triangle graph.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
