    "introduction": "Cycles studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A cycle is a closed path with no repeated vertices except the start and end.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: A cycle is a closed path with no repeated vertices except the start and end.\n\nWorked Example: A-B-C-A is a cycle in a triangle.\n\nVerification: It starts and ends at A and uses valid edges.\n\n**Key Point:** A cycle is a closed path with no repeated vertices except the start and end.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A-B-C-A is a cycle in a triangle.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A cycle is a route returning home.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Cycles are central to graph structure and planarity.",
    "practicePrompt": "Does A-B-A count as a cycle in a simple graph? Explain.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
