    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Connectedness",
    "introduction": "Connectedness studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A graph is connected if every pair of vertices has a path between them.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: A graph is connected if every pair of vertices has a path between them.\n\nWorked Example: A chain A-B-C is connected.\n\nVerification: A can reach C through B.\n\n**Key Point:** A graph is connected if every pair of vertices has a path between them.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A chain A-B-C is connected.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Connected means every dot can be reached.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Connected components partition a graph into maximal connected subgraphs.",
    "practicePrompt": "Decide whether two separate triangles form a connected graph.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
