  {
    "sourceRow": 1123,
    "id": "ug-year-1__discrete-mathematics__graph-theory__spanning-trees",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Spanning trees",
    "introduction": "Spanning trees studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A spanning tree is a tree containing all vertices of a connected graph.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: A spanning tree is a tree containing all vertices of a connected graph.\n\nWorked Example: A square graph has a spanning tree by removing one edge.\n\nVerification: All vertices remain connected and no cycle remains.\n\n**Key Point:** A spanning tree is a tree containing all vertices of a connected graph.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A square graph has a spanning tree by removing one edge.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "It is a loop-free backbone.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Spanning trees support network design and algorithms such as Kruskal's algorithm.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find a spanning tree of a triangle graph.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
