  {
    "sourceRow": 1124,
    "id": "ug-year-1__discrete-mathematics__graph-theory__euler-trails",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Euler trails",
    "introduction": "Euler trails studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: An undirected connected graph has an Euler trail exactly when it has 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nCriterion: An undirected connected graph has an Euler trail exactly when it has 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree.\n\nWorked Example: A path of three edges has exactly two odd-degree endpoints, so it has an Euler trail.\n\nVerification: Trace each edge once from one end to the other.\n\n**Key Point:** An undirected connected graph has an Euler trail exactly when it has 0 or 2 vertices of odd degree.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A path of three edges has exactly two odd-degree endpoints, so it has an Euler trail.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Euler trails use every edge once.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Euler's theorem characterizes edge-traversal routes by parity of degrees.",
    "practicePrompt": "Check whether a square has an Euler circuit.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1125,
    "id": "ug-year-1__discrete-mathematics__graph-theory__hamiltonian-paths-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Hamiltonian paths introduction",
    "introduction": "Hamiltonian paths introduction studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once.\n\nWorked Example: In a square, A-B-C-D is Hamiltonian if those edges exist.\n\nVerification: Each vertex appears once.\n\n**Key Point:** A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In a square, A-B-C-D is Hamiltonian if those edges exist.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Hamiltonian paths visit dots, not necessarily all edges.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Hamiltonian problems are generally harder than Euler trail tests.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give a Hamiltonian path in a pentagon.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1126,
    "id": "ug-year-1__discrete-mathematics__graph-theory__planarity-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Discrete Mathematics",
    "topic": "Graph Theory",
    "subtopic": "Planarity introduction",
    "introduction": "Planarity introduction studies networks made of points and connections. It helps reason about routes, structure, and constraints.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane without edge crossings except at vertices.\n\nHow It Works: Draw the vertices and edges, then check the required path, degree, cycle, or coloring condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Graph properties depend only on connections, not on the drawing style unless planarity is involved.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\n\nCommon mistake: Judging a graph property from a messy drawing instead of its connections.\n\nCorrect idea: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\n\nDefinition: A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane without edge crossings except at vertices.\n\nWorked Example: A triangle is planar.\n\nVerification: Draw it with no crossing edges.\n\n**Key Point:** A graph is planar if it can be drawn in the plane without edge crossings except at vertices.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A triangle is planar.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use the vertices, edges, and definitions; redraw the graph if needed.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in computer networks, maps, scheduling, circuits, and algorithms.\nVisual Understanding: Represent vertices as dots and edges as lines or arrows, then trace the required structure.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Planar means drawable without crossings.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Euler's formula V-E+F=2 guides connected planar graphs.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is K4 planar? Try drawing it without crossings.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
