    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1128,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__probability-axioms",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Probability axioms",
    "introduction": "Probability axioms describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: P(A)>=0, P(S)=1, and disjoint events have additive probabilities.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nAxioms: P(A)>=0, P(S)=1, and disjoint events have additive probabilities.\n\nWorked Example: If A and B are disjoint with probabilities 0.2 and 0.3, their union has probability 0.5.\n\nVerification: 0.5 is between 0 and 1.\n\n**Key Point:** P(A)>=0, P(S)=1, and disjoint events have additive probabilities.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If A and B are disjoint with probabilities 0.2 and 0.3, their union has probability 0.5.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Axioms are the rules probability must obey.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Kolmogorov axioms define probability as a measure on events.",
    "practicePrompt": "Can a probability be 1.2? Name the axiom violated.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1129,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__conditional-probability",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Conditional probability",
    "introduction": "Conditional probability describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0.\n\nWorked Example: If P(A and B)=0.12 and P(B)=0.3, then P(A|B)=0.4.\n\nVerification: The condition has non-zero probability.\n\n**Key Point:** P(A|B)=P(A intersection B)/P(B), where P(B)>0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If P(A and B)=0.12 and P(B)=0.3, then P(A|B)=0.4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Given B means restrict the sample space to B.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Conditional probability leads to Bayes theorem and independence.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find P(A|B) if P(A and B)=1/8 and P(B)=1/2.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1130,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__independence",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Independence",
    "introduction": "Independence describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Events A and B are independent if P(A intersection B)=P(A)P(B).\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nDefinition: Events A and B are independent if P(A intersection B)=P(A)P(B).\n\nWorked Example: Two fair coin tosses are independent because P(HH)=1/4=(1/2)(1/2).\n\nVerification: Knowing one toss does not change the other.\n\n**Key Point:** Events A and B are independent if P(A intersection B)=P(A)P(B).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Two fair coin tosses are independent because P(HH)=1/4=(1/2)(1/2).\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Independent means information does not change chance.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Independence can extend to families of events with stronger conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Test independence when P(A)=0.4, P(B)=0.5, P(A and B)=0.1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1131,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__bayes-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Bayes' theorem",
    "introduction": "Bayes' theorem is a specific idea in Probability Foundations. Bayes' theorem updates a probability after new evidence using P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Bayes' theorem updates a probability after new evidence using P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B).\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Bayes' theorem. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of probability and statistics, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Probability I when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of treating a limit, estimate, or probability statement as true without checking independence, sample size, or model assumptions.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: If a test is positive, Bayes' theorem combines test accuracy with the original disease rate.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Bayes' theorem updates a probability after new evidence using P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a test is positive, Bayes' theorem combines test accuracy with the original disease rate.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Bayes' theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Probability I, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Bayes' theorem.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Bayes' theorem is about one main check: Bayes' theorem updates a probability after new evidence using P(A|B)=P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The denominator P(B) normalizes all possible causes of the evidence. Ignoring base rates gives misleading conclusions. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Probability Foundations.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Bayes' theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1132,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__discrete-random-variables",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Discrete random variables",
    "introduction": "Discrete random variables describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A discrete random variable takes countable values and assigns a probability to each value.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nDefinition: A discrete random variable takes countable values and assigns a probability to each value.\n\nWorked Example: Number of heads in two coin tosses can be 0, 1, or 2.\n\nVerification: The values form a finite list.\n\n**Key Point:** A discrete random variable takes countable values and assigns a probability to each value.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Number of heads in two coin tosses can be 0, 1, or 2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A random variable turns outcomes into numbers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Discrete random variables are measurable functions with countable range.",
    "practicePrompt": "Make a table for X=number of heads in two tosses.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1133,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__expectation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Expectation",
    "introduction": "Expectation describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: E(X)=sum x P(X=x) for a discrete random variable.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: E(X)=sum x P(X=x) for a discrete random variable.\n\nWorked Example: If X is 0 or 1 with probability 1/2 each, E(X)=1/2.\n\nVerification: The weighted average lies between 0 and 1.\n\n**Key Point:** E(X)=sum x P(X=x) for a discrete random variable.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If X is 0 or 1 with probability 1/2 each, E(X)=1/2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Expectation is long-run average value.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Expectation is a linear operator when integrability conditions hold.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find E(X) for values 1,2,3 with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1134,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__variance",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Variance",
    "introduction": "Variance describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Var(X)=E[(X-E(X))^2]=E(X^2)-[E(X)]^2.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: Var(X)=E[(X-E(X))^2]=E(X^2)-[E(X)]^2.\n\nWorked Example: For fair coin X=0,1, variance is 1/4.\n\nVerification: E(X)=1/2 and E(X^2)=1/2.\n\n**Key Point:** Var(X)=E[(X-E(X))^2]=E(X^2)-[E(X)]^2.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For fair coin X=0,1, variance is 1/4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Variance measures average squared spread.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Variance is the second central moment and is always non-negative.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find variance for X=0 and X=2 with equal probabilities.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1135,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__bernoulli",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Bernoulli",
    "introduction": "Bernoulli describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A Bernoulli random variable takes value 1 for success and 0 for failure.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nDefinition: A Bernoulli random variable takes value 1 for success and 0 for failure.\n\nWorked Example: One coin toss with success=head is Bernoulli with p=1/2.\n\nVerification: Only two values occur.\n\n**Key Point:** A Bernoulli random variable takes value 1 for success and 0 for failure.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: One coin toss with success=head is Bernoulli with p=1/2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Bernoulli means one yes-or-no trial.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Bernoulli variables are building blocks for binomial distributions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give a Bernoulli model for a pass/fail test.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1136,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__binomial",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Binomial",
    "introduction": "Binomial describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: If X counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials, P(X=k)=C(n,k)p^k(1-p)^(n-k).\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: If X counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials, P(X=k)=C(n,k)p^k(1-p)^(n-k).\n\nWorked Example: For n=3,p=1/2,k=2, probability is 3/8.\n\nVerification: There are C(3,2)=3 ways to place two successes.\n\n**Key Point:** If X counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials, P(X=k)=C(n,k)p^k(1-p)^(n-k).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For n=3,p=1/2,k=2, probability is 3/8.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Binomial counts successes in repeated yes-or-no trials.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The binomial distribution has mean np and variance np(1-p).",
    "practicePrompt": "Find probability of exactly 2 heads in 4 fair tosses.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1137,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__geometric",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Geometric",
    "introduction": "Geometric describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: For independent trials with success probability p, P(X=k)=(1-p)^(k-1)p for first success on trial k.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: For independent trials with success probability p, P(X=k)=(1-p)^(k-1)p for first success on trial k.\n\nWorked Example: If p=1/2, first success on trial 3 has probability (1/2)^2(1/2)=1/8.\n\nVerification: Two failures then one success.\n\n**Key Point:** For independent trials with success probability p, P(X=k)=(1-p)^(k-1)p for first success on trial k.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If p=1/2, first success on trial 3 has probability (1/2)^2(1/2)=1/8.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Geometric waits for the first success.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The geometric distribution has the memoryless property in discrete time.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find probability first head occurs on the 4th toss.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1138,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__poisson",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Poisson",
    "introduction": "Poisson describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: P(X=k)=e^(-lambda) lambda^k/k!, for k=0,1,2,... and lambda>0.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nFormula: P(X=k)=e^(-lambda) lambda^k/k!, for k=0,1,2,... and lambda>0.\n\nWorked Example: If lambda=2, P(X=0)=e^-2.\n\nVerification: Substitute k=0 so lambda^0/0!=1.\n\n**Key Point:** P(X=k)=e^(-lambda) lambda^k/k!, for k=0,1,2,... and lambda>0.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If lambda=2, P(X=0)=e^-2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Poisson counts rare events in a fixed interval.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Poisson can approximate binomial when n is large and p is small.",
    "practicePrompt": "For lambda=3, write P(X=2).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1139,
    "id": "ug-year-1__probability-i__probability-foundations__continuous-variables-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 1",
    "chapter": "Probability I",
    "topic": "Probability Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Continuous variables introduction",
    "introduction": "Continuous variables introduction describes uncertainty using events or random variables. It helps convert random behavior into numbers we can calculate.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A continuous random variable takes values over intervals and uses density, not point probabilities.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the sample space, event, or random variable, then apply the correct probability rule.\n\nWhy It Works: Probability rules work because events are sets and probabilities obey fixed axioms.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\n\nCommon mistake: Using a formula without checking independence, conditioning, or discreteness.\n\nCorrect idea: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\n\nDefinition: A continuous random variable takes values over intervals and uses density, not point probabilities.\n\nWorked Example: For a uniform value on [0,1], P(0.2<X<0.5)=0.3.\n\nVerification: Interval length is 0.3.\n\n**Key Point:** A continuous random variable takes values over intervals and uses density, not point probabilities.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a uniform value on [0,1], P(0.2<X<0.5)=0.3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Name the assumptions first, then choose the formula.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in data analysis, risk, games, reliability, and scientific modelling.\nVisual Understanding: Use a tree, Venn diagram, table, or area under a curve to see probabilities.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Continuous probability is area under a density curve.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Densities integrate to 1 and point probabilities are usually 0.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is P(X=0.5)=0 for a uniform continuous variable?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
