    "introduction": "Existence uniqueness introduction explains a specific idea inside Ordinary Differential Equations. It shows what the object means, how it is used, and what must be checked before solving. This helps students use Differential Equations accurately in examples and proofs.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Existence-uniqueness theorems give conditions under which an initial value problem has one local solution. How It Works: Write the given objects, check the hypotheses, then apply the definition or theorem step by step. Why It Works: The method works because the hypotheses preserve the exact mathematical structure being studied. Where We Use It: It is used in mechanics, population models, circuits, heat, and control systems. Common mistake: Solving without checking equation type or initial conditions. Correct idea: Classify the ODE first, then choose the matching method and verify by substitution. Theorem: Existence-uniqueness theorems give conditions under which an initial value problem has one local solution. Worked Example: If f and partial f/partial y are continuous near a point, y'=f(x,y) has a unique local solution there. Verification: The hypotheses control nearby slopes. **Key Point:** Existence uniqueness introduction depends on the ODE form and its hypotheses.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If f and partial f/partial y are continuous near a point, y'=f(x,y) has a unique local solution there.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the ODE first, then choose the matching method and verify by substitution.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in mechanics, population models, circuits, heat, and control systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a slope field, phase plane, or solution curve to see behavior.",
