    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A p-value is the probability, under the null hypothesis, of data at least as extreme as observed. How It Works: Write the given objects, check the hypotheses, then apply the definition or theorem step by step. Why It Works: The method works because the hypotheses preserve the exact mathematical structure being studied. Where We Use It: It is used in experiments, surveys, quality control, medicine, business, and machine learning. Common mistake: Using a formula without checking the model, sample assumptions, or interpretation. Correct idea: State the random variables, sample method, and assumptions before calculating. Definition: A p-value is the probability, under the null hypothesis, of data at least as extreme as observed. Worked Example: A p-value 0.03 means such extreme data would occur about 3% of the time if the null were true. Verification: It is not the probability the null is true. **Key Point:** p-values depends on the model and assumptions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A p-value 0.03 means such extreme data would occur about 3% of the time if the null were true.\nExample 2 - Contrast: State the random variables, sample method, and assumptions before calculating.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in experiments, surveys, quality control, medicine, business, and machine learning.\nVisual Understanding: Use tables, density curves, scatter plots, or sampling diagrams to see the idea.",
