    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: f_n converges uniformly to f if the same error bound works for all x in the domain after some N. How It Works: Write the given objects, check the hypotheses, then apply the definition or theorem step by step. Why It Works: The method works because the hypotheses preserve the exact mathematical structure being studied. Where We Use It: It is used in analysis, approximation, power series, differential equations, and numerical methods. Common mistake: Assuming pointwise convergence preserves every property. Correct idea: Use uniform convergence or a theorem with the correct hypotheses before interchanging limits. Definition: f_n converges uniformly to f if the same error bound works for all x in the domain after some N. Worked Example: f_n(x)=x/n on [0,1] converges uniformly to 0. Verification: Maximum error is 1/n. **Key Point:** Continuity under uniform convergence depends on the type of convergence and theorem hypotheses.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f_n(x)=x/n on [0,1] converges uniformly to 0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use uniform convergence or a theorem with the correct hypotheses before interchanging limits.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, approximation, power series, differential equations, and numerical methods.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine a whole graph moving toward another graph, then check whether all points get close together.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Continuity under uniform convergence studies change, closeness, or accumulated value. A graph helps you see the meaning before using symbols.",
