    "sourceRow": 1275,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__complex-plane",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Complex plane",
    "introduction": "Complex plane studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The complex plane represents z=a+bi as the point (a,b).\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: The complex plane represents z=a+bi as the point (a,b).\n\nWorked Example: z=3-2i is plotted at (3,-2).\n\nVerification: Real part is horizontal and imaginary part is vertical.\n\n**Key Point:** The complex plane represents z=a+bi as the point (a,b).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: z=3-2i is plotted at (3,-2).\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "The complex plane is the coordinate plane for complex numbers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It identifies C with R^2 while multiplication adds rotation and scaling.",
    "practicePrompt": "Plot -1+4i and state its real and imaginary parts.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1276,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__complex-differentiability",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Complex differentiability",
    "introduction": "Complex differentiability studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A complex function is differentiable at z0 if the difference quotient has the same limit from every complex direction.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: A complex function is differentiable at z0 if the difference quotient has the same limit from every complex direction.\n\nWorked Example: f(z)=z^2 has derivative 2z because [(z+h)^2-z^2]/h=2z+h.\n\nVerification: Let h approach 0 from any direction.\n\n**Key Point:** A complex function is differentiable at z0 if the difference quotient has the same limit from every complex direction.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^2 has derivative 2z because [(z+h)^2-z^2]/h=2z+h.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Complex differentiability is stricter than real differentiability.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Complex differentiability on a region leads to analyticity under standard conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use the definition to find derivative of f(z)=z^2 at z=1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1277,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__cauchy-riemann-equations",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy-Riemann equations",
    "introduction": "Cauchy-Riemann equations studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: For f=u+iv, the Cauchy-Riemann equations are u_x=v_y and u_y=-v_x, with needed differentiability conditions.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nCriterion: For f=u+iv, the Cauchy-Riemann equations are u_x=v_y and u_y=-v_x, with needed differentiability conditions.\n\nWorked Example: For f(z)=z^2, u=x^2-y^2 and v=2xy; u_x=2x=v_y and u_y=-2y=-v_x.\n\nVerification: Both equations hold.\n\n**Key Point:** For f=u+iv, the Cauchy-Riemann equations are u_x=v_y and u_y=-v_x, with needed differentiability conditions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z^2, u=x^2-y^2 and v=2xy; u_x=2x=v_y and u_y=-2y=-v_x.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "They test whether real and imaginary parts fit complex differentiability.",
    "advancedExplanation": "They are necessary, and with suitable smoothness sufficient, for analyticity.",
    "practicePrompt": "Check Cauchy-Riemann equations for f(z)=conjugate z.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1278,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__analytic-functions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Analytic functions",
    "introduction": "Analytic functions studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A function is analytic on a region if it is complex differentiable at every point of that region.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: A function is analytic on a region if it is complex differentiable at every point of that region.\n\nWorked Example: f(z)=z^3 is analytic on C.\n\nVerification: Polynomial derivative rules work everywhere.\n\n**Key Point:** A function is analytic on a region if it is complex differentiable at every point of that region.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^3 is analytic on C.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Analytic means differentiable throughout an open region.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Analytic functions have power-series expansions locally.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give an analytic function and a non-analytic function on C.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1279,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__elementary-complex-functions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Elementary complex functions",
    "introduction": "Elementary complex functions studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Elementary complex functions extend polynomials, exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions to complex inputs.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: Elementary complex functions extend polynomials, exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions to complex inputs.\n\nWorked Example: e^(i pi)=-1 is a complex exponential identity.\n\nVerification: Euler formula gives e^(i theta)=cos theta+i sin theta.\n\n**Key Point:** Elementary complex functions extend polynomials, exponential, logarithmic, and trigonometric functions to complex inputs.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: e^(i pi)=-1 is a complex exponential identity.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "They extend familiar functions into the complex plane.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Branches matter for multi-valued functions such as logarithm and powers.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why complex logarithm needs a branch choice.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1280,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-functions__conformal-maps-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Functions",
    "subtopic": "Conformal maps introduction",
    "introduction": "Conformal maps introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A conformal map preserves angles locally where it is analytic and has non-zero derivative.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: A conformal map preserves angles locally where it is analytic and has non-zero derivative.\n\nWorked Example: f(z)=z^2 is conformal away from z=0.\n\nVerification: Derivative 2z is non-zero when z is not 0.\n\n**Key Point:** A conformal map preserves angles locally where it is analytic and has non-zero derivative.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=z^2 is conformal away from z=0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Conformal maps bend shapes while keeping tiny angles.",
    "advancedExplanation": "They connect complex analysis with geometry and harmonic functions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is f(z)=z^2 not conformal at z=0?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1281,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__contours",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Contours",
    "introduction": "Contours studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A contour is a directed curve used as a path for complex integration.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: A contour is a directed curve used as a path for complex integration.\n\nWorked Example: The unit circle contour can be written z(t)=e^(it), 0<=t<=2pi.\n\nVerification: It starts and ends at 1 and moves counterclockwise.\n\n**Key Point:** A contour is a directed curve used as a path for complex integration.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: The unit circle contour can be written z(t)=e^(it), 0<=t<=2pi.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A contour is a road for integrating complex functions.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Contour choice controls residues, winding, and integral values.",
    "practicePrompt": "Parametrize the circle |z|=2 counterclockwise.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1282,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__cauchy-s-integral-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy's Integral Theorem",
    "introduction": "Cauchy's Integral Theorem is a specific idea in Complex Integration. Cauchy's Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Cauchy's Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Cauchy's Integral Theorem. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of analysis, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Complex Analysis when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using an integral, limit, or operator theorem without checking continuity, convergence, boundedness, or domain conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For f(z)=z on the unit circle, the contour integral is zero because f is entire.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Cauchy's Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z on the unit circle, the contour integral is zero because f is entire.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Cauchy's Integral Theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Complex Analysis, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Cauchy's Integral Theorem.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Cauchy's Integral Theorem is about one main check: Cauchy's Integral Theorem says the integral of a holomorphic function around a suitable closed contour is zero. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The theorem depends on holomorphicity on a region containing the contour and its interior. It leads to Cauchy's integral formula and residue theory. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Complex Integration.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Cauchy's Integral Theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1283,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__cauchy-integral-formula",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Cauchy integral formula",
    "introduction": "Cauchy integral formula studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: If f is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour C, then f(a)=1/(2pi i) integral_C f(z)/(z-a) dz.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nTheorem: If f is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour C, then f(a)=1/(2pi i) integral_C f(z)/(z-a) dz.\n\nWorked Example: Integral of 1/(z-0) around unit circle equals 2pi i.\n\nVerification: The formula gives f(0)=1 for f(z)=1.\n\n**Key Point:** If f is analytic inside and on a simple closed contour C, then f(a)=1/(2pi i) integral_C f(z)/(z-a) dz.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Integral of 1/(z-0) around unit circle equals 2pi i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A boundary integral can recover an inside value.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The formula implies analytic functions are infinitely differentiable.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use Cauchy integral formula to evaluate integral_C 1/(z-2) dz when 2 is inside C.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1284,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__taylor-series",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Taylor series",
    "introduction": "Taylor series studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: An analytic function can be represented locally by a power series about a point.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nTheorem: An analytic function can be represented locally by a power series about a point.\n\nWorked Example: e^z=1+z+z^2/2!+... around 0.\n\nVerification: Differentiating term by term recovers e^z.\n\n**Key Point:** An analytic function can be represented locally by a power series about a point.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: e^z=1+z+z^2/2!+... around 0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Taylor series is a local polynomial-like expansion.",
    "advancedExplanation": "In complex analysis, analyticity is equivalent to local power-series representation.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the first four terms of sin z about 0.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1285,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__laurent-series",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Laurent series",
    "introduction": "Laurent series studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A Laurent series allows negative powers and represents functions near isolated singularities.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinition: A Laurent series allows negative powers and represents functions near isolated singularities.\n\nWorked Example: 1/(z(z-1)) has negative powers around z=0 in a punctured region.\n\nVerification: The negative-power part signals a singularity.\n\n**Key Point:** A Laurent series allows negative powers and represents functions near isolated singularities.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 1/(z(z-1)) has negative powers around z=0 in a punctured region.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Laurent series is Taylor series with possible pole terms.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The coefficient of (z-a)^(-1) is the residue.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the Laurent series idea for 1/z near 0.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1286,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__zeros-and-poles",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Zeros and poles",
    "introduction": "Zeros and poles studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A zero is where f(a)=0; a pole is an isolated singularity where f grows like a negative power.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nDefinitions: A zero is where f(a)=0; a pole is an isolated singularity where f grows like a negative power.\n\nWorked Example: f(z)=(z-2)^3 has a zero of order 3 at z=2.\n\nVerification: The factor (z-2) appears three times.\n\n**Key Point:** A zero is where f(a)=0; a pole is an isolated singularity where f grows like a negative power.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(z)=(z-2)^3 has a zero of order 3 at z=2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Zeros are roots; poles are controlled blow-ups.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Orders of zeros and poles drive residue and argument-principle calculations.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the order of the pole of 1/(z-1)^4.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1287,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__residue-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Residue theorem",
    "introduction": "Residue theorem studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: For isolated singularities inside C, integral_C f(z) dz = 2pi i times the sum of residues.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nTheorem: For isolated singularities inside C, integral_C f(z) dz = 2pi i times the sum of residues.\n\nWorked Example: Integral of 1/z around unit circle is 2pi i.\n\nVerification: Residue at 0 is 1.\n\n**Key Point:** For isolated singularities inside C, integral_C f(z) dz = 2pi i times the sum of residues.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Integral of 1/z around unit circle is 2pi i.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Residues are the pieces that survive contour integration.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Residue methods evaluate real integrals and count zeros.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use residue theorem for integral of 3/z around the unit circle.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1288,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__argument-principle-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Argument principle introduction",
    "introduction": "Argument principle introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The argument principle relates change in argument around a contour to zeros minus poles inside.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nTheorem: The argument principle relates change in argument around a contour to zeros minus poles inside.\n\nWorked Example: For f(z)=z^2 around unit circle, winding doubles, so zeros counted with multiplicity are 2.\n\nVerification: z^2 has a double zero at 0.\n\n**Key Point:** The argument principle relates change in argument around a contour to zeros minus poles inside.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(z)=z^2 around unit circle, winding doubles, so zeros counted with multiplicity are 2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Watch how f(C) winds around 0.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is a bridge between complex integration and root counting.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain what zeros minus poles means in the argument principle.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1289,
    "id": "ug-year-3__complex-analysis__complex-integration__rouche-theorem-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Complex Analysis",
    "topic": "Complex Integration",
    "subtopic": "Rouche theorem introduction",
    "introduction": "Rouche theorem introduction studies complex numbers as points, functions, or integrals. It reveals strong links between algebra, geometry, and calculus.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: If |g|<|f| on a contour, then f and f+g have the same number of zeros inside.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the complex variable, domain, contour, or singularity, then check the theorem's conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Complex methods work because analyticity strongly controls local and global behavior.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\n\nCommon mistake: Applying real-variable intuition without checking complex differentiability or singularities.\n\nCorrect idea: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\n\nTheorem: If |g|<|f| on a contour, then f and f+g have the same number of zeros inside.\n\nWorked Example: z^3 and z^3+0.1 have the same number of zeros inside a large enough unit contour when |0.1|<|z^3| on it.\n\nVerification: On |z|=1, |z^3|=1>0.1.\n\n**Key Point:** If |g|<|f| on a contour, then f and f+g have the same number of zeros inside.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: z^3 and z^3+0.1 have the same number of zeros inside a large enough unit contour when |0.1|<|z^3| on it.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check analyticity, contour location, and branch choices before using complex theorems.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in fluid flow, signal processing, geometry, number theory, and mathematical physics.\nVisual Understanding: Picture points on the complex plane and watch how functions move or wind them.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A smaller boundary disturbance does not change the zero count.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Rouche theorem is a powerful root-counting tool.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use Rouche idea to compare z^4 and z^4+1/2 on |z|=1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1290,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__group-actions",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Group actions",
    "introduction": "Group actions studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A group action assigns each group element a bijection of a set, compatibly with the group operation.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A group action assigns each group element a bijection of a set, compatibly with the group operation.\n\nWorked Example: Rotations of a square act on its four vertices.\n\nVerification: Composing rotations matches group multiplication.\n\n**Key Point:** A group action assigns each group element a bijection of a set, compatibly with the group operation.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Rotations of a square act on its four vertices.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A group action is a group moving a set.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Actions convert group structure into symmetry of objects.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give an action of Z on integers by n.k=k+n.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1291,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__orbit-stabilizer",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Orbit-stabilizer",
    "introduction": "Orbit-stabilizer studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: For a finite group action, |G| = |Orbit(x)| |Stabilizer(x)|.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nTheorem: For a finite group action, |G| = |Orbit(x)| |Stabilizer(x)|.\n\nWorked Example: A square's rotation group has 4 elements; a vertex orbit has 4 points and stabilizer size 1.\n\nVerification: 4=4x1.\n\n**Key Point:** For a finite group action, |G| = |Orbit(x)| |Stabilizer(x)|.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A square's rotation group has 4 elements; a vertex orbit has 4 points and stabilizer size 1.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Orbit counts where a point can go; stabilizer counts what fixes it.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Orbit-stabilizer organizes counting in group actions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find orbit and stabilizer of a vertex under rotations of a triangle.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1292,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__class-equation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Class equation",
    "introduction": "Class equation studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The class equation partitions a finite group into conjugacy classes, separating central elements.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nTheorem: The class equation partitions a finite group into conjugacy classes, separating central elements.\n\nWorked Example: In an abelian group, every conjugacy class has one element.\n\nVerification: gxg^-1=x for all g.\n\n**Key Point:** The class equation partitions a finite group into conjugacy classes, separating central elements.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In an abelian group, every conjugacy class has one element.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Class equation counts elements by conjugacy behavior.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is used to prove results about p-groups and centers.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why do abelian groups have singleton conjugacy classes?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1293,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__sylow-theorems",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Sylow theorems",
    "introduction": "Sylow theorems studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Sylow theorems describe subgroups of order p^k inside finite groups.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nTheorems: Sylow theorems describe subgroups of order p^k inside finite groups.\n\nWorked Example: A group of order 12 has Sylow 3-subgroups of order 3.\n\nVerification: 3 is the highest power of 3 dividing 12.\n\n**Key Point:** Sylow theorems describe subgroups of order p^k inside finite groups.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A group of order 12 has Sylow 3-subgroups of order 3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Sylow subgroups capture prime-power parts of a group.",
    "advancedExplanation": "They are central tools for classifying finite groups.",
    "practicePrompt": "State possible numbers of Sylow 3-subgroups in a group of order 12.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1294,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__direct-products",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Direct products",
    "introduction": "Direct products studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The direct product G x H combines two groups component by component.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: The direct product G x H combines two groups component by component.\n\nWorked Example: Z2 x Z3 has operation (a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d) modulo each part.\n\nVerification: Each coordinate follows its own group law.\n\n**Key Point:** The direct product G x H combines two groups component by component.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z2 x Z3 has operation (a,b)+(c,d)=(a+c,b+d) modulo each part.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Direct product runs two groups side by side.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Direct products build larger groups from smaller factors.",
    "practicePrompt": "List all elements of Z2 x Z2.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1295,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__advanced-groups__finite-abelian-groups-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Advanced Groups",
    "subtopic": "Finite abelian groups introduction",
    "introduction": "Finite abelian groups introduction studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Every finite abelian group decomposes into a product of cyclic groups of prime-power order.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nTheorem: Every finite abelian group decomposes into a product of cyclic groups of prime-power order.\n\nWorked Example: Z6 is isomorphic to Z2 x Z3 because 2 and 3 are coprime.\n\nVerification: Both groups have matching cyclic structure.\n\n**Key Point:** Every finite abelian group decomposes into a product of cyclic groups of prime-power order.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z6 is isomorphic to Z2 x Z3 because 2 and 3 are coprime.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Finite abelian groups are made from cyclic building blocks.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This is a classification theorem for finite abelian groups.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why Z4 and Z2 x Z2 are not isomorphic.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1296,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Ideals",
    "introduction": "Ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: An ideal is an additive subgroup that absorbs multiplication by ring elements.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: An ideal is an additive subgroup that absorbs multiplication by ring elements.\n\nWorked Example: 2Z is an ideal of Z.\n\nVerification: Any integer times an even integer is even.\n\n**Key Point:** An ideal is an additive subgroup that absorbs multiplication by ring elements.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 2Z is an ideal of Z.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "An ideal is a ring subset closed under outside multiplication.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Ideals are kernels of ring homomorphisms and define quotients.",
    "practicePrompt": "Show that 5Z is an ideal of Z.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1297,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__quotient-rings",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Quotient rings",
    "introduction": "Quotient rings studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A quotient ring R/I is made from cosets of an ideal I, with operations defined by cosets.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A quotient ring R/I is made from cosets of an ideal I, with operations defined by cosets.\n\nWorked Example: Z/5Z has residue classes 0,1,2,3,4.\n\nVerification: Adding classes modulo 5 is well-defined.\n\n**Key Point:** A quotient ring R/I is made from cosets of an ideal I, with operations defined by cosets.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z/5Z has residue classes 0,1,2,3,4.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A quotient ring treats ideal elements as zero.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Quotient rings are fundamental in algebraic geometry and number theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "In Z/6Z, explain why [2]+[5]=[1] and why answers depend only on remainders.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1298,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__prime-ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Prime ideals",
    "introduction": "Prime ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A proper ideal P is prime if ab in P implies a in P or b in P.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A proper ideal P is prime if ab in P implies a in P or b in P.\n\nWorked Example: In Z, 5Z is prime because 5 prime divides ab only when it divides a or b.\n\nVerification: Euclid's lemma verifies it.\n\n**Key Point:** A proper ideal P is prime if ab in P implies a in P or b in P.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In Z, 5Z is prime because 5 prime divides ab only when it divides a or b.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Prime ideals generalize prime numbers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "R/P is an integral domain exactly when P is prime.",
    "practicePrompt": "Is 6Z a prime ideal of Z? Give a counterexample.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1299,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__maximal-ideals",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Maximal ideals",
    "introduction": "Maximal ideals studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A proper ideal M is maximal if no proper ideal lies strictly between M and the whole ring.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A proper ideal M is maximal if no proper ideal lies strictly between M and the whole ring.\n\nWorked Example: In Z, pZ is maximal when p is prime.\n\nVerification: Z/pZ is a field.\n\n**Key Point:** A proper ideal M is maximal if no proper ideal lies strictly between M and the whole ring.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In Z, pZ is maximal when p is prime.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Maximal ideals are largest proper ideals.",
    "advancedExplanation": "R/M is a field exactly when M is maximal in a commutative ring with 1.",
    "practicePrompt": "In Z, why is (5) maximal but (6) not maximal? Explain using quotient rings.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1300,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__euclidean-domains",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Euclidean domains",
    "introduction": "Euclidean domains studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A Euclidean domain has a division algorithm controlled by a size function.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A Euclidean domain has a division algorithm controlled by a size function.\n\nWorked Example: Z is Euclidean using absolute value.\n\nVerification: a=bq+r with |r|<|b|.\n\n**Key Point:** A Euclidean domain has a division algorithm controlled by a size function.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z is Euclidean using absolute value.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Euclidean domains allow gcd computations like integers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Every Euclidean domain is a PID and a UFD.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use Euclidean algorithm in Z to find gcd(30,12).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1301,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__pids",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "PIDs",
    "introduction": "PIDs studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A principal ideal domain is an integral domain where every ideal is generated by one element.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A principal ideal domain is an integral domain where every ideal is generated by one element.\n\nWorked Example: Z is a PID because every ideal is nZ.\n\nVerification: n is the gcd of all elements in the ideal.\n\n**Key Point:** A principal ideal domain is an integral domain where every ideal is generated by one element.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z is a PID because every ideal is nZ.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "PID means every ideal has one generator.",
    "advancedExplanation": "PIDs connect ideal theory with factorization.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give the generator of the ideal 12Z in Z.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1302,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__ufds",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "UFDs",
    "introduction": "UFDs studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A unique factorization domain has factorization into irreducibles uniquely up to units and order.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A unique factorization domain has factorization into irreducibles uniquely up to units and order.\n\nWorked Example: Z is a UFD by the fundamental theorem of arithmetic.\n\nVerification: 84=2^2 x 3 x 7 uniquely up to order.\n\n**Key Point:** A unique factorization domain has factorization into irreducibles uniquely up to units and order.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z is a UFD by the fundamental theorem of arithmetic.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "UFD means primes factor numbers reliably.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Every PID is a UFD, but not every UFD is a PID.",
    "practicePrompt": "Factor 180 into primes and state the uniqueness idea.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1303,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__polynomial-factorisation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Polynomial factorisation",
    "introduction": "Polynomial factorisation studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Polynomial factorisation writes a polynomial as a product of simpler polynomials over a chosen field or ring.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: Polynomial factorisation writes a polynomial as a product of simpler polynomials over a chosen field or ring.\n\nWorked Example: x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1) over R.\n\nVerification: Multiplying returns x^2-1.\n\n**Key Point:** Polynomial factorisation writes a polynomial as a product of simpler polynomials over a chosen field or ring.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1) over R.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Factorisation depends on the coefficient field.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Irreducibility changes when the base field changes.",
    "practicePrompt": "Factor x^2+1 over R and over C.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1304,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__field-extensions-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Field extensions introduction",
    "introduction": "Field extensions introduction studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A field extension K/F is a larger field K containing a smaller field F.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A field extension K/F is a larger field K containing a smaller field F.\n\nWorked Example: C is a field extension of R.\n\nVerification: Every real number is complex with imaginary part 0.\n\n**Key Point:** A field extension K/F is a larger field K containing a smaller field F.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: C is a field extension of R.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Extensions add new numbers while keeping field rules.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Field extensions organize roots of polynomials and Galois theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why Q(sqrt(2)) is an extension of Q.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1305,
    "id": "ug-year-3__abstract-algebra-ii__rings-and-fields__finite-fields-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Abstract Algebra II",
    "topic": "Rings and Fields",
    "subtopic": "Finite fields introduction",
    "introduction": "Finite fields introduction studies algebraic structure through operations and exact axioms. It helps classify symmetry, arithmetic, and polynomial behavior.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A finite field is a field with finitely many elements; its size is always a prime power.\n\nHow It Works: Write the operation or ring data, then check closure, identity, inverse, ideal, or factorization conditions.\n\nWhy It Works: Algebraic theorems work because operations preserve structure under maps, products, or quotients.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\n\nCommon mistake: Assuming familiar integer rules hold in every algebraic structure.\n\nCorrect idea: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\n\nDefinition: A finite field is a field with finitely many elements; its size is always a prime power.\n\nWorked Example: Z/5Z is a finite field with 5 elements.\n\nVerification: Every non-zero residue modulo 5 has an inverse.\n\n**Key Point:** A finite field is a field with finitely many elements; its size is always a prime power.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Z/5Z is a finite field with 5 elements.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Check the axioms and the chosen structure before using a rule.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in symmetry, number theory, coding, cryptography, and higher algebra.\nVisual Understanding: Use operation tables, symmetry moves, quotient classes, or factor trees.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Finite fields are arithmetic systems with limited elements.",
    "advancedExplanation": "They are vital in coding theory and cryptography.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find the inverse of 2 in Z/5Z.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1306,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__topological-spaces",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Topological spaces",
    "introduction": "Topological spaces is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A topological space is a set with a collection of open sets closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A topological space is a set with a collection of open sets closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.\n\nWorked Example: R with usual open intervals generating open sets is a topological space.\n\nVerification: Unions and finite intersections of open sets stay open.\n\n**Key Point:** A topological space is a set with a collection of open sets closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: R with usual open intervals generating open sets is a topological space.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Topology studies closeness without measuring distance.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Topological spaces generalize metric spaces.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give the discrete topology on {a,b}.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1307,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__bases",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Bases",
    "introduction": "Bases is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A basis is a collection of open sets whose unions create all open sets of a topology.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A basis is a collection of open sets whose unions create all open sets of a topology.\n\nWorked Example: Open intervals form a basis for the usual topology on R.\n\nVerification: Every usual open set is a union of intervals.\n\n**Key Point:** A basis is a collection of open sets whose unions create all open sets of a topology.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Open intervals form a basis for the usual topology on R.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A basis is a small open-set toolkit.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Bases let us define topologies efficiently.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why do open intervals form a basis on R?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1308,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__subbases",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Subbases",
    "introduction": "Subbases is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A subbasis is a collection whose finite intersections form a basis.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A subbasis is a collection whose finite intersections form a basis.\n\nWorked Example: Open rays can form a subbasis for the usual topology on R.\n\nVerification: Intersections of rays create intervals.\n\n**Key Point:** A subbasis is a collection whose finite intersections form a basis.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Open rays can form a subbasis for the usual topology on R.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "A subbasis is an even smaller toolkit.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Subbases define product topologies and weak topologies.",
    "practicePrompt": "How can rays generate open intervals?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1309,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__continuous-maps",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Continuous maps",
    "introduction": "Continuous maps is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A map is continuous if the preimage of every open set is open.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A map is continuous if the preimage of every open set is open.\n\nWorked Example: f(x)=x^2 is continuous on R because preimages of open sets are open.\n\nVerification: This matches usual calculus continuity.\n\n**Key Point:** A map is continuous if the preimage of every open set is open.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: f(x)=x^2 is continuous on R because preimages of open sets are open.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Continuity preserves open-set structure backward.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Topological continuity works without distances.",
    "practicePrompt": "Check continuity of a constant map using preimages.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1310,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__product-topology",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Product topology",
    "introduction": "Product topology is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Product topology is generated by products of open sets from each factor.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: Product topology is generated by products of open sets from each factor.\n\nWorked Example: Open rectangles form a basis for R x R.\n\nVerification: Unions of open rectangles make open regions.\n\n**Key Point:** Product topology is generated by products of open sets from each factor.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Open rectangles form a basis for R x R.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Product topology combines spaces coordinate by coordinate.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It supports function spaces and multi-variable topology.",
    "practicePrompt": "Describe a basic open set in R x R.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1311,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__quotient-topology",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Quotient topology",
    "introduction": "Quotient topology is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Quotient topology makes a surjective map continuous and identifies points according to an equivalence relation.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: Quotient topology makes a surjective map continuous and identifies points according to an equivalence relation.\n\nWorked Example: Gluing endpoints of [0,1] gives a circle-like quotient.\n\nVerification: The endpoints are treated as one point.\n\n**Key Point:** Quotient topology makes a surjective map continuous and identifies points according to an equivalence relation.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Gluing endpoints of [0,1] gives a circle-like quotient.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Quotient topology formalizes gluing.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is central in constructing new spaces.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is quotient topology useful for gluing spaces?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1312,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__separation-axioms",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Separation axioms",
    "introduction": "Separation axioms is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Separation axioms describe how well points and closed sets can be separated by open sets.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: Separation axioms describe how well points and closed sets can be separated by open sets.\n\nWorked Example: Hausdorff spaces separate two distinct points by disjoint open neighborhoods.\n\nVerification: R is Hausdorff with usual topology.\n\n**Key Point:** Separation axioms describe how well points and closed sets can be separated by open sets.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Hausdorff spaces separate two distinct points by disjoint open neighborhoods.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Separation controls how distinct points behave.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Hausdorffness gives uniqueness of limits.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why uniqueness of limits can fail without Hausdorff condition.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1313,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__compactness",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Compactness",
    "introduction": "Compactness is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A space is compact if every open cover has a finite subcover.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A space is compact if every open cover has a finite subcover.\n\nWorked Example: [0,1] is compact in R by Heine-Borel.\n\nVerification: It is closed and bounded in R.\n\n**Key Point:** A space is compact if every open cover has a finite subcover.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: [0,1] is compact in R by Heine-Borel.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Compactness means finitely controllable covering.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Compactness replaces finiteness in many theorems.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is (0,1) not compact in R?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1314,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__connectedness",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Connectedness",
    "introduction": "Connectedness is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A space is connected if it cannot be split into two separated non-empty open pieces.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A space is connected if it cannot be split into two separated non-empty open pieces.\n\nWorked Example: [0,1] is connected.\n\nVerification: There is no gap that separates it into two open parts.\n\n**Key Point:** A space is connected if it cannot be split into two separated non-empty open pieces.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: [0,1] is connected.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Connected means one piece topologically.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Continuous images of connected sets are connected.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is [0,1] union [2,3] disconnected?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1315,
    "id": "ug-year-3__topology-i__general-topology__path-connectedness",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Topology I",
    "topic": "General Topology",
    "subtopic": "Path connectedness",
    "introduction": "Path connectedness is a general-topology idea. It studies space using open sets, continuity, and shape properties.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A space is path connected if any two points can be joined by a continuous path.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the underlying set and open sets, then check the required topological condition.\n\nWhy It Works: Topology works because open-set rules preserve closeness-like behavior without needing distances.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\n\nCommon mistake: Using distance intuition when the topology may not come from a metric.\n\nCorrect idea: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\n\nDefinition: A space is path connected if any two points can be joined by a continuous path.\n\nWorked Example: R^2 is path connected using straight-line paths.\n\nVerification: Line segment between two points stays in R^2.\n\n**Key Point:** A space is path connected if any two points can be joined by a continuous path.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: R^2 is path connected using straight-line paths.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Use open sets and the exact definition instead of relying only on pictures.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in analysis, geometry, manifolds, functional analysis, and modern mathematics.\nVisual Understanding: Imagine points surrounded by open neighborhoods and watch how sets cover or connect.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Path connected means travel is possible inside the space.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Path connectedness implies connectedness, but not conversely in all spaces.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give a path between (0,0) and (1,2) in R^2.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1316,
    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__floating-point-arithmetic",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Floating point arithmetic",
    "introduction": "Floating point arithmetic is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Floating point arithmetic represents real numbers approximately using finite precision.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nPrinciple: Floating point arithmetic represents real numbers approximately using finite precision.\n\nWorked Example: 0.1 may not be stored exactly in binary floating point.\n\nVerification: Finite bits cannot represent every real decimal exactly.\n\n**Key Point:** Floating point arithmetic represents real numbers approximately using finite precision.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: 0.1 may not be stored exactly in binary floating point.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Floating point is rounded computer arithmetic.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Error bounds and stability are central in numerical computing.",
    "practicePrompt": "Explain why 0.1+0.2 may not equal exactly 0.3 in a computer.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1317,
