    "subtopic": "Error analysis",
    "introduction": "Error analysis is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Error analysis studies the difference between exact and computed values.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nDefinition: Error analysis studies the difference between exact and computed values.\n\nWorked Example: If exact value is 10 and approximation is 9.8, absolute error is 0.2.\n\nVerification: |10-9.8|=0.2.\n\n**Key Point:** Error analysis studies the difference between exact and computed values.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If exact value is 10 and approximation is 9.8, absolute error is 0.2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Error analysis measures trust in answers.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Forward and backward error explain algorithm reliability.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find absolute and relative error for approximation 49 to exact 50.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
