    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__bisection",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Bisection",
    "introduction": "Bisection is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Bisection repeatedly halves an interval where a continuous function changes sign.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nAlgorithm: Bisection repeatedly halves an interval where a continuous function changes sign.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2-2 on [1,2], midpoint 1.5 gives positive value, so keep [1,1.5].\n\nVerification: The sign change remains in the kept interval.\n\n**Key Point:** Bisection repeatedly halves an interval where a continuous function changes sign.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2-2 on [1,2], midpoint 1.5 gives positive value, so keep [1,1.5].\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Bisection traps a root by halving.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is slow but guaranteed under continuity and sign change.",
    "practicePrompt": "Do one bisection step for x^2-3 on [1,2].",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1319,
    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__newton-method",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Newton method",
    "introduction": "Newton method is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Newton method uses tangent lines to approximate roots: x_(n+1)=x_n-f(x_n)/f'(x_n).\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nAlgorithm: Newton method uses tangent lines to approximate roots: x_(n+1)=x_n-f(x_n)/f'(x_n).\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2-2 and x0=1.5, x1=1.5-0.25/3=1.4167 approximately.\n\nVerification: Substitution gives a value closer to sqrt(2).\n\n**Key Point:** Newton method uses tangent lines to approximate roots: x_(n+1)=x_n-f(x_n)/f'(x_n).",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2-2 and x0=1.5, x1=1.5-0.25/3=1.4167 approximately.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Newton follows tangent intersections.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It can converge fast but may fail if derivative is zero or start is poor.",
    "practicePrompt": "Do one Newton step for f(x)=x^2-5 from x0=2.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1320,
    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__secant-method",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Secant method",
    "introduction": "Secant method is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Secant method approximates Newton method using two previous points instead of the derivative.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nAlgorithm: Secant method approximates Newton method using two previous points instead of the derivative.\n\nWorked Example: Use the line through (x0,f(x0)) and (x1,f(x1)) to estimate the root.\n\nVerification: The secant line approximates the tangent line.\n\n**Key Point:** Secant method approximates Newton method using two previous points instead of the derivative.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Use the line through (x0,f(x0)) and (x1,f(x1)) to estimate the root.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Secant avoids computing derivatives.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It has superlinear convergence under suitable conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Write the secant update using two starting values.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
