    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__newton-divided-differences",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Newton divided differences",
    "introduction": "Newton divided differences is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Newton divided differences build an interpolation polynomial using nested coefficients.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nMethod: Newton divided differences build an interpolation polynomial using nested coefficients.\n\nWorked Example: For two points, divided difference is (f(x1)-f(x0))/(x1-x0).\n\nVerification: It equals the slope of the interpolating line.\n\n**Key Point:** Newton divided differences build an interpolation polynomial using nested coefficients.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For two points, divided difference is (f(x1)-f(x0))/(x1-x0).\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Divided differences update interpolation efficiently.",
    "advancedExplanation": "They support adding new nodes without rebuilding everything.",
    "practicePrompt": "Find divided difference for (1,4),(3,10).",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1324,
    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__numerical-differentiation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Numerical differentiation",
    "introduction": "Numerical differentiation is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Numerical differentiation estimates derivatives from nearby function values.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nDefinition: Numerical differentiation estimates derivatives from nearby function values.\n\nWorked Example: Forward difference f'(x) approximately [f(x+h)-f(x)]/h.\n\nVerification: It is the average rate over a small interval.\n\n**Key Point:** Numerical differentiation estimates derivatives from nearby function values.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Forward difference f'(x) approximately [f(x+h)-f(x)]/h.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "It estimates slope from sampled data.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Roundoff and truncation errors must be balanced.",
    "practicePrompt": "Approximate derivative of x^2 at x=2 using h=0.1.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
