    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__trapezoidal-rule",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Trapezoidal rule",
    "introduction": "Trapezoidal rule is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Trapezoidal rule estimates area by trapezoids: h/2[f(a)+f(b)] for one interval.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nFormula: Trapezoidal rule estimates area by trapezoids: h/2[f(a)+f(b)] for one interval.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x on [0,2], estimate is 1/2 x 2 x (0+2)=2.\n\nVerification: Exact triangle area is also 2.\n\n**Key Point:** Trapezoidal rule estimates area by trapezoids: h/2[f(a)+f(b)] for one interval.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x on [0,2], estimate is 1/2 x 2 x (0+2)=2.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "It replaces a curve by a straight top.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Composite trapezoidal error depends on second derivative behavior.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use trapezoidal rule for f(x)=x^2 on [0,1].",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1326,
    "id": "ug-year-3__numerical-analysis__numerical-computation__simpson-rule",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Numerical Analysis",
    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Simpson rule",
    "introduction": "Simpson rule is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Simpson rule approximates area using a parabola: (h/3)[f(a)+4f(m)+f(b)] with two equal subintervals.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nFormula: Simpson rule approximates area using a parabola: (h/3)[f(a)+4f(m)+f(b)] with two equal subintervals.\n\nWorked Example: For f(x)=x^2 on [0,2], h=1 gives (1/3)(0+4+4)=8/3.\n\nVerification: Exact integral is 8/3.\n\n**Key Point:** Simpson rule approximates area using a parabola: (h/3)[f(a)+4f(m)+f(b)] with two equal subintervals.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For f(x)=x^2 on [0,2], h=1 gives (1/3)(0+4+4)=8/3.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Simpson fits a curved top, not a straight one.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is exact for polynomials up to degree 3.",
    "practicePrompt": "Use Simpson rule for f(x)=x^2 on [0,2].",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
