    "topic": "Numerical Computation",
    "subtopic": "Runge-Kutta methods",
    "introduction": "Runge-Kutta methods is a numerical-analysis method. It helps compute approximate answers when exact formulas are hard or impossible.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Runge-Kutta methods use several slope estimates per step to solve ODEs more accurately.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the input data, choose the algorithm, compute carefully, and estimate or check error.\n\nWhy It Works: Numerical methods work by replacing hard exact objects with controlled approximations.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\n\nCommon mistake: Trusting a decimal answer without checking error or convergence conditions.\n\nCorrect idea: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\n\nDefinition: Runge-Kutta methods use several slope estimates per step to solve ODEs more accurately.\n\nWorked Example: RK4 combines four slopes in one weighted average.\n\nVerification: Multiple slopes better estimate curve bending.\n\n**Key Point:** Runge-Kutta methods use several slope estimates per step to solve ODEs more accurately.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: RK4 combines four slopes in one weighted average.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Estimate error, check assumptions, and compare with known behavior when possible.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in engineering, finance, physics simulation, data science, and scientific computing.\nVisual Understanding: Use intervals, tangent lines, tables, or area panels to see the approximation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Runge-Kutta samples slopes inside the step.",
    "advancedExplanation": "RK methods balance accuracy and cost in numerical ODEs.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why can RK4 be more accurate than Euler's method?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1332,
    "id": "ug-year-3__partial-differential-equations__pde-foundations__classification-second-order-pdes",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Partial Differential Equations",
    "topic": "PDE Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Classification second-order PDEs",
    "introduction": "Classification second-order PDEs is a partial-differential-equation idea. It studies functions depending on several variables and their partial derivatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: For A u_xx + B u_xy + C u_yy + ...=0, classify by B^2-4AC: elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the PDE type, variables, derivatives, and boundary or initial data before solving.\n\nWhy It Works: PDE methods work because derivative structure controls propagation, diffusion, or equilibrium.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing boundary conditions and initial conditions or using the wrong PDE type.\n\nCorrect idea: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\n\nCriterion: For A u_xx + B u_xy + C u_yy + ...=0, classify by B^2-4AC: elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic.\n\nWorked Example: u_xx+u_yy=0 has A=1,B=0,C=1, so B^2-4AC=-4; it is elliptic.\n\nVerification: The discriminant is negative.\n\n**Key Point:** For A u_xx + B u_xy + C u_yy + ...=0, classify by B^2-4AC: elliptic, parabolic, or hyperbolic.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: u_xx+u_yy=0 has A=1,B=0,C=1, so B^2-4AC=-4; it is elliptic.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\nVisual Understanding: Picture a surface changing over space and time, with edges or starting shape fixed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Classification tells equation type.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Classification guides suitable boundary or initial conditions.",
    "practicePrompt": "Classify u_xx+2u_xy+u_yy=0 using B^2-AC. What type is it, and why?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
