    "id": "ug-year-3__partial-differential-equations__pde-foundations__wave-equation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Partial Differential Equations",
    "topic": "PDE Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Wave equation",
    "introduction": "Wave equation is a partial-differential-equation idea. It studies functions depending on several variables and their partial derivatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The wave equation u_tt=c^2 u_xx models vibrating strings and waves.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the PDE type, variables, derivatives, and boundary or initial data before solving.\n\nWhy It Works: PDE methods work because derivative structure controls propagation, diffusion, or equilibrium.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing boundary conditions and initial conditions or using the wrong PDE type.\n\nCorrect idea: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\n\nEquation: The wave equation u_tt=c^2 u_xx models vibrating strings and waves.\n\nWorked Example: u(x,t)=sin(x-ct) solves the one-dimensional wave equation.\n\nVerification: Second derivatives satisfy u_tt=c^2u_xx.\n\n**Key Point:** The wave equation u_tt=c^2 u_xx models vibrating strings and waves.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: u(x,t)=sin(x-ct) solves the one-dimensional wave equation.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\nVisual Understanding: Picture a surface changing over space and time, with edges or starting shape fixed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Wave equation transports oscillations.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is hyperbolic and has finite propagation speed.",
    "practicePrompt": "Check whether sin(x-ct) solves u_tt=c^2u_xx.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
