    "sourceRow": 1336,
    "id": "ug-year-3__partial-differential-equations__pde-foundations__laplace-equation",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Partial Differential Equations",
    "topic": "PDE Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Laplace equation",
    "introduction": "Laplace equation is a partial-differential-equation idea. It studies functions depending on several variables and their partial derivatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Laplace equation is Delta u=0, meaning the sum of second spatial partial derivatives is zero.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the PDE type, variables, derivatives, and boundary or initial data before solving.\n\nWhy It Works: PDE methods work because derivative structure controls propagation, diffusion, or equilibrium.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing boundary conditions and initial conditions or using the wrong PDE type.\n\nCorrect idea: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\n\nEquation: Laplace equation is Delta u=0, meaning the sum of second spatial partial derivatives is zero.\n\nWorked Example: u=x^2-y^2 satisfies u_xx+u_yy=2-2=0.\n\nVerification: The second derivatives cancel.\n\n**Key Point:** Laplace equation is Delta u=0, meaning the sum of second spatial partial derivatives is zero.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: u=x^2-y^2 satisfies u_xx+u_yy=2-2=0.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\nVisual Understanding: Picture a surface changing over space and time, with edges or starting shape fixed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Laplace equation describes equilibrium.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Solutions are harmonic functions with strong mean-value properties.",
    "practicePrompt": "Verify u=x^2-y^2 is harmonic.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
