    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Partial Differential Equations",
    "topic": "PDE Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Separation of variables",
    "introduction": "Separation of variables is a partial-differential-equation idea. It studies functions depending on several variables and their partial derivatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Separation of variables tries a solution as a product of single-variable functions.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the PDE type, variables, derivatives, and boundary or initial data before solving.\n\nWhy It Works: PDE methods work because derivative structure controls propagation, diffusion, or equilibrium.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing boundary conditions and initial conditions or using the wrong PDE type.\n\nCorrect idea: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\n\nMethod: Separation of variables tries a solution as a product of single-variable functions.\n\nWorked Example: For u(x,t)=X(x)T(t), derivatives split into X and T parts.\n\nVerification: Substitution can separate equations into ODEs.\n\n**Key Point:** Separation of variables tries a solution as a product of single-variable functions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For u(x,t)=X(x)T(t), derivatives split into X and T parts.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\nVisual Understanding: Picture a surface changing over space and time, with edges or starting shape fixed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Separation turns one PDE into simpler ODEs.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It works when geometry and conditions fit product forms.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why does separation require compatible boundary conditions?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
