  {
    "sourceRow": 1341,
    "id": "ug-year-3__partial-differential-equations__pde-foundations__maximum-principle-introduction",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Partial Differential Equations",
    "topic": "PDE Foundations",
    "subtopic": "Maximum principle introduction",
    "introduction": "Maximum principle introduction is a partial-differential-equation idea. It studies functions depending on several variables and their partial derivatives.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A maximum principle says certain PDE solutions attain maxima on the boundary, under suitable hypotheses.\n\nHow It Works: Identify the PDE type, variables, derivatives, and boundary or initial data before solving.\n\nWhy It Works: PDE methods work because derivative structure controls propagation, diffusion, or equilibrium.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing boundary conditions and initial conditions or using the wrong PDE type.\n\nCorrect idea: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\n\nTheorem: A maximum principle says certain PDE solutions attain maxima on the boundary, under suitable hypotheses.\n\nWorked Example: A harmonic function on a bounded region cannot have a strict interior maximum unless constant.\n\nVerification: Laplace equation has no interior source term.\n\n**Key Point:** A maximum principle says certain PDE solutions attain maxima on the boundary, under suitable hypotheses.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A harmonic function on a bounded region cannot have a strict interior maximum unless constant.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Classify the equation and state all conditions before choosing a method.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in heat flow, waves, fluids, electrostatics, finance, and image processing.\nVisual Understanding: Picture a surface changing over space and time, with edges or starting shape fixed.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Boundary values control interior extremes.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Maximum principles give uniqueness and estimates for elliptic and parabolic PDEs.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why does maximum principle help prove uniqueness?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1342,
    "id": "ug-year-3__operations-research__optimization__linear-programming",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Operations Research",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Linear programming",
    "introduction": "Linear programming is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Linear programming optimizes a linear objective subject to linear constraints.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nDefinition: Linear programming optimizes a linear objective subject to linear constraints.\n\nWorked Example: Maximize P=2x+3y over a polygonal feasible region by checking corners.\n\nVerification: Linear objectives attain optima at extreme points when bounded.\n\n**Key Point:** Linear programming optimizes a linear objective subject to linear constraints.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Maximize P=2x+3y over a polygonal feasible region by checking corners.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Linear programming chooses the best feasible point.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is convex optimization over polyhedra.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give objective and constraints for a simple production problem.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1343,
    "id": "ug-year-3__operations-research__optimization__simplex-method",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Operations Research",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Simplex method",
    "introduction": "Simplex method is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The simplex method moves from one feasible corner to a better adjacent corner until no improvement is possible.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nAlgorithm: The simplex method moves from one feasible corner to a better adjacent corner until no improvement is possible.\n\nWorked Example: In a max problem, a positive entering reduced cost can improve the objective.\n\nVerification: Pivoting changes the active corner.\n\n**Key Point:** The simplex method moves from one feasible corner to a better adjacent corner until no improvement is possible.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: In a max problem, a positive entering reduced cost can improve the objective.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Simplex walks along polygon corners.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is efficient in practice though worst-case exponential.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why does simplex check adjacent corners?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1344,
    "id": "ug-year-3__operations-research__optimization__duality",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Operations Research",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Duality",
    "introduction": "Duality is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Linear programming duality pairs every primal problem with a dual problem whose optimum matches under suitable conditions.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nTheorem: Linear programming duality pairs every primal problem with a dual problem whose optimum matches under suitable conditions.\n\nWorked Example: A max resource problem has a min price-shadow problem.\n\nVerification: Weak duality says dual value bounds primal value.\n\n**Key Point:** Linear programming duality pairs every primal problem with a dual problem whose optimum matches under suitable conditions.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A max resource problem has a min price-shadow problem.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Duality compares production and prices.",
