    "introduction": "Transportation problem is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A transportation problem minimizes shipping cost from sources to destinations with supply and demand constraints.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nDefinition: A transportation problem minimizes shipping cost from sources to destinations with supply and demand constraints.\n\nWorked Example: Shipping 10 units from one factory to one shop costs cost per unit times 10.\n\nVerification: Supply and demand must balance or be adjusted.\n\n**Key Point:** A transportation problem minimizes shipping cost from sources to destinations with supply and demand constraints.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Shipping 10 units from one factory to one shop costs cost per unit times 10.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
