    "id": "ug-year-3__operations-research__optimization__assignment-problem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 3",
    "chapter": "Operations Research",
    "topic": "Optimization",
    "subtopic": "Assignment problem",
    "introduction": "Assignment problem is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: An assignment problem matches agents to tasks at minimum cost or maximum profit, one-to-one.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nDefinition: An assignment problem matches agents to tasks at minimum cost or maximum profit, one-to-one.\n\nWorked Example: Assigning 3 workers to 3 jobs uses one worker per job.\n\nVerification: Each row and column is used once.\n\n**Key Point:** An assignment problem matches agents to tasks at minimum cost or maximum profit, one-to-one.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Assigning 3 workers to 3 jobs uses one worker per job.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Assignment is perfect matching with costs.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It can be solved by the Hungarian algorithm.",
    "practicePrompt": "Why is assignment problem a special transportation problem?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
