    "introduction": "Network flow introduction is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Network flow sends quantity through directed edges with capacity limits.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nDefinition: Network flow sends quantity through directed edges with capacity limits.\n\nWorked Example: A pipe of capacity 5 cannot carry flow 7.\n\nVerification: Capacity constraints bound each edge.\n\n**Key Point:** Network flow sends quantity through directed edges with capacity limits.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: A pipe of capacity 5 cannot carry flow 7.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Network flow moves material through a graph.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Max-flow min-cut theorem links flow value to cuts.",
    "practicePrompt": "Give a small network with a source, sink, and capacities.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
