    "introduction": "Integer programming introduction is an operations-research idea. It helps choose good decisions under constraints, costs, or uncertainty.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Integer programming requires some or all decision variables to be integers.\n\nHow It Works: Define variables, objective, constraints, and method before computing or optimizing.\n\nWhy It Works: Optimization works by comparing feasible choices using a clear mathematical rule.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\n\nCommon mistake: Optimizing a value before checking whether the choice is feasible.\n\nCorrect idea: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\n\nDefinition: Integer programming requires some or all decision variables to be integers.\n\nWorked Example: Choosing 3 machines is allowed, but 3.5 machines is not.\n\nVerification: The variable represents countable objects.\n\n**Key Point:** Integer programming requires some or all decision variables to be integers.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Choosing 3 machines is allowed, but 3.5 machines is not.\nExample 2 - Contrast: Feasibility comes first; optimization compares only allowed choices.\nExample 3 - Application: It is used in transport, scheduling, staffing, logistics, games, and service systems.\nVisual Understanding: Use a feasible region, network, table, decision tree, or payoff matrix.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Integer programming handles indivisible choices.",
    "advancedExplanation": "It is often much harder than linear programming.",
