    "topic": "Curves and Surfaces",
    "subtopic": "Regular curves",
    "introduction": "Regular curves is a specific idea in Curves and Surfaces. Regular curves studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Regular curves studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Regular curves. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of geometry and topology, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Differential Geometry I when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of confusing a local picture with a global property.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For Regular curves, use a circle, sphere, torus, or simple surface. Identify the local object and the global invariant being studied.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Regular curves studies spaces, shapes, or invariants that remain meaningful under allowed transformations. Local and global conditions must be separated.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For Regular curves, use a circle, sphere, torus, or simple surface. Identify the local object and the global invariant being studied.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Regular curves may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Differential Geometry I, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Regular curves.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Regular curves is easiest when you attach it to one small example. Name the setting, check the condition, then use the rule only for that setting.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Regular curves separates local coordinates from global invariants. Advanced results often measure obstruction, curvature, or homotopy information.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Regular curves, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
