  {
    "sourceRow": 1399,
    "id": "ug-year-4-honours__number-theory__elementary-and-algebraic-number-theory__euler-s-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 4 / Honours",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory",
    "subtopic": "Euler's theorem",
    "introduction": "Euler's theorem is a specific idea in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory. Euler's theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Euler's theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Euler's theorem. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of algebra, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Number Theory when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using a theorem for the wrong algebraic object or a map that does not preserve structure.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For n=10, phi(10)=4 and gcd(3,10)=1. So 3^4=81 is congruent to 1 modulo 10.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Euler's theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For n=10, phi(10)=4 and gcd(3,10)=1. So 3^4=81 is congruent to 1 modulo 10.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Euler's theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Euler's theorem.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Euler's theorem is about one main check: Euler's theorem says if gcd(a,n)=1, then a^phi(n) is congruent to 1 modulo n. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "This is Lagrange's theorem in the unit group modulo n. The coprimality condition is essential. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Euler's theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
    "sourceRow": 1400,
    "id": "ug-year-4-honours__number-theory__elementary-and-algebraic-number-theory__fermat-s-little-theorem",
    "classLevel": "UG Year 4 / Honours",
    "chapter": "Number Theory",
    "topic": "Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory",
    "subtopic": "Fermat's Little Theorem",
    "introduction": "Fermat's Little Theorem is a specific idea in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory. Fermat's Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Fermat's Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Fermat's Little Theorem. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of algebra, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Number Theory when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using a theorem for the wrong algebraic object or a map that does not preserve structure.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For p=7 and a=3, 3^6=729, and 729 leaves remainder 1 modulo 7.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** Fermat's Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For p=7 and a=3, 3^6=729, and 729 leaves remainder 1 modulo 7.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fermat's Little Theorem may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Number Theory, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fermat's Little Theorem.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Fermat's Little Theorem is about one main check: Fermat's Little Theorem says if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then a^(p-1) is congruent to 1 modulo p. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "The theorem is Lagrange's theorem applied to the multiplicative group of nonzero residues modulo p. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Elementary and Algebraic Number Theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Fermat's Little Theorem, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
