  {
    "sourceRow": 1454,
    "id": "pg-msc-core__advanced-algebra__field-and-galois-theory__fundamental-theorem-of-galois-theory",
    "classLevel": "PG / MSc Core",
    "chapter": "Advanced Algebra",
    "topic": "Field and Galois Theory",
    "subtopic": "Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory",
    "introduction": "Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory is a specific idea in Field and Galois Theory. The Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory gives an inclusion-reversing correspondence between subgroups of the Galois group and intermediate fields. It matters because students must know exactly when the method or theorem is valid.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: The Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory gives an inclusion-reversing correspondence between subgroups of the Galois group and intermediate fields.\n\nHow It Works: Start by writing the exact setting for Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory. State the needed condition, then carry out the calculation, construction, or proof step that the topic allows.\n\nWhy It Works: The result follows from the structure of algebra, not from the name alone. If the key condition changes, the conclusion may no longer be true.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Advanced Algebra when a problem needs this exact definition, theorem, or modelling assumption.\n\nCommon mistake: Students may make the error of using a theorem for the wrong algebraic object or a map that does not preserve structure.\n\nCorrect idea: State the condition first, solve second, and check the answer against the original setting.\n\nWorked Example: For a Galois extension L/K, the fixed field of a larger subgroup is a smaller intermediate field.\n\nVerification: Check the hypothesis, recompute the small example, and compare with a known special case.\n\n**Key Point:** The Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory gives an inclusion-reversing correspondence between subgroups of the Galois group and intermediate fields.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a Galois extension L/K, the fixed field of a larger subgroup is a smaller intermediate field.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If the required condition is missing, Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory may not apply even when the notation looks similar.\nExample 3 - Application: In Advanced Algebra, this topic helps decide whether a calculation, model, theorem, or algorithm is valid.\nVisual Model: Use a small diagram, table, graph, or symbolic example that shows the exact object involved in Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory is about one main check: The Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory gives an inclusion-reversing correspondence between subgroups of the Galois group and intermediate fields. Use the worked example to see the condition before using the rule.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Normal subgroups correspond to intermediate extensions that are Galois over the base field. This turns field questions into group questions. This is the deeper reason the exact name and hypothesis matter in Field and Galois Theory.",
    "practicePrompt": "For Fundamental Theorem of Galois Theory, write the exact definition or theorem, give one worked example with numbers or symbols, and explain one condition that must be checked.",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
