  {
    "sourceRow": 1666,
    "id": "pg-msc-electives__cryptography__mathematical-cryptography__discrete-logarithm",
    "classLevel": "PG / MSc Electives",
    "chapter": "Cryptography",
    "topic": "Mathematical Cryptography",
    "subtopic": "Discrete logarithm",
    "introduction": "A discrete logarithm asks for the exponent that produces a target value in modular arithmetic. The idea is used in cryptography because exponentiation can be easy while reversing it can be hard. A solution exists only when the target lies in the subgroup generated by the base.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: A discrete logarithm asks for x such that g^x is congruent to h modulo n.\n\nHow It Works: First list powers of g modulo n. Then check whether h appears in that repeating list. If it appears, the matching exponent is a solution.\n\nWhy It Works: The powers of g form the subgroup generated by g. Values outside that subgroup have no discrete logarithm to the base g.\n\nWhere We Use It: Discrete logarithms are central in Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve cryptography, and public-key security.\n\nCommon mistake: Saying the exponent is taken modulo n.\n\nCorrect idea: Exponents repeat modulo the order of g, not necessarily modulo n.\n\nWorked Example: Modulo 7, 3^2 is congruent to 2, so x=2 solves 3^x congruent to 2 modulo 7. The order of 3 modulo 7 is 6, so exponents repeat modulo 6.\n\nVerification: 3^2=9, and 9 leaves remainder 2 when divided by 7.\n\n**Key Point:** A discrete logarithm exists only inside the subgroup generated by the base, and exponents repeat modulo the base's order.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Modulo 7, powers of 3 are 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1. Since 2 appears at exponent 2, x=2 is a solution.\nExample 2 - Contrast: If h is not in the subgroup generated by g, then no discrete logarithm to base g exists.\nExample 3 - Application: Cryptographic groups choose large orders so reversing g^x to find x is computationally hard.\nVisual Model: Make a cycle table of powers of g and mark where the target h appears.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Think of powers of g as a repeating cycle. A discrete logarithm asks where the target value appears in that cycle. The cycle length is the order of g.",
    "advancedExplanation": "Group-theoretically, the problem is solved inside the cyclic subgroup generated by g. For base 3 modulo 7, the subgroup has order 6, so exponents are equivalent modulo 6. This order, not the modulus 7 itself, controls repeated exponents.",
    "practicePrompt": "Modulo 7, list the powers of 3 until they repeat. Which exponent solves 3^x congruent to 4 modulo 7, and what is the order of 3?",
    "updatedAt": 1786245296137
  },
  {
