    "introduction": "Hyperbolic dynamics is a research-level idea in Advanced Dynamical Systems. It gives exact language for advanced mathematical objects or methods. Learning it helps students separate correct reasoning from similar-looking but false claims.",
    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Hyperbolic dynamics studies systems where tangent directions split into uniformly expanding and contracting parts.\n\nHow It Works: First identify the objects involved. Next check the assumptions and the allowed operations. Then apply the definition only within that valid setting.\n\nWhy It Works: The assumptions control the conclusion. If a condition is removed, a counterexample may satisfy the words but fail the result.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Applied Mathematics - Dynamical Systems and Control for proofs, models, algorithms, and research questions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the concept as a slogan or formula without checking its setting.\n\nCorrect idea: Write the object, assumption, conclusion, and one test example before using it.\n\nDefinition: Hyperbolic dynamics studies systems where tangent directions split into uniformly expanding and contracting parts.\n\nWorked Example: For a saddle fixed point, one direction attracts while another repels.\n\nVerification: Check the hypotheses, compare with the example, and test a simple boundary case.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture phase portraits, state spaces, trajectories, feedback loops, and invariant sets. Look for what remains invariant and what changes.\n\nMemory Trick: Use O-A-C: Object, Assumption, Conclusion.\n\n**Key Point:** Hyperbolic dynamics studies systems where tangent directions split into uniformly expanding and contracting parts.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: For a saddle fixed point, one direction attracts while another repels.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Remove one required assumption and the conclusion may fail, even if the notation still looks familiar.\nExample 3 - Application: This concept helps researchers decide whether a proof, model, or algorithm is valid in Advanced Dynamical Systems.\nVisual Model: Use phase portraits, state spaces, trajectories, feedback loops, and invariant sets to see the structure before writing formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Hyperbolic dynamics should be learned by asking three questions. What object is studied? What assumption is needed? What conclusion is allowed?",
    "advancedExplanation": "Uniform hyperbolicity gives structural stability and symbolic coding of orbits. At PhD level, this topic is valuable because its hypotheses, counterexamples, and canonical examples guide current research.",
