    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Nonlinear control designs inputs for systems whose equations are not linear in state or control.\n\nHow It Works: First identify the objects involved. Next check the assumptions and the allowed operations. Then apply the definition only within that valid setting.\n\nWhy It Works: The assumptions control the conclusion. If a condition is removed, a counterexample may satisfy the words but fail the result.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Applied Mathematics - Dynamical Systems and Control for proofs, models, algorithms, and research questions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the concept as a slogan or formula without checking its setting.\n\nCorrect idea: Write the object, assumption, conclusion, and one test example before using it.\n\nDefinition: Nonlinear control designs inputs for systems whose equations are not linear in state or control.\n\nWorked Example: A pendulum controller must handle sine terms, not only straight-line motion.\n\nVerification: Check the hypotheses, compare with the example, and test a simple boundary case.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture phase portraits, state spaces, trajectories, feedback loops, and invariant sets. Look for what remains invariant and what changes.\n\nMemory Trick: Use O-A-C: Object, Assumption, Conclusion.\n\n**Key Point:** Nonlinear control designs inputs for systems whose equations are not linear in state or control.",
