    "detailedExplanation": "Basic Idea: Completeness says semantic truth and syntactic provability match for first-order logic.\n\nHow It Works: First identify the objects involved. Next check the assumptions and the allowed operations. Then apply the theorem only within that valid setting.\n\nWhy It Works: The assumptions control the conclusion. If a condition is removed, a counterexample may satisfy the words but fail the result.\n\nWhere We Use It: It is used in Mathematical Logic and Foundations for proofs, models, algorithms, and research questions.\n\nCommon mistake: Treating the concept as a slogan or formula without checking its setting.\n\nCorrect idea: Write the object, assumption, conclusion, and one test example before using it.\n\nTheorem: Completeness says semantic truth and syntactic provability match for first-order logic.\n\nWorked Example: If a sentence is true in all models, it is provable.\n\nVerification: Check the hypotheses, compare with the example, and test a simple boundary case.\n\nVisual Understanding: Picture formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines. Look for what remains invariant and what changes.\n\nMemory Trick: Use O-A-C: Object, Assumption, Conclusion.\n\n**Key Point:** Completeness says semantic truth and syntactic provability match for first-order logic.",
    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: If a sentence is true in all models, it is provable.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Remove one required assumption and the conclusion may fail, even if the notation still looks familiar.\nExample 3 - Application: This concept helps researchers decide whether a proof, model, or algorithm is valid in Proof Theory.\nVisual Model: Use formal languages, proof trees, models, ordinals, and machines to see the structure before writing formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Completeness should be learned by asking three questions. What object is studied? What assumption is needed? What conclusion is allowed?",
