    "realtimeExamples": "Example 1 - Easy: Symmetric functions encode partitions and representations.\nExample 2 - Counterexample: Remove one required assumption and the conclusion may fail, even if the notation still looks familiar.\nExample 3 - Application: This concept helps researchers decide whether a proof, model, or algorithm is valid in Advanced Combinatorics.\nVisual Model: Use graphs, sets, blocks, codes, and counting diagrams to see the structure before writing formal notation.",
    "simplifiedExplanation": "Algebraic combinatorics should be learned by asking three questions. What object is studied? What assumption is needed? What conclusion is allowed?",
